@file FormalConjectures/Arxiv/0805.3777/TensorRank.lean
@decl Arxiv.«0805.3777».isGenericRank_of_le_critical
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: isGenericRank_of_le_critical
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Friedland's conjecture.** In the critical range $m_3 \leq (m_1 - 1)(m_2 - 1)$, and away from the formats $(3, 2p+1, 2p+1)$, the generic rank of a tensor of format $(m_1, m_2, m_3)$ is the value $\lceil m_1m_2m_3 / (m_1 + m_2 + m_3 - 2) \rceil$ predicted by a dimension count [Fri12, Conjecture 5.1]. It is known for $m_1 \leq 4$ and for $(n, n, n)$, and has been verified numerically throughout the critical range for $m_3 \leq 20$ [BFZ24, Section 4.5].
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Arxiv/0805.3777/TensorRank.lean
@decl Arxiv.«0805.3777».isGenericRank_three_even
-- L4DEL: title: The generic rank of a $3 \times 2p \times 2p$ tensor is $\lceil 12p^2 / (4p + 1) \rceil$, the value $r_0$ predicted by a dimension count [Fri12, (5.3)]
-- L4DEL: intuition: The generic rank of a $3 \times 2p \times 2p$ tensor is $\lceil 12p^2 / (4p + 1) \rceil$, the value $r_0$ predicted by a dimension count [Fri12, (5.3)]. The hypothesis $2 \leq p$ is the ordering $3 \leq 2p$ of the format.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Arxiv/0805.3777/TensorRank.lean
@decl Arxiv.«0805.3777».isGenericRank_three_odd
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: isGenericRank_three_odd
-- L4DEL: intuition: The generic rank of a $3 \times (2p+1) \times (2p+1)$ tensor is $\lceil 3(2p+1)^2 / (4p + 3) \rceil + 1$, one more than the value $r_0$ predicted by a dimension count [Fri12, (5.4)]. These are the only formats in the critical range known to exceed $r_0$. The case $p = 1$ says that the generic rank of a $3 \times 3 \times 3$ tensor is `5`, not `4`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

