@file FormalConjectures/Arxiv/2507.17780/4.lean
@decl Arxiv.«2507.17780».friendshipF4
-- L4DEL: title: The friendship graph $F_4$: a hub vertex $0$ joined to four triangles, whose rims are the pairs $\{1,2\}, \{3,4\}, \{5,6\}, \{7,8\}$
-- L4DEL: intuition: The friendship graph $F_4$: a hub vertex $0$ joined to four triangles, whose rims are the pairs $\{1,2\}, \{3,4\}, \{5,6\}, \{7,8\}$. It has $12$ edges on $9$ vertices ($8$ spokes and $4$ rim edges).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Arxiv/2507.17780/4.lean
@decl Arxiv.«2507.17780».tx_graffiti_conjecture_4
-- L4DEL: title: TxGraffiti Conjecture 4: for every nontrivial connected graph $G$, the saturation number is at most the harmonic index, $\mu^*(G) \le H(G)$
-- L4DEL: intuition: TxGraffiti Conjecture 4: for every nontrivial connected graph $G$, the saturation number is at most the harmonic index, $\mu^*(G) \le H(G)$. This conjecture is **FALSE**. **Counterexample.** The friendship graph $F_4$ (`friendshipF4`) is connected and nontrivial, with $\mu^*(F_4) = 4$ and $H(F_4) = 18/5$. Indeed the four rim edges $\{1,2\}, \{3,4\}, \{5,6\}, \{7,8\}$ form a maximal matching of size $4$, and no maximal matching is smaller, so $\mu^*(F_4) = 4$; the eight spokes contribute $2/(8+2) = 1/5$ each and the four rim edges contribute $2/(2+2) = 1/2$ each, giving $H(F_4) = 8 \cdot 1/5 + 4 \cdot 1/2 = 18/5$. Since $4 > 18/5$, the bound fails. More generally the friendship graphs $F_k$ satisfy $\mu^*(F_k) = k$ and $H(F_k) = 2k/(k+1) + k/2$, so the conjecture fails for every $k \ge 4$. An exhaustive search confirms that $F_4$ is the smallest counterexample (order $9$). The unbounded separation was first shown by Bıyıkoğlu, whose family of edges joined to an independent set makes $\mu^*/H$ arbitrarily large; the windmill generalisation and its exact limit appear in arXiv:2606.15761.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Arxiv/2602.05192/FirstProof4.lean
@decl Arxiv.«2602.05192».finiteAdditiveConvolution
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: finiteAdditiveConvolution
-- L4DEL: intuition: Define $p \boxplus_n q(x)$ to be the polynomial $$ (p \boxplus_n q)(x) = \sum_{k=0}^n c_k x^{n-k} $$ where the coefficients $c_k$ are given by the formula: $$ c_k = \sum_{i+j=k} \frac{(n-i)! (n-j)!}{n! (n-k)!} a_i b_j $$ for $k = 0, 1, \dots, n$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

