-- L4DEL: intuition: The group homomorphism $(\sigma_1, \sigma_2) \mapsto \mathrm{sgn}(\sigma_1) \cdot \mathrm{sgn}(\sigma_2)^{-1}$ from $\mathrm{S}_m \times \mathrm{S}_n$ to $\{+1, -1\}$. Its kernel is exactly $\Gamma_{m \oplus n}$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Arxiv/2606.03696/BondyLongestCycles.lean
@decl Arxiv.«2606.03696».bondy_conjecture
-- L4DEL: title: Conjecture 1 (Bondy, 1980).** Let $k \geq 1$ and let $G$ be a $k$-connected graph on $n$ vertices
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Conjecture 1 (Bondy, 1980).** Let $k \geq 1$ and let $G$ be a $k$-connected graph on $n$ vertices. If $\delta(G) \geq \frac{n + k(k-1)}{k+1}$, then for every longest cycle $C$ of $G$, every path in $G - V(C)$ has at most $k-1$ vertices. The bound on the number of vertices of a path is written as `+ 1 ≤ k` rather than `≤ k - 1`, because subtraction on `ℕ` is truncated. The two forms agree for `k ≥ 1`. A longest cycle is a cycle whose length is the circumference of `G`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Arxiv/2606.03696/BondyLongestCycles.lean
@decl Arxiv.«2606.03696».bondy_conjecture.variants.large
-- L4DEL: title: Theorem 1.1 (Ma-Ning-Zhao, 2026).** The conjecture holds for every graph with enough vertices
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Theorem 1.1 (Ma-Ning-Zhao, 2026).** The conjecture holds for every graph with enough vertices. The full conjecture, for graphs of every size, stays open.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Arxiv/2606.03696/BondyLongestCycles.lean
@decl Arxiv.«2606.03696».bondy_conjecture.variants.one
-- L4DEL: title: The case $k = 1$ is Dirac's theorem
-- L4DEL: intuition: The case $k = 1$ is Dirac's theorem. The condition reads $\delta(G) \geq n/2$, and no path in $G - V(C)$ can hold a vertex, so a longest cycle covers every vertex.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

