@file FormalConjectures/Books/BugeaudDistributionModuloOne/IntDistanceDistribution.lean
@decl Bugeaud01.problem_10_3
-- L4DEL: title: Problem 10.3
-- L4DEL: intuition: Problem 10.3. To prove that there exists a positive real number~$c$ such that $\lVert e^n \rVert > e^{−cn}$, for every~$n \ge 1$. Posed by Mahler [Mah53].
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Books/BugeaudDistributionModuloOne/IntDistanceDistribution.lean
@decl Bugeaud01.problem_10_3_of_waldschmidt
-- L4DEL: title: Waldschmidt's conjecture is stronger than Mahler's: since $\log n \le n$ for $n \ge 1$, the polynomial lower bound $n^{-c}$ dominates the exponential lower bound $e^{-cn}$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Waldschmidt's conjecture is stronger than Mahler's: since $\log n \le n$ for $n \ge 1$, the polynomial lower bound $n^{-c}$ dominates the exponential lower bound $e^{-cn}$. For the $n = 1$ case (not covered by Waldschmidt's $n \ge 2$), we choose a larger constant using the numerical bound $\lVert e \rVert = 3 - e > 0$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Books/BugeaudDistributionModuloOne/IntDistanceDistribution.lean
@decl Bugeaud01.waldschmidt
-- L4DEL: title: Waldschmidt [Wal03] conjectured that a stronger result holds, namely that there exists a positive real number~$c$ such that $\lVert e^n \rVert > n^{-c}$ for every~$n \ge 2$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Waldschmidt [Wal03] conjectured that a stronger result holds, namely that there exists a positive real number~$c$ such that $\lVert e^n \rVert > n^{-c}$ for every~$n \ge 2$. This is supported by metrical results [Kok45]. Note: the bound $n^{-c}$ equals $1$ when $n = 1$ for all $c$, while the distance to the nearest integer is always at most $1/2$, so the conjecture must start at $n \ge 2$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Books/BugeaudDistributionModuloOne/Problem10_32.lean
@decl Bugeaud32.adamczewski_faverjon
-- L4DEL: title: Adamczewski–Faverjon** [AF20, Conjecture A.3], proved there from Part (i) of Theorem 1.1
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Adamczewski–Faverjon** [AF20, Conjecture A.3], proved there from Part (i) of Theorem 1.1. Let $b_1, \dots, b_r$ be multiplicatively independent positive integers, and for every $i$ let $\xi_i$ be a real number that is automatic in base $b_i$. Then $\xi_1, \dots, \xi_r$ are algebraically independent over $\mathbb{Q}$, unless one of them is rational. Two points of fidelity. The hypothesis is the *joint* multiplicative independence of $b_1, \dots, b_r$ that [AF20] defines on p. 3 (no nonzero integer tuple $(n_i)$ has $\prod_i b_i^{n_i} = 1$), not the weaker pairwise condition; it is what the proof feeds to Part (i) of Theorem 1.1, via the multiplicative independence of $1/b_1, \dots, 1/b_r$. Automaticity is the broad notion that the proof of Conjecture A.3 uses: the base-$b_i$ digits of $\xi_i$ are $k$-automatic for some $k \ge 2$, which need not be $b_i$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Books/BugeaudDistributionModuloOne/Problem10_32.lean
@decl Bugeaud32.problem_10_32
-- L4DEL: title: Problem 10.32
-- L4DEL: intuition: Problem 10.32. Is there an irrational real number whose expansions in two multiplicatively independent bases can both be generated by a finite automaton? The answer is no, by [AF20].
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Books/BugeaudDistributionModuloOne/Problem10_32.lean
@decl Bugeaud32.problem_10_32.variants.strong
-- L4DEL: title: Problem 10.32, strong form
-- L4DEL: intuition: Problem 10.32, strong form. No irrational real number is automatic in two multiplicatively independent bases, in the broad sense that its digit sequence in each of the two bases is `k`-automatic for some `k`. This is [AF20, Conjecture A.2], with the broad notion of automaticity that its proof provides.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Books/BugeaudDistributionModuloOne/Problem10_4.lean
@decl Bugeaud04.Spectrum
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: Spectrum
-- L4DEL: intuition: The spectrum of a sequence $(x_n)_{n \ge 1}$ of real numbers is the set of irrational real numbers $\theta \in (0, 1)$ such that the sequence $(x_n - n\theta)_{n \ge 1}$ is not uniformly distributed modulo one.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Books/BugeaudDistributionModuloOne/Problem10_5.lean
@decl Bugeaud05.problem_10_5_of_moreover
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: problem_10_5_of_moreover
-- L4DEL: intuition: The "moreover" form of Problem 10.5 implies the first part: applying the cluster-point density to the subinterval $[1 - \varepsilon, 1]$ yields the required lower bound on the limsup.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Books/BugeaudDistributionModuloOne/Problem10_53.lean
@decl Bugeaud53.problem_10_53
-- L4DEL: title: Problem 10.53
-- L4DEL: intuition: Problem 10.53. There is an irrational real number $\xi$ such that $E(\xi) < \log 2$ and $E(\xi, b) < \log b$ for some integer $b \ge 2$. Answered by Temur [Tem26].
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Books/BugeaudDistributionModuloOne/Problem10_53.lean
@decl Bugeaud53.problem_10_53.variants.all_bases
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: all_bases
-- L4DEL: intuition: Problem 10.53, in the stronger form that asks for every base at once: there is an irrational real number $\xi$ such that $E(\xi) < \log 2$ and $E(\xi, b) < \log b$ for every integer $b \ge 2$. Answered by Temur [Tem26].
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Books/BugeaudDistributionModuloOne/Problem10_53.lean
@decl Bugeaud53.problem_10_53.variants.temur
-- L4DEL: title: Temur [Tem26], Theorem 1
-- L4DEL: intuition: Temur [Tem26], Theorem 1. There is an irrational $\xi \in (0, 1)$ all of whose partial quotients are $1$ or $2$, such that $$\lVert b^k \xi \rVert > 154^{-2^b} \quad (b \ge 2,\ k \ge 0),$$ where $\lVert \cdot \rVert$ denotes the distance to the nearest integer. The bound on the partial quotients gives $E(\xi) \le (\log 2) / 4$, and the displayed inequality forces the base $b$ expansion of $\xi$ to omit a block of zeros, which gives $E(\xi, b) < \log b$ for every $b \ge 2$. Temur's $\xi$ is moreover computable.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Books/BugeaudDistributionModuloOne/Problem10_53.lean
@decl Bugeaud53.problem_10_53.variants.uncountable
-- L4DEL: title: There are uncountably many numbers as in Problem 10.53
-- L4DEL: intuition: There are uncountably many numbers as in Problem 10.53. This follows from a result of Broderick, Bugeaud, Fishman, Kleinbock and Weiss [BBFKW10] applied to the Gauss-Cantor set $\{[0; 1, a_1, 1, a_2, \ldots] : a_j \in \{1, 2\}\}$, as observed in [Tem26].
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Books/BugeaudDistributionModuloOne/Problem10_6.lean
@decl Bugeaud06.boshernitzan
-- L4DEL: title: Boshernitzan's theorem** [Bos94]
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Boshernitzan's theorem** [Bos94]. Given a real sublacunary sequence $r$, the set of real numbers $\xi$ for which $(\{\xi r_n\})_{n \ge 1}$ is *not* dense modulo one has Hausdorff dimension zero.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Books/BugeaudDistributionModuloOne/Problem10_6.lean
@decl Bugeaud06.countingFn
