@file FormalConjectures/Books/BugeaudDistributionModuloOne/Problem10_6.lean
@decl Bugeaud06.example_hasIntermediateGrowth
-- L4DEL: title: `mSeq` has intermediate (subexponential but super-polynomial) growth: for every `0 < α < 1` its terms eventually dominate $\exp(n^\alpha)$
-- L4DEL: intuition: `mSeq` has intermediate (subexponential but super-polynomial) growth: for every `0 < α < 1` its terms eventually dominate $\exp(n^\alpha)$. This is the ratio floor of `example_isGenuineSublacunary` fed into `hasIntermediateGrowth_of_isGenuinelySublacunary`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Books/BugeaudDistributionModuloOne/Problem10_6.lean
@decl Bugeaud06.example_isGenuineSublacunary
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: example_isGenuineSublacunary
-- L4DEL: intuition: The sequence `mSeq`, given by $m_{n+1} = \lceil m_n (1 + 1/\log n) \rceil$, is genuinely sublacunary: taking $c = 1$, we have $m_{n+1}/m_n \ge 1 + 1/\log n$ because $\lceil m_n (1 + 1/\log n) \rceil \ge m_n (1 + 1/\log n)$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Books/BugeaudDistributionModuloOne/Problem10_6.lean
@decl Bugeaud06.furstenberg_two_three
-- L4DEL: title: Furstenberg's theorem** [Fur67] (the $\times 2, \times 3$ case)
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Furstenberg's theorem** [Fur67] (the $\times 2, \times 3$ case). For every irrational number $\xi$, the two-parameter family $(\{\xi \, 2^m 3^n\})_{m, n \ge 1}$ is dense modulo one.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Books/BugeaudDistributionModuloOne/Problem10_6.lean
@decl Bugeaud06.HasIntermediateGrowth
-- L4DEL: title: The sequence $m$ eventually grows at least as fast as $\exp(n^{\alpha})$, i.e., super-exponential growth when $\alpha > 1$, and stretched-exponential when $0 < \alpha < 1$
-- L4DEL: intuition: The sequence $m$ eventually grows at least as fast as $\exp(n^{\alpha})$, i.e., super-exponential growth when $\alpha > 1$, and stretched-exponential when $0 < \alpha < 1$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Books/BugeaudDistributionModuloOne/Problem10_6.lean
