@file FormalConjectures/Books/BugeaudDistributionModuloOne/Problem10_61.lean
@decl Bugeaud61.problem_10_61.variants.almost_every
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: almost_every
-- L4DEL: intuition: The hypothesis $\xi \in C(\alpha)$ cannot be dropped: by Koksma's metric theorem [Kok35], for a fixed $\alpha > 1$ the sequence $(\xi \alpha^n)_{n \ge 1}$ is uniformly distributed modulo one for Lebesgue-almost every $\xi$. Problem 10.61 asks a null set of starting points to defeat an almost-everywhere law.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Books/BugeaudDistributionModuloOne/Problem10_61.lean
@decl Bugeaud61.routeAExponent
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: routeAExponent
-- L4DEL: intuition: The Route A exponent $A(\alpha) = \log 2 / \log \alpha + \log 2 / \log(1 / \rho)$ of [Ste26], where $\rho$ is the largest modulus of a conjugate of $\alpha$ other than $\alpha$ itself. It is the sum of the box dimensions of $C(\alpha)$ and of the window in which the conjugate contributions live.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

