@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1000.lean
@decl Erdos1000.erdos_1000
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: erdos_1000
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $A=\{n_1<n_2<\cdots\}$ be an infinite sequence of positive integers, and let $\phi_A(k)$ count the number of $1\leq m\leq n_k$ such that the fraction $\frac{m}{n_k}$ cannot be written as $\frac{b}{n_j}$ for any integer $b$ and any $j<k$; equivalently, $$ \frac{n_k}{(m,n_k)}\nmid n_j $$ for all $1\leq j<k$. Is there a sequence $A$ such that $$ \lim_{N\to \infty}\frac{1}{N}\sum_{k\leq N}\frac{\phi_A(k)}{n_k}=0? $$ This was solved by Haight [Ha] who proved that such a sequence does exist (contrary to Erdős' expectations).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1000.lean
@decl Erdos1000.erdos_1000.variants.liminf_eq_zero
-- L4DEL: title: The study of $\phi_A$ was introduced by Cassels [Ca50b], who proved that there exist sequences such that $$ \liminf_{N\to \infty}\frac{1}{N}\sum_{k\leq N}\frac{\phi_A(k)}{n_k}=0
-- L4DEL: intuition: The study of $\phi_A$ was introduced by Cassels [Ca50b], who proved that there exist sequences such that $$ \liminf_{N\to \infty}\frac{1}{N}\sum_{k\leq N}\frac{\phi_A(k)}{n_k}=0. $$
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1000.lean
