@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1002.lean
@decl Erdos1002.erdos_1002
-- L4DEL: title: For any $0<\alpha<1$, let $f(\alpha,n)=\frac{1}{\log n}\sum_{1\leq k\leq n}(\tfrac{1}{2}- \{ \alpha k\})$
-- L4DEL: intuition: For any $0<\alpha<1$, let $f(\alpha,n)=\frac{1}{\log n}\sum_{1\leq k\leq n}(\tfrac{1}{2}- \{ \alpha k\})$. Does $f(\alpha,n)$ have an asymptotic distribution function? In other words, is there a non-decreasing function $g$ such that $g(-\infty)=0$, $g(\infty)=1$, and $\lim_{n\to \infty}\lvert \{ \alpha\in (0,1): f(\alpha,n)\leq c\}\rvert=g(c)$?
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1002.lean
@decl Erdos1002.erdos_1002.variants.kesten
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: kesten
-- L4DEL: intuition: Kesten [Ke60] proved that if $f(\alpha,\beta,n)=\frac{1}{\log n}\sum_{1\leq k\leq n}(\tfrac{1}{2}- \{\beta+\alpha k\})$ then $f(\alpha,\beta,n)$ has asymptotic distribution function $g(c)=\frac{1}{\pi}\int_{-\infty}^{\rho c}\frac{1}{1+t^2}\mathrm{d}t$, where $\rho>0$ is an explicit constant.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

