@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1005.lean
@decl Erdos1005.erdos_1005
-- L4DEL: title: Let $\frac{a_1}{b_1}, \frac{a_2}{b_2}, \ldots$ be the Farey fractions of order $n \geq 4$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $\frac{a_1}{b_1}, \frac{a_2}{b_2}, \ldots$ be the Farey fractions of order $n \geq 4$. Let $f(n)$ be the largest integer such that if $1 \leq k < l \leq k + f(n)$ then $\frac{a_k}{b_k}$ and $\frac{a_l}{b_l}$ are similarly ordered, in other words $(a_k - a_l)(b_k - b_l) \geq 0$. Estimate $f(n)$: in particular, is there a constant $c > 0$ such that $f(n) = (c + o(1)) n$ for all large $n$? Mayer [Ma42] proved that $f(n) \to \infty$ and Erdős [Er43] that $f(n) \gg n$. Van Doorn [vD25b] proved $(1/12 - o(1)) n \le f(n) \le n / 4 + O(1)$ and conjectured that $f(n) = (1/4 + o(1)) n$, which was proved by Cipollini and GPT-5.5; see `erdos_1005.variants.constant`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1005.lean
@decl Erdos1005.f
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: f
-- L4DEL: intuition: `f n` is the largest integer such that any two Farey fractions of order `n` whose indices differ by at most `f n` are similarly ordered: it is the minimum, over all pairs `x < y` of Farey fractions of order `n` with `(x.num - y.num) * (x.den - y.den) < 0`, of the number of Farey fractions strictly between `x` and `y`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1005.lean
@decl Erdos1005.IsFarey
-- L4DEL: title: A rational `q` is a Farey fraction of order `n` if it lies in `[0, 1]` and has denominator at most `n`
-- L4DEL: intuition: A rational `q` is a Farey fraction of order `n` if it lies in `[0, 1]` and has denominator at most `n`. (Every `q : ℚ` is stored in lowest terms, so `q.den` and `q.num` are the reduced denominator and numerator.)
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1007.lean
@decl Erdos1007.erdos_1007
-- L4DEL: title: The dimension of a graph $G$ is the minimal $n$ such that $G$ can be embedded in $\mathbb{R}^n$ such that every edge of $G$ is a unit line segment
-- L4DEL: intuition: The dimension of a graph $G$ is the minimal $n$ such that $G$ can be embedded in $\mathbb{R}^n$ such that every edge of $G$ is a unit line segment. What is the smallest number of edges in a graph with dimension $4$? Answer: The smallest number of edges is $9$, achieved solely by $K_{3,3}$, proved by House [Ho13]. An alternative proof was given by Chaffee and Noble [ChNo16], who also prove that the smallest number of edges in a graph of dimension $5$ is $15$ (achieved by $K_6$ and $K_{1,3,3}$).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

