@decl Erdos102.erdos_102.variants.hunter
-- L4DEL: title: Erdős [Er95] suggested that perhaps $h_c(n) \gg_c n^{1/2}$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Erdős [Er95] suggested that perhaps $h_c(n) \gg_c n^{1/2}$. Zach Hunter pointed out that this is false: the points of $\{1, \dots, m\}^d$ with $n \approx m^d$, randomly projected to $\mathbb{R}^2$, meet every line in $\ll_d n^{1/d}$ points and determine $\gg_d n^2$ lines with more than three points. This gives $h_c(n) \ll n^{1 / \log(1/c)}$; we state the underlying form: for every $d \geq 1$ there is $c > 0$ with $h_c(n) \ll_d n^{1/d}$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/102.lean
@decl Erdos102.erdos_102.variants.upper_sqrt
-- L4DEL: title: It is easy to see that $h_c(n) \ll_c n^{1/2}$: for all sufficiently small $c > 0$ there are admissible configurations (e.g
-- L4DEL: intuition: It is easy to see that $h_c(n) \ll_c n^{1/2}$: for all sufficiently small $c > 0$ there are admissible configurations (e.g. grids) in which no line contains more than $C_c n^{1/2}$ points.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

