@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1030.lean
@decl Erdos1030.erdos_1030
-- L4DEL: title: Let $R(k,l)$ be the usual Ramsey number: the smallest $n$ such that if the edges of $K_n$ are coloured red and blue then there exists either a red $K_k$ or a blue $K_l$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $R(k,l)$ be the usual Ramsey number: the smallest $n$ such that if the edges of $K_n$ are coloured red and blue then there exists either a red $K_k$ or a blue $K_l$. Prove the existence of some $c>0$ such that $$\lim_{k\to \infty}\frac{R(k+1,k)}{R(k,k)}> 1+c.$$ A problem of Erdős and Sós.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1034.lean
@decl Erdos1034.erdos_1034
-- L4DEL: title: Let $G$ be a graph on $n$ vertices with $>n^2/4$ many edges
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $G$ be a graph on $n$ vertices with $>n^2/4$ many edges. Must there be a triangle $T$ in $G$ and vertices $y_1,\ldots,y_t$, where $t>(\frac{1}{2}-o(1))n$, such that every $y_i$ is joined to at least two vertices of $T$? A conjecture of Erdős and Faudree; a stronger version of [905]. This has been solved in the negative by Ma and Tang [MaTa25], who construct a graph with $n$ vertices and $>n^2/4$ edges in which every triangle has at most $(2-(5/2)^{1/2}+o(1))n$ vertices adjacent to at least two of its vertices (note that $2-(5/2)^{1/2}\approx 0.4189$).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1034.lean
@decl Erdos1034.erdos_1034.variants.k4_free
-- L4DEL: title: Erdős suggested that the answer is different if $G$ has no $K_4$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Erdős suggested that the answer is different if $G$ has no $K_4$. In the comments Ma and Tang sketch a proof that the conjecture remains false even if we assume that $G$ contains no $K_4$, constructing a graph with $n$ vertices, $>n^2/4$ edges, and no $K_4$, in which every triangle has at most $(2\sqrt{3}-3+o(1))n$ vertices adjacent to at least two of its vertices (note that $2\sqrt{3}-3\approx 0.464$).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1034.lean
@decl Erdos1034.erdos_1034.variants.lower_bound
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: lower_bound
-- L4DEL: intuition: Erdős and Faudree asked about the threshold $h(n)$ such that every graph with $n$ vertices and $>n^2/4$ edges contained a triangle and $h(n)$ other vertices which are connected to at least two vertices of the triangle. The fact that every graph with $>n^2/4$ edges contains a book of size $n/6$ shows that $$(1/6-o(1))n \leq h(n).$$
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1034.lean
@decl Erdos1034.erdos_1034.variants.upper_bound
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: upper_bound
-- L4DEL: intuition: The construction of Ma and Tang [MaTa25] of a graph with $n$ vertices and $>n^2/4$ edges in which every triangle has at most $(2-(5/2)^{1/2}+o(1))n$ vertices adjacent to at least two of its vertices shows that, for the threshold $h(n)$ of `erdos_1034.variants.lower_bound`, $$h(n) \leq (2-(5/2)^{1/2}+o(1))n.$$
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

