@decl Erdos1036.NoLargeBiclique
-- L4DEL: title: Neither `G` nor its complement contains a balanced complete bipartite graph $K_{k,k}$ on more than `m` vertices as a (not necessarily induced) subgraph
-- L4DEL: intuition: Neither `G` nor its complement contains a balanced complete bipartite graph $K_{k,k}$ on more than `m` vertices as a (not necessarily induced) subgraph. That is, there are no two disjoint sets of `k` vertices with $2k > m$ such that every pair of vertices from different sets is adjacent in `G`, or every such pair is non-adjacent in `G`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1037.lean
@decl Erdos1037.erdos_1037
-- L4DEL: title: Let $G$ be a graph on $n$ vertices in which every degree occurs at most twice, and the number of distinct degrees is $>(\frac{1}{2}+\epsilon)n$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $G$ be a graph on $n$ vertices in which every degree occurs at most twice, and the number of distinct degrees is $>(\frac{1}{2}+\epsilon)n$. Must $G$ contain a trivial (empty or complete) subgraph of size 'much larger' than $\log n$? A question of Chen and Erdős. The answer is no - Cambie, Chan, and Hunter have in the comment section given a simple construction of a graph on $n$ vertices with at least $\frac{3}{4}n$ distinct degrees, every degree appears at most twice, and the largest trivial subgraph has size $O(\log n)$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

