@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1049.lean
@decl Erdos1049.erdos_1049
-- L4DEL: title: Let $t>1$ be a rational number
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $t>1$ be a rational number. Is $\sum_{n=1}^\infty\frac{1}{t^n-1}=\sum_{n=1}^\infty \frac{\tau(n)}{t^n}$ irrational, where $\tau(n)$ counts the divisors of $n$? A conjecture of Chowla.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1049.lean
@decl Erdos1049.lambert_convergent
-- L4DEL: title: Convergent case (`|t| > 1`)
-- L4DEL: intuition: Convergent case (`|t| > 1`). Substitute `r := t⁻¹` so `‖r‖ < 1`, then apply Mathlib's series identity `tsum_pow_div_one_sub_eq_tsum_sigma` at `k = 0`: $$\sum_{n \ge 1} \frac{r^n}{1 - r^n} = \sum_{n \ge 1} \sigma_0(n) \cdot r^n.$$ After clearing denominators, both sides match the Lambert identity: LHS becomes `1/(t^n - 1)` and RHS becomes `τ(n) / t^n`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1049.lean
@decl Erdos1049.lambert_divergent
-- L4DEL: title: Divergent case (`|t| ≤ 1`)
-- L4DEL: intuition: Divergent case (`|t| ≤ 1`). Both `tsum`s equal `0` in this regime, but for different reasons in each sub-case. We split on `t ∈ {1, 0, -1}` and the generic `|t| < 1, t ≠ 0` remainder, and use the same `key` non-summability lemma below to handle the cases where the series diverges. - `t = 1`: every LHS term is `1 / (1 - 1) = 0` (Lean convention), so the LHS sum is trivially `0`. RHS is `Σ τ(n)`, non-summable. - `t = 0`: every RHS term is `τ(n) / 0 = 0` (Lean convention), so the RHS sum is trivially `0`. LHS is `Σ (-1)`, non-summable. - `t = -1`: alternating; LHS vanishes at even `n` but odd `n` give terms of magnitude `1/2`, an infinite set. RHS terms have magnitude `τ(n) ≥ 1`. - `|t| < 1, t ≠ 0`: standard; bounded denominator gives lower-bounded reciprocal on LHS, and `|t^n| ≤ 1` plus `τ(n) ≥ 1` gives the RHS bound. In every case, Lean's `tsum_eq_zero_of_not_summable` collapses the non- summable side to `0`, matching the `0` on the other side.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1049.lean
@decl Erdos1049.lambert_series_eq_num_divisor_sum
-- L4DEL: title: The classical Lambert series identity: $\sum_{n=1}^\infty \frac{1}{t^n - 1} = \sum_{n=1}^\infty \frac{\tau(n)}{t^n}$, where $\tau(n)$ counts the divisors of $n$
-- L4DEL: intuition: The classical Lambert series identity: $\sum_{n=1}^\infty \frac{1}{t^n - 1} = \sum_{n=1}^\infty \frac{\tau(n)}{t^n}$, where $\tau(n)$ counts the divisors of $n$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/105.lean
@decl Erdos105.erdos_105
-- L4DEL: title: Let $A,B\subset \mathbb{R}^2$ be disjoint sets of size $n$ and $n-3$ respectively, with not all of $A$ contained on a single line
