@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1050.lean
@decl Erdos1050.erdos_1050.variants.transcendental
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: transcendental
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Erdős Problem 1050, variant [Er88c] (open).** Erdős conjectured that $\sum_{n=1}^\infty \frac{1}{2^n + t}$ is *transcendental* for every integer $t \ne 0$ — strictly stronger than the irrationality established by Borwein [Bo91]. This remains open. Two exclusions make the series well-posed and the claim non-vacuous, exactly as in Borwein's theorem: $t \ne -2^n$ for all $n \ge 1$ (so no denominator vanishes), and $t \ne 0$ (at $t = 0$ the series is the rational $\sum_{n \ge 1} 2^{-n} = 1$, hence not transcendental).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1051.lean
@decl Erdos1051.erdos_1051
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: erdos_1051
-- L4DEL: intuition: Is it true that if $a_0 < a_1 < a_2 < \cdots$ is a strictly increasing sequence of integers with $\liminf a_n^{1/2^n} > 1$, then the series $\sum_{n=0}^\infty \frac{1}{a_n \cdot a_{n+1}}$ is irrational? This was solved in the affirmative by Aletheia [Fe26]. This was extended by Barreto, Kang, Kim, Kovač, and Zhang [BKKKZ26], who essentially give a complete answer: if $\phi=\frac{1+\sqrt{5}}{2}$ is the golden ratio and $1\leq a_1 < a_2 < \cdots$ is a monotonically increasing sequence of integers such that $\limsup a_n^{1/\phi^{n}}=\infty$ then $\sum_{n=1}^\infty \frac{1}{a_na_{n+1}}$ is irrational. Conversely, for any $1 < C < \infty$ there exists a sequence of integers $1\leq a_1<\cdots$ such that $\lim a_n^{1/\phi^{n}}=C$ where this infinite sum is a rational number. (Further, more general, results are available in [BKKKZ26].) This was formalized in Lean by Baretto.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1051.lean
@decl Erdos1051.erdos_1051.variants.rapid_growth
-- L4DEL: title: Erdős [Er88c] notes that if the sequence grows rapidly to infinity (specifically, if $a_{n+1} \geq C \cdot a_n^2$ for some constant $C > 0$), then the series is irrational
-- L4DEL: intuition: Erdős [Er88c] notes that if the sequence grows rapidly to infinity (specifically, if $a_{n+1} \geq C \cdot a_n^2$ for some constant $C > 0$), then the series is irrational.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

