@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1055.lean
@decl Erdos1055.erdos_1055
-- L4DEL: title: A prime $p$ is in class $1$ if the only prime divisors of $p+1$ are $2$ or $3$
-- L4DEL: intuition: A prime $p$ is in class $1$ if the only prime divisors of $p+1$ are $2$ or $3$. In general, a prime $p$ is in class $r$ if every prime factor of $p+1$ is in some class $\leq r-1$, with equality for at least one prime factor. Are there infinitely many primes in each class?
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1055.lean
@decl Erdos1055.erdos_1055.variants.erdos_limit
-- L4DEL: title: A prime $p$ is in class $1$ if the only prime divisors of $p+1$ are $2$ or $3$
-- L4DEL: intuition: A prime $p$ is in class $1$ if the only prime divisors of $p+1$ are $2$ or $3$. In general, a prime $p$ is in class $r$ if every prime factor of $p+1$ is in some class $\leq r-1$, with equality for at least one prime factor. If $p_r$ is the least prime in class $r$, then how does $p_r^{1/r}$ behave? Erdos conjectured that this tends to infinity.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1055.lean
@decl Erdos1055.erdos_1055.variants.selfridge_limit
-- L4DEL: title: A prime $p$ is in class $1$ if the only prime divisors of $p+1$ are $2$ or $3$
-- L4DEL: intuition: A prime $p$ is in class $1$ if the only prime divisors of $p+1$ are $2$ or $3$. In general, a prime $p$ is in class $r$ if every prime factor of $p+1$ is in some class $\leq r-1$, with equality for at least one prime factor. If $p_r$ is the least prime in class $r$, then how does $p_r^{1/r}$ behave? Selfridge conjectured that this is bounded.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1055.lean
@decl Erdos1055.exists_p
-- L4DEL: title: A prime $p$ is in class $1$ if the only prime divisors of $p+1$ are $2$ or $3$
-- L4DEL: intuition: A prime $p$ is in class $1$ if the only prime divisors of $p+1$ are $2$ or $3$. In general, a prime $p$ is in class $r$ if every prime factor of $p+1$ is in some class $\leq r-1$, with equality for at least one prime factor. Show that for each $r$ there exists a prime $p$ of class $r$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1055.lean
@decl Erdos1055.IsOfClass
-- L4DEL: title: A prime $p$ is in class $1$ if the only prime divisors of $p+1$ are $2$ or $3$
-- L4DEL: intuition: A prime $p$ is in class $1$ if the only prime divisors of $p+1$ are $2$ or $3$. In general, a prime $p$ is in class $r$ if every prime factor of $p+1$ is in some class $\leq r-1$, with equality for at least one prime factor. The classes partition the primes, so `IsOfClass r p` says that $p$ is in class *exactly* $r$: the first conjunct of the successor case rules out every class $\leq r - 1$ for $p$ itself. Without it `IsOfClass 2` would also hold of every prime of class $1$, because at $r = 2$ the "with equality" clause quantifies over the single value $m = 1$ and so says nothing.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

