@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1067.lean
@decl Erdos1067.erdos_1067
-- L4DEL: title: Does every graph with chromatic number $\aleph_1$ contain an infinitely connected subgraph with chromatic number $\aleph_1$?
-- L4DEL: intuition: Does every graph with chromatic number $\aleph_1$ contain an infinitely connected subgraph with chromatic number $\aleph_1$? Komjáth [Ko13] proved that it is consistent that the answer is no. This was improved by Soukup [So15], who constructed a counterexample using no extra set-theoretical assumptions. A simpler elementary example was given by Bowler and Pitz [BoPi24]. This was formalized in Lean by Alexeev using Aristotle and Aleph Prover.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1067.lean
@decl Erdos1067.erdos_1067.variants.infinite_edge_connectivity
-- L4DEL: title: Does every graph with chromatic number $\aleph_1$ contain an infinitely edge-connected subgraph with chromatic number $\aleph_1$?
-- L4DEL: intuition: Does every graph with chromatic number $\aleph_1$ contain an infinitely edge-connected subgraph with chromatic number $\aleph_1$? Thomassen [Th17] proved that the answer is yes: every graph of uncountable chromatic number has a subgraph with infinite edge-connectivity and uncountable chromatic number.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

