@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1075.lean
@decl Erdos1075.erdos_1075
-- L4DEL: title: Let $r\geq 3$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $r\geq 3$. There exists $c_r>r^{-r}$ such that, for any $\epsilon>0$, if $n$ is sufficiently large, the following holds. Any $r$-uniform hypergraph on $n$ vertices with at least $(1+\epsilon)(n/r)^r$ many edges contains a subgraph on $m$ vertices with at least $c_rm^r$ edges, where $m=m(n)\to \infty$ as $n\to \infty$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1079.lean
@decl Erdos1079.erdos_1079
-- L4DEL: title: Let $r\geq 4$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $r\geq 4$. If $G$ is a graph on $n$ vertices with at least $\mathrm{ex}(n;K_r)$ edges then must $G$ contain a vertex with degree $d\gg_r n$ whose neighbourhood contains at least $\mathrm{ex}(d;K_{r-1})$ edges? As Erdős [Er75] says 'if true this would be a nice generalisation of Turán's theorem'. This is true (unless $G$ it itself the Turán graph), and was proved by Bollobás and Thomason [BoTh81]. Bondy [Bo83b] showed that if $G$ has $>\mathrm{ex}(n;K_r)$ edges then the corresponding vertex can be chosen to be of maximum degree in $G$. The number of edges in the neighbourhood of $v$ is the number of edges of $G$ both of whose endpoints are adjacent to $v$. Graphs on a single vertex are excluded, since there every degree is $0$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/108.lean
@decl Erdos108.erdos_108
-- L4DEL: title: For every r ≥ 4 and k ≥ 2 is there some finite f(k,r) such that every graph of chromatic number ≥ f(k,r) contains a subgraph of girth ≥ r and chromatic number ≥ k?
-- L4DEL: intuition: For every r ≥ 4 and k ≥ 2 is there some finite f(k,r) such that every graph of chromatic number ≥ f(k,r) contains a subgraph of girth ≥ r and chromatic number ≥ k?
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1080.lean
@decl Erdos1080.erdos_1080
-- L4DEL: title: Let $G$ be a bipartite graph on $n$ vertices such that one part has $\lfloor n^{2/3}\rfloor$ vertices
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $G$ be a bipartite graph on $n$ vertices such that one part has $\lfloor n^{2/3}\rfloor$ vertices. Is there a constant $c>0$ such that if $G$ has at least $cn$ edges then $G$ must contain a $C_6$? The answer is no, as shown by De Caen and Székely [DeSz92], who in fact show a stronger result. Let $f(n,m)$ be the maximum number of edges of a bipartite graph between $n$ and $m$ vertices which does not contain either a $C_4$ or $C_6$. A positive answer to this question would then imply $f(n,\lfloor n^{2/3}\rfloor)\ll n$. De Caen and Székely prove $n^{10/9}\gg f(n,\lfloor n^{2/3}\rfloor) \gg n^{58/57+o(1)}$ for $m\sim n^{2/3}$. They also prove more generally that, for $n^{1/2}\leq m\leq n$, $f(n,m) \ll (nm)^{2/3},$ which was also proved by Faudree and Simonovits. This was formalized in Lean by Alexeev using Aristotle.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

