@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1119.lean
@decl Erdos1119.erdos_1119
-- L4DEL: title: Let $\mathfrak{m}$ be an infinite cardinal with $\aleph_0 < \mathfrak{m} < \mathfrak{c} = 2^{\aleph_0}$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $\mathfrak{m}$ be an infinite cardinal with $\aleph_0 < \mathfrak{m} < \mathfrak{c} = 2^{\aleph_0}$. Let $\{f_\alpha\}$ be a family of entire functions such that, for every $z_0 \in \mathbb{C}$, there are at most $\mathfrak{m}$ distinct values of $f_\alpha(z_0)$. Must $\{f_\alpha\}$ have cardinality at most $\mathfrak{m}$? This is Problem 2.46 in [Ha74], where it is attributed to Erdős. The question is **independent of ZFC**, so the headline statement carries `answer(sorry)`: it is neither provable nor refutable from the usual axioms of set theory. The answer is yes if $\mathfrak{m}^+ < \mathfrak{c}$ (see `erdos_1119.variants.easy_case`), so the question reduces to the case $\mathfrak{m}^+ = \mathfrak{c}$, where it is undecidable: Kumar and Shelah [KuSh17] produced a model of $\mathfrak{c} = \aleph_2$ in which the answer is yes (with $\mathfrak{m} = \aleph_1$), while Schilhan and Weinert [ScWe24] produced a different model of $\mathfrak{c} = \aleph_2$ in which the answer is no.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1119.lean
@decl Erdos1119.erdos_1119.test.empty_family
-- L4DEL: title: Sanity check: the empty family of entire functions satisfies both the value-bound hypothesis and the cardinality conclusion of `erdos_1119`, for any infinite $\mathfrak{m}$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Sanity check: the empty family of entire functions satisfies both the value-bound hypothesis and the cardinality conclusion of `erdos_1119`, for any infinite $\mathfrak{m}$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1119.lean
@decl Erdos1119.erdos_1119.variants.erdos_wetzel
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: erdos_wetzel
-- L4DEL: intuition: Erdős's theorem [Er64g], answering a question of Wetzel: if $\mathfrak{c} > \aleph_1$, then every family of entire functions taking only countably many distinct values at each point $z_0 \in \mathbb{C}$ is itself countable.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1119.lean
@decl Erdos1119.erdos_1119.variants.erdos_wetzel_ch
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: erdos_wetzel_ch
-- L4DEL: intuition: Erdős [Er64g] also showed that the previous statement fails under the continuum hypothesis: if $\mathfrak{c} = \aleph_1$, then there is an **uncountable** family of entire functions taking only countably many distinct values at each point $z_0 \in \mathbb{C}$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1121.lean
@decl Erdos1121.erdos_1121
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: erdos_1121
-- L4DEL: intuition: If $C_1,\ldots,C_n$ are circles in $\mathbb{R}^2$ with radii $r_1,\ldots,r_n$ such that no line disjoint from all the circles divides them into two non-empty sets then the circles can be covered by a circle of radius $r=\sum r_i$. This is true, and was proved by Goodman and Goodman [GoGo45] (whose proof also generalises to higher dimensions). A generalisation to convex bodies was proved by Hadwiger [Ha47]. An alternative proof is given by Bezdek and Litvak [BeLi16].
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1121.lean
@decl Erdos1121.erdos_1121.variants.higher_dimension
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: higher_dimension
-- L4DEL: intuition: The proof of Goodman and Goodman [GoGo45] also generalises to higher dimensions: if $B_1,\ldots,B_n$ are balls in $\mathbb{R}^d$ with radii $r_1,\ldots,r_n$ such that no hyperplane disjoint from all the balls divides them into two non-empty sets then the balls can be covered by a ball of radius $r=\sum r_i$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1125.lean
@decl Erdos1125.erdos_1125
-- L4DEL: title: Let $f:\mathbb{R}\to \mathbb{R}$ be such that $$2f(x) \leq f(x+h)+f(x+2h)$$ for every $x\in \mathbb{R}$ and $h>0$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $f:\mathbb{R}\to \mathbb{R}$ be such that $$2f(x) \leq f(x+h)+f(x+2h)$$ for every $x\in \mathbb{R}$ and $h>0$. Must $f$ be monotonic? A problem of Kemperman [Ke69], who proved it is true if $f$ is measurable. Erdős [Er81b] wrote 'if it were my problem I would offer \$500 for it'. This was solved by Laczkovich [La84].
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1126.lean
@decl Erdos1126.erdos_1126
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: erdos_1126
-- L4DEL: intuition: If $$f(x+y)=f(x)+f(y)$$ for almost all $x,y\in \mathbb{R}$ then there exists a function $g$ such that $$g(x+y)=g(x)+g(y)$$ for all $x,y\in\mathbb{R}$ such that $f(x)=g(x)$ for almost all $x$. Proved independently by de Bruijn [dB66] and Jurkat [Ju65].
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

