@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1133.lean
@decl Erdos1133.erdos_1133
-- L4DEL: title: Let $C>0$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $C>0$. There exists $\epsilon>0$ such that if $n$ is sufficiently large the following holds. For any $x_1,\ldots,x_n\in [-1,1]$ there exist $y_1,\ldots,y_n\in [-1,1]$ such that, if $P$ is a polynomial of degree $m<(1+\epsilon)n$ with $P(x_i)=y_i$ for at least $(1-\epsilon)n$ many $1\leq i\leq n$, then $$\max_{x\in [-1,1]}\lvert P(x)\rvert >C.$$ Yang [Ya26, Theorem 2.1] proved this, including repeated nodes, by taking $\rho=0$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1133.lean
@decl Erdos1133.erdos_1133.variants.weaker
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: weaker
-- L4DEL: intuition: Erdős proved that, for any $C>0$, there exists $\epsilon>0$ such that if $n$ is sufficiently large and $m=\lfloor (1+\epsilon)n\rfloor$ then for any $x_1,\ldots,x_m\in [-1,1]$ there is a polynomial $P$ of degree $n$ such that $\lvert P(x_i)\rvert\leq 1$ for $1\leq i\leq m$ and $\max_{x\in [-1,1]}\lvert P(x)\rvert>C$. The robust interpolation statement above also implies this result. Erdős in [Er67] could not prove the robust statement even for $m=n$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

