@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1145.lean
@decl Erdos1145.Erdos1145Prop
-- L4DEL: title: Let $A=\{1\leq a_1 < a_2 < \cdots\}$ and $B=\{1\leq b_1 < b_2 < \cdots\}$ be sets of integers with $a_n/b_n\to 1$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $A=\{1\leq a_1 < a_2 < \cdots\}$ and $B=\{1\leq b_1 < b_2 < \cdots\}$ be sets of integers with $a_n/b_n\to 1$. If $A+B$ contains all sufficiently large positive integers then is it true that $\limsup 1_A\ast 1_B(n)=\infty$? Formalization note: There's some discussion in the comments of [erdosproblems.com/28] and [erdosproblems.com/1145] about whether or not $0$ should be included in $A$ or $B$ and has been left purposely ambiguous. Problem 1145 was originally written as $A + B = \mathbb{N}$, which would imply that $0$ would need to exist in $A$ or $B$ to include $1$ in $A + B$. However, it's been made more general and rewritten as "sufficiently large positive integers". The formalization below is the version that includes $0$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1145.lean
@decl Erdos1145.erdos_1145
-- L4DEL: title: Let $A=\{1\leq a_1 < a_2 < \cdots\}$ and $B=\{1\leq b_1 < b_2 < \cdots\}$ be sets of integers with $a_n/b_n\to 1$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $A=\{1\leq a_1 < a_2 < \cdots\}$ and $B=\{1\leq b_1 < b_2 < \cdots\}$ be sets of integers with $a_n/b_n\to 1$. If $A+B$ contains all sufficiently large positive integers then is it true that $\limsup 1_A\ast 1_B(n)=\infty$? A conjecture of Erdős and Sárközy.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

