@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1150.lean
@decl Erdos1150.erdos_1150
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: erdos_1150
-- L4DEL: intuition: Is there some constant $c > 0$ such that, for all large enough $n$ and all polynomials $P$ of degree $n$ with coefficients in $\{-1, 1\}$, $$\max_{|z|=1} |P(z)| > (1 + c) \sqrt{n}?$$
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1150.lean
@decl Erdos1150.erdos_1150.variants.parseval_lower_bound
-- L4DEL: title: The trivial lower bound from Parseval's identity: for any polynomial $P$ of degree $n$ with coefficients in $\{-1, 1\}$, we have $\max_{|z|=1} |P(z)| \geq \sqrt{n+1}$
-- L4DEL: intuition: The trivial lower bound from Parseval's identity: for any polynomial $P$ of degree $n$ with coefficients in $\{-1, 1\}$, we have $\max_{|z|=1} |P(z)| \geq \sqrt{n+1}$. This follows from Parseval's identity: $$\frac{1}{2\pi} \int_0^{2\pi} |P(e^{i\theta})|^2 d\theta = \sum_{k=0}^{n} |a_k|^2 = n+1$$ since each $|a_k|^2 = 1$. The circle average is bounded by the pointwise supremum squared, so $\max_{|z|=1} |P(z)|^2 \ge n+1$, whence $\max_{|z|=1} |P(z)| \ge \sqrt{n+1}$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

