@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1167.lean
@decl Erdos1167.erdos_1167
-- L4DEL: title: Erdős Problem 1167.** Let $r \geq 2$ be finite, $\gamma \geq 2$, and $\lambda$ be an infinite cardinal
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Erdős Problem 1167.** Let $r \geq 2$ be finite, $\gamma \geq 2$, and $\lambda$ be an infinite cardinal. Let $\kappa_\alpha > r$ be cardinals for all $\alpha < \gamma$. Is it true that $$2^\lambda \to (\kappa_\alpha + 1)_{\alpha < \gamma}^{r+1}$$ implies $$\lambda \to (\kappa_\alpha)_{\alpha < \gamma}^r?$$ Here $+$ means cardinal addition, so that $\kappa_\alpha + 1 = \kappa_\alpha$ if $\kappa_\alpha$ is infinite. A problem of Erdős, Hajnal, and Rado.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1167.lean
@decl Erdos1167.erdos_1167.unrestricted_is_false
-- L4DEL: title: The unrestricted version of Erdős Problem 1167 (without the condition $\gamma \geq 2$ from the original Erdős–Hajnal list) is false
-- L4DEL: intuition: The unrestricted version of Erdős Problem 1167 (without the condition $\gamma \geq 2$ from the original Erdős–Hajnal list) is false. Taking $\gamma = 1$, $\kappa_0 = \aleph_1$, $\lambda = \aleph_0$: the premise $2^{\aleph_0} \to (\aleph_1 + 1)^3_1$ holds since $2^{\aleph_0} \geq \aleph_1$, but the conclusion $\aleph_0 \to (\aleph_1)^2_1$ fails since $\aleph_0 < \aleph_1$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1167.lean
@decl Erdos1167.erdos_1167.variants.binary_colors
-- L4DEL: title: Binary-color case.** The $\gamma = 2$ specialization (two color classes)
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Binary-color case.** The $\gamma = 2$ specialization (two color classes).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1167.lean
@decl Erdos1167.erdos_1167.variants.finite_targets
-- L4DEL: title: Finite-target case.** When all $\kappa_\alpha$ are finite, $\kappa_\alpha + 1$ is the ordinary natural-number successor
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Finite-target case.** When all $\kappa_\alpha$ are finite, $\kappa_\alpha + 1$ is the ordinary natural-number successor. Special case of `erdos_1167`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1167.lean
@decl Erdos1167.erdos_1167.variants.infinite_targets
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: infinite_targets
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Infinite-target case.** When all $\kappa_\alpha \geq \aleph_0$ are infinite and bounded by $\lambda$, $\kappa_\alpha + 1 = \kappa_\alpha$, so the hypothesis simplifies to a "pure" stepping-down lemma: $$2^\lambda \to (\kappa_\alpha)_{\alpha<\gamma}^{r+1} \implies \lambda \to (\kappa_\alpha)_{\alpha<\gamma}^r.$$ The condition $\kappa_\alpha \leq \lambda$ is needed to avoid a size obstruction: without it, the conclusion would require a subset of $\lambda$ of size $\kappa_\alpha > \lambda$, which is impossible (see `infinite_targets_needs_bound`).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1167.lean
@decl Erdos1167.erdos_1167.variants.infinite_targets_needs_bound
-- L4DEL: title: The `infinite_targets` variant without the bound $\kappa_\alpha \leq \lambda$ is false
-- L4DEL: intuition: The `infinite_targets` variant without the bound $\kappa_\alpha \leq \lambda$ is false. Taking $\gamma = 1$, $\kappa_0 = 2^{\aleph_0}$, $\lambda = \aleph_0$: the premise $2^{\aleph_0} \to (2^{\aleph_0})^3_1$ holds since $2^{\aleph_0} \leq 2^{\aleph_0}$, but the conclusion $\aleph_0 \to (2^{\aleph_0})^2_1$ fails by Cantor's theorem.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1167.lean
@decl Erdos1167.erdos_1167.variants.r_eq_two
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: r_eq_two
-- L4DEL: intuition: **$r = 2$ case.** The stepping-down from 3-uniform to 2-uniform partition relations: $2^\lambda \to (\kappa_\alpha + 1)_{\alpha<\gamma}^3$ implies $\lambda \to (\kappa_\alpha)_{\alpha<\gamma}^2$. Generalises the classical Erdős–Rado stepping-up/down theorem for pairs.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

