@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1175.lean
@decl Erdos1175.erdos_1175
-- L4DEL: title: Let $\kappa$ be an uncountable cardinal
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $\kappa$ be an uncountable cardinal. Must there exist a cardinal $\lambda$ such that every graph with chromatic number $\lambda$ contains a triangle-free subgraph with chromatic number $\kappa$? Shelah proved that a negative answer is consistent when $\kappa = \lambda = \aleph_1$ (see `erdos_1175.variants.aleph_one`).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1175.lean
@decl Erdos1175.erdos_1175.test.exists_triangle_free_subgraph
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: exists_triangle_free_subgraph
-- L4DEL: intuition: Every graph has a triangle-free subgraph: the bottom subgraph (with no edges) witnesses triangle-freeness, so the existential `∃ H : G.Subgraph, H.coe.CliqueFree 3` in `erdos_1175` is non-vacuous.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1175.lean
@decl Erdos1175.erdos_1175.test.threshold_implies_exact
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: threshold_implies_exact
-- L4DEL: intuition: The threshold variant `threshold_formulation` is stronger than the exact-equality form `erdos_1175`: if every graph with `chromaticCardinal ≥ μ` has the desired triangle-free subgraph, then in particular every graph with `chromaticCardinal = μ` does too.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1175.lean
@decl Erdos1175.erdos_1175.variants.aleph_one
-- L4DEL: title: The case $\kappa = \lambda = \aleph_1$ of Problem 1175: does every graph with chromatic number $\aleph_1$ contain a triangle-free subgraph with chromatic number $\aleph_1$?
-- L4DEL: intuition: The case $\kappa = \lambda = \aleph_1$ of Problem 1175: does every graph with chromatic number $\aleph_1$ contain a triangle-free subgraph with chromatic number $\aleph_1$? Shelah proved that a negative answer is consistent with ZFC [KoSh88, Theorem 1]: there is a model of ZFC containing a graph with chromatic number $\aleph_1$ all of whose triangle-free subgraphs have countable chromatic number. Consistency results about models of ZFC are not directly formalizable inside Lean's fixed model, so only the underlying question is recorded here. Note that this concerns only the choice $\lambda = \aleph_1$; it does not rule out a larger $\lambda$ in `erdos_1175`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1175.lean
@decl Erdos1175.erdos_1175.variants.threshold_formulation
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: threshold_formulation
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Threshold reformulation variant.** Replaces `chromaticCardinal = λ` in the hypothesis of `erdos_1175` with `λ ≤ chromaticCardinal` (a graph of chromatic number ≥ λ has a triangle-free subgraph of chromatic number κ). This is a strengthening of `erdos_1175` (see `erdos_1175.test.threshold_implies_exact`).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

