@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1193.lean
@decl Erdos1193.erdos_1193.parts.i
-- L4DEL: title: Let $A\subset \mathbb{N}$ and let $g(n)$ be a non-decreasing function of $n$ which is always $>0$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $A\subset \mathbb{N}$ and let $g(n)$ be a non-decreasing function of $n$ which is always $>0$. Is the lower density of $$\{ n : 1_A\ast 1_A(n)=g(n)\}$$ always $0$? The answer is trivially no to both questions: indeed if $A=\mathbb{N}$ (assuming $0\in\mathbb{N}$) then $1_A\ast 1_A(n)=n+1$ for all $n$. Presumably Erdős had some additional restrictions on either $g$ or $A$ in mind, but these are not recorded in [Er80].
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

