-- L4DEL: intuition: Roughness criterion (sufficiency for the anchor conditions): if $a < s$ for all $s$ in the leg and the leg stays below $a + P^-(a)$, then $a$ is coprime to the whole leg. Stated via divisibility: no prime factor of $a$ divides any $s$ with $a < s < a + p$ for all prime factors $p$ of $a$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1212.lean
@decl Erdos1212.erdos_1212
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: erdos_1212
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $G$ be the graph with vertex set those pairs $(x,y)\in \mathbb{N}^2$ with $\mathrm{gcd}(x,y)=1$, in which we join two vertices if the differ in only one coordinate, and there by $\pm 1$. Is there a path going to infinity on $G$, say $P$, such that for all $(x,y)\in P$ both $\min(x,y)>1$ and at least one of $x$ or $y$ is composite? The weaker version (only $\min(x,y) > 1$) was solved by C. Stewart via the prime-pair path $(p_k, p_{k+1}) \to (p_{k+1}, p_{k+2})$, as recounted in [Er80]; the compositeness condition forbids those anchors and the question is open.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

