@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/123.lean
@decl Erdos123.erdos_123.variants.powers_2_3
-- L4DEL: title: A simpler case: the set of numbers of the form $2^k 3^l$ ($k, l ≥ 0$) is d-complete
-- L4DEL: intuition: A simpler case: the set of numbers of the form $2^k 3^l$ ($k, l ≥ 0$) is d-complete. This was initially conjectured by Erdős in 1992, who called it a "nice and difficult" problem, but it was quickly proven by Jansen and others using a simple inductive argument: - If $n = 2m$ is even, apply the inductive hypothesis to $m$ and double all summands. - If $n$ is odd, let $3^k$ be the largest power of $3$ with $3^k ≤ n$, and apply the inductive hypothesis to $n - 3^k$ (which is even).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/123.lean
@decl Erdos123.erdos_123.variants.powers_2_3_5_snug
