@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/180.lean
@decl Erdos180.erdos_180
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: erdos_180
-- L4DEL: intuition: If $\mathcal{F}$ is a finite set of finite graphs then $\mathrm{ex}(n;\mathcal{F})$ is the maximum number of edges a graph on $n$ vertices can have without containing any subgraphs from $\mathcal{F}$. Note that it is trivial that $\mathrm{ex}(n;\mathcal{F})\leq \mathrm{ex}(n;G)$ for every $G\in\mathcal{F}$. Is it true that, for every $\mathcal{F}$, there exists $G\in\mathcal{F}$ such that $$\mathrm{ex}(n;G)\ll_{\mathcal{F}}\mathrm{ex}(n;\mathcal{F})?$$ This is the Erdős–Simonovits compactness conjecture. The answer is no: OpenAI [OpenAI26] give a family of connected bipartite graphs, none of them acyclic, for which no single member controls the family extremal number. See `erdos_180.variants.counterexample`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

