@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/204.lean
@decl Erdos204.erdos_204
-- L4DEL: title: Are there $n$ such that there is a covering system with moduli the divisors of $n$ which is 'as disjoint as possible'?
-- L4DEL: intuition: Are there $n$ such that there is a covering system with moduli the divisors of $n$ which is 'as disjoint as possible'? That is, for all $d\mid n$ with $d>1$ there is an associated $a_d$ such that every integer is congruent to some $a_d\pmod{d}$, and if there is some integer $x$ with $$x\equiv a_d\pmod{d}\textrm{ and }x\equiv a_{d'}\pmod{d'}$$then $(d,d')=1$. The density of such $n$ is zero. Erdős and Graham believed that no such $n$ exist. Adenwalla [Ad25] has proved there are no such $n$. This was formalized by van Doorn in Lean using Aristotle.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/205.lean
@decl Erdos205.erdos_205.parts.i
-- L4DEL: title: Is it true that all sufficiently large $n$ can be written as $2^k+m$ for some $k\geq 0$, where $\Omega(m)<\log\log m$?
-- L4DEL: intuition: Is it true that all sufficiently large $n$ can be written as $2^k+m$ for some $k\geq 0$, where $\Omega(m)<\log\log m$? (Here $\Omega(m)$ is the number of prime divisors of $m$ counted with multiplicity.) Barreto and Leeham, using ChatGPT and Aristotle, have proved a negative answer, which was quantified by Tao and Alexeev (see the comments): in fact there are infinitely many $n$ such that, for all $k$ with $2^k<n$, $n-2^k$ has at least $$\gg \left(\frac{\log n}{\log\log n}\right)^{1/2}$$ many prime factors.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/205.lean
@decl Erdos205.erdos_205.parts.ii
-- L4DEL: title: Is it true that all sufficiently large $n$ can be written as $2^k+m$ for some $k\geq 0$, where $\Omega(m)<\log\log m$?
-- L4DEL: intuition: Is it true that all sufficiently large $n$ can be written as $2^k+m$ for some $k\geq 0$, where $\Omega(m)<\log\log m$? (Here $\Omega(m)$ is the number of prime divisors of $m$ counted with multiplicity.) What about $<\epsilon \log\log m$? Barreto and Leeham, using ChatGPT and Aristotle, have proved a negative answer, which was quantified by Tao and Alexeev (see the comments).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/205.lean
@decl Erdos205.erdos_205.parts.iii
-- L4DEL: title: Is it true that all sufficiently large $n$ can be written as $2^k+m$ for some $k\geq 0$, where $\Omega(m)<\log\log m$?
-- L4DEL: intuition: Is it true that all sufficiently large $n$ can be written as $2^k+m$ for some $k\geq 0$, where $\Omega(m)<\log\log m$? (Here $\Omega(m)$ is the number of prime divisors of $m$ counted with multiplicity.) Or some more slowly growing function? Barreto and Leeham, using ChatGPT and Aristotle, have proved a negative answer, which was quantified by Tao and Alexeev (see the comments).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

