@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/206.lean
@decl Erdos206.erdos_206
-- L4DEL: title: Let $x>0$ be a real number
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $x>0$ be a real number. For any $n\geq 1$ let $$R_n(x) = \sum_{i=1}^n\frac{1}{m_i}<x$$ be the maximal sum of $n$ distinct unit fractions which is $<x$. Is it true that, for almost all $x$, for sufficiently large $n$, we have $$R_{n+1}(x)=R_n(x)+\frac{1}{m},$$ where $m$ is minimal such that $m$ does not appear in $R_n(x)$ and the right-hand side is $<x$? (That is, are the best underapproximations eventually always constructed in a 'greedy' fashion?) Kovač [Ko24b] has proved that this is false - in fact as false as possible: the set of $x\in (0,\infty)$ for which the best underapproximations are eventually 'greedy' has Lebesgue measure zero.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/206.lean
@decl Erdos206.EventuallyGreedy
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: EventuallyGreedy
-- L4DEL: intuition: `x` has eventually greedy best Egyptian underapproximations: from some `n₀` on, the best `n`-term underapproximations of `x` are the prefixes of a single strictly increasing sequence of denominators, so each is obtained from the previous by appending the minimal admissible denominator.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

