@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/22.lean
@decl Erdos22.erdos_22.variants.bollobas_erdos_lower
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: bollobas_erdos_lower
-- L4DEL: intuition: The construction of Bollobás and Erdős [BoEr76]: for every $\epsilon > 0$ and $\delta > 0$, for all sufficiently large $n$ there is a $K_4$-free graph on $n$ vertices with independence number at most $\delta n$ and at least $(1/8 - \epsilon)n^2$ edges. Together with `erdos_22.variants.szemeredi_upper` this shows that the Ramsey–Turán density of $K_4$ is $1/8$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/22.lean
@decl Erdos22.erdos_22.variants.fox_loh_zhao
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: fox_loh_zhao
-- L4DEL: intuition: The quantitative strengthening proved by Fox, Loh, and Zhao [FLZ15]: there is a constant $C > 0$ such that for all sufficiently large $n$ there exists a $K_4$-free graph on $n$ vertices with at least $n^2/8$ edges whose largest independent set has size at most $$C \cdot \frac{(\log\log n)^{3/2}}{(\log n)^{1/2}} \cdot n.$$
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/22.lean
@decl Erdos22.erdos_22.variants.szemeredi_upper
-- L4DEL: title: The matching upper bound, due to Szemerédi [Sz72]: a $K_4$-free graph on $n$ vertices whose independence number is sublinear in $n$ has at most $(1/8 + o(1))n^2$ edges
-- L4DEL: intuition: The matching upper bound, due to Szemerédi [Sz72]: a $K_4$-free graph on $n$ vertices whose independence number is sublinear in $n$ has at most $(1/8 + o(1))n^2$ edges. That is, for every $\epsilon > 0$ there is a $\delta > 0$ such that for all sufficiently large $n$, every $K_4$-free graph $G$ on $n$ vertices with $\alpha(G) \leq \delta n$ has at most $(1/8 + \epsilon)n^2$ edges.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

