@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/263.lean
@decl Erdos263.erdos_263.variants.doubly_exponential_all_but_countable
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: doubly_exponential_all_but_countable
-- L4DEL: intuition: Koizumi [Ko25] showed that $a_n = \lfloor \alpha^{2^n} \rfloor$ is an irrationality sequence for all but countably many $\alpha > 1$, in the nondecreasing sense `IsWeakIrrationalitySequence`. The strictly increasing predicate would fail on the whole interval $1 < \alpha < 4/3$, where $a_0 = a_1 = 1$. [Ko25] Koizumi, J., Irrationality of the reciprocal sum of doubly exponential sequences, arXiv:2504.05933 (2025).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/263.lean
@decl Erdos263.erdos_263.variants.folklore
-- L4DEL: title: A folklore result states that any $a_n$ satisfying $\lim_{n \to \infty} a_n^{\frac{1}{2^n}} = \infty$ has $\sum \frac{1}{a_n}$ converging to an irrational number
-- L4DEL: intuition: A folklore result states that any $a_n$ satisfying $\lim_{n \to \infty} a_n^{\frac{1}{2^n}} = \infty$ has $\sum \frac{1}{a_n}$ converging to an irrational number.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/263.lean
@decl Erdos263.erdos_263.variants.sub_doubly_exponential
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: sub_doubly_exponential
-- L4DEL: intuition: Kovač and Tao [KoTa24] proved that any strictly increasing sequence $a_n$ such that $\sum \frac{1}{a_n}$ converges and $\lim \frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n^2} = 0$ is not an irrationality sequence in the above sense. [KoTa24] Kovač, V. and Tao T., On several irrationality problems for Ahmes series. arXiv:2406.17593 (2024).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/263.lean
@decl Erdos263.erdos_263.variants.super_doubly_exponential_eventual
-- L4DEL: title: The same folklore result with the growth hypothesis stated as an eventual lower bound $a_{n+1} \geq c\, a_n^{2+\varepsilon}$
-- L4DEL: intuition: The same folklore result with the growth hypothesis stated as an eventual lower bound $a_{n+1} \geq c\, a_n^{2+\varepsilon}$. Unlike the real-valued `liminf` in `erdos_263.variants.super_doubly_exponential`, this form also covers sequences such as $a_n = 2^{(n+1)!}$, for which the ratio $a_{n+1} / a_n^{2+\varepsilon}$ tends to $+\infty$ and the real `liminf` defaults to $0$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/263.lean
@decl Erdos263.IsIrrationalitySequence
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: IsIrrationalitySequence
-- L4DEL: intuition: We call a **strictly increasing** sequence $a_n$ of positive integers an _irrationality sequence_ if for any sequence $b_n$ of positive integers with $\frac{a_n}{b_n} \to 1$ as $n \to \infty$, the sum $\sum \frac{1}{b_n}$ converges to an irrational number. Note: erdosproblems.com/263 was corrected on 2026-04-02 to require the sequence to be increasing; the pre-correction statement (no monotonicity hypothesis) had a counterexample to Q2 that is not increasing (see `erdos_263.parts.ii` below). Note: This is one of many possible notions of "irrationality sequences". See FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/264.lean for another possible definition.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/263.lean
@decl Erdos263.IsWeakIrrationalitySequence
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: IsWeakIrrationalitySequence
-- L4DEL: intuition: The nondecreasing version of `IsIrrationalitySequence`, as used by Koizumi [Ko25]: the sequence is positive and nondecreasing, and every positive sequence asymptotic to it has irrational reciprocal sum.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/264.lean
@decl Erdos264.erdos_264.parts.i
