@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/264.lean
@decl Erdos264.erdos_264.variants.ko_tao_neg
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: ko_tao_neg
-- L4DEL: intuition: Kovač and Tao [KoTa24] generally proved that any strictly increasing sequence of positive integers $a_n$ such that $\sum \frac{1}{a_n}$ converges and $$ \liminf_{n \to \infty} (a_n^2 \sum_{k > n} \frac{1}{a_k^2}) > 0 $$ is not an irrationality sequence. [KoTa24] Kovač, V. and Tao T., On several irrationality problems for Ahmes series. arXiv:2406.17593 (2024).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/264.lean
@decl Erdos264.erdos_264.variants.ko_tao_pos
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: ko_tao_pos
-- L4DEL: intuition: On the other hand, Kovač and Tao [KoTa24] do prove that for any function $F$ with $\lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{F(n + 1)}{F(n)} = \infty$ there exists such an irrationality sequence with $a_n \sim F(n)$. [KoTa24] Kovač, V. and Tao T., On several irrationality problems for Ahmes series. arXiv:2406.17593 (2024).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/264.lean
@decl Erdos264.IsIrrationalitySequence
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: IsIrrationalitySequence
-- L4DEL: intuition: A sequence $a_n$ of integers is called an irrationality sequence if for every bounded sequence of integers $b_n$ with $a_n + b_n \neq 0$ and $b_n \neq 0$ for all $n$, the sum $$ \sum \frac{1}{a_n + b_n} $$ is irrational. Note: there are other possible definitions of this concept. See FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/263.lean for another possible definition.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/266.lean
@decl Erdos266.erdos_266.variants.all_rationals
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: all_rationals
-- L4DEL: intuition: In fact, Kovač and Tao proved in [KoTa24] that there exists a strictly increasing sequence $a_n$ of positive integers such that $\sum \frac{1}{a_n + t}$ converges to a rational number for all $t \in \mathbb{Q}$ such that $t \ne -a_n$ for any $n$. [KoTa24] Kovač, V. and Tao T., On several irrationality problems for Ahmes series. arXiv:2406.17593 (2024).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/267.lean
@decl Erdos267.erdos_267
-- L4DEL: title: Let $F_1=F_2=1$ and $F_{n+1} = F_n + F_{n-1}$ be the Fibonacci sequence
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $F_1=F_2=1$ and $F_{n+1} = F_n + F_{n-1}$ be the Fibonacci sequence. Let $n_1 < n_2 < \dots$ be an infinite sequence with $\frac{n_{k+1}}{n_k} \ge c > 1$. Must $\sum_k \frac 1 {F_{n_k}}$ be irrational?
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/267.lean
