@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/298.lean
@decl Erdos298.erdos_298
-- L4DEL: title: Does every set $A \subseteq \mathbb{N}$ of positive density contain some finite $S \subset A$ such that $\sum_{n \in S} \frac{1}{n} = 1$?
-- L4DEL: intuition: Does every set $A \subseteq \mathbb{N}$ of positive density contain some finite $S \subset A$ such that $\sum_{n \in S} \frac{1}{n} = 1$? The answer is yes, proved by Bloom [Bl21] (even if 'positive density' is interpreted as 'positive upper density', which is likely what Erdős intended). The theorem below uses the positive-upper-density interpretation; the literal natural-density interpretation is recorded separately. This was formalized in Lean 3 by Bloom and Mehta.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/298.lean
