-- L4DEL: intuition: The literal natural-density interpretation of Erdős Problem 298 follows from [Bl21].
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/299.lean
@decl Erdos299.erdos_299
-- L4DEL: title: Is there an infinite sequence $a_1 < a_2 < \dots$ such that $a_{i+1} - a_i = O(1)$ and no finite sum of $\frac{1}{a_i}$ is equal to 1?
-- L4DEL: intuition: Is there an infinite sequence $a_1 < a_2 < \dots$ such that $a_{i+1} - a_i = O(1)$ and no finite sum of $\frac{1}{a_i}$ is equal to 1? There does not exist such a sequence, which follows from the positive solution to [erdosproblems.com/298] by Bloom [Bl21]. This was formalized in Lean 3 by Bloom and Mehta.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/299.lean
@decl Erdos299.erdos_299.variants.density
-- L4DEL: title: The corresponding question is also false if one replaces sequences such that $a_{i+1} - a_i = O(1)$ with sets of positive density, as follows from [Bl21]
-- L4DEL: intuition: The corresponding question is also false if one replaces sequences such that $a_{i+1} - a_i = O(1)$ with sets of positive density, as follows from [Bl21]. The statement is as follows: If $A \subset \mathbb{N}$ has positive upper density (and hence certainly if $A$ has positive density) then there is a finite $S \subset A$ such that $\sum_{n \in S} \frac{1}{n} = 1$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

