@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/314.lean
@decl Erdos314.epsilon
-- L4DEL: title: $\epsilon(n) = \sum_{n\leq k\leq m}\frac{1}{k}-1$, the overshoot of the minimal block sum reaching $1$
-- L4DEL: intuition: $\epsilon(n) = \sum_{n\leq k\leq m}\frac{1}{k}-1$, the overshoot of the minimal block sum reaching $1$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/314.lean
@decl Erdos314.erdos_314
-- L4DEL: title: Let $n\geq 1$ and let $m$ be minimal such that $\sum_{n\leq k\leq m}\frac{1}{k}\geq 1$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $n\geq 1$ and let $m$ be minimal such that $\sum_{n\leq k\leq m}\frac{1}{k}\geq 1$. We define $$\epsilon(n) = \sum_{n\leq k\leq m}\frac{1}{k}-1.$$ How small can $\epsilon(n)$ be? Is it true that $$\liminf n^2\epsilon(n)=0?$$ This is true, and shown by Lim and Steinerberger [LiSt24], who further proved that, for any $\delta>0$, there exist infinitely many $n$ and $m$ such that $$n^2\left\lvert \sum_{n\leq k\leq m}\frac{1}{k}-1\right\rvert\ll \frac{1}{(\log n)^{5/4-\delta}}.$$ Erdős and Graham (and also Lim and Steinerberger) believe that the exponent of $2$ is best possible here, in that $\liminf \epsilon(n) n^{2+\delta}=\infty$ for all $\delta>0$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/314.lean
@decl Erdos314.mMin
-- L4DEL: title: `mMin n` is the minimal `m` such that $\sum_{n\leq k\leq m}\frac{1}{k}\geq 1$; such an `m` exists for `n ≥ 1` since the harmonic series diverges
-- L4DEL: intuition: `mMin n` is the minimal `m` such that $\sum_{n\leq k\leq m}\frac{1}{k}\geq 1$; such an `m` exists for `n ≥ 1` since the harmonic series diverges.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/315.lean
@decl Erdos315.c₀
-- L4DEL: title: The Vardi constant $c_0=\lim u_n^{1/2^n}=1.264085\cdots$
-- L4DEL: intuition: The Vardi constant $c_0=\lim u_n^{1/2^n}=1.264085\cdots$. With the `0`-indexing of `u`, the $n$-th term $u_n^{1/2^n}$ of the defining sequence is `(u i : ℝ) ^ ((1 / 2 : ℝ) ^ (i + 1))` at `i = n - 1`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/315.lean
@decl Erdos315.erdos_315
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: erdos_315
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $u_1=1$ and $u_{n+1}=u_n(u_n+1)$, so that $\sum_{k\geq 1}\frac{1}{u_k+1}$ and $u_k=\lfloor c_0^{2^k}+1\rfloor$ for $k\geq 1$, where $$c_0=\lim u_n^{1/2^n}=1.264085\cdots.$$ Let $a_1<a_2<\cdots $ be any other sequence with $\sum \frac{1}{a_k}=1$. Is it true that $$\liminf a_n^{1/2^n}<c_0=1.264085\cdots?$$ This is true, and was proved independently by Kamio [Ka25] and Li and Tang [LiTa25]. An earlier interpretation of this question on this site defined $u_1=2$ and $u_{n+1}=u_n^2-u_n+1$ (Sylvester's sequence), which is the same sequence shifted by $1$; we use the phrasing above as more faithful to [ErGr80]. The constant $c_0$ is called the Vardi constant.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/315.lean
@decl Erdos315.u
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: u
-- L4DEL: intuition: The sequence $u_1=1$, $u_{n+1}=u_n(u_n+1)$, indexed here so that `u i` is $u_{i+1}$: `u 0 = 1`, `u 1 = 2`, `u 2 = 6`, `u 3 = 42`, … (so `u i + 1` is Sylvester's sequence $2, 3, 7, 43, \ldots$).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/316.lean
@decl Erdos316.erdos_316
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: erdos_316
-- L4DEL: intuition: Is it true that if $A \subseteq \mathbb{N}\setminus\{1\}$ is a finite set with $\sum_{n \in A} \frac{1}{n} < 2$ then there is a partition $A=A_1 \sqcup A_2$ such that $\sum_{n \in A_i} \frac{1}{n} < 1$ for $i=1,2$? This is not true in general, as shown by Sándor [Sa97]. The minimal counterexample is $\{2,3,4,5,6,7,10,11,13,14,15\}$, found by Tom Stobart. This was formalized in Lean by Mehta.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/316.lean
@decl Erdos316.erdos_316.variants.generalized
-- L4DEL: title: This is not true in general, as shown by Sándor [Sa97], who observed that the proper divisors of $120$ form a counterexample
-- L4DEL: intuition: This is not true in general, as shown by Sándor [Sa97], who observed that the proper divisors of $120$ form a counterexample. More generally, Sándor shows that for any $n\geq 2$ there exists a finite set $A\subseteq \mathbb{N}\backslash\{1\}$ with $\sum_{k\in A}\frac{1}{k} < n$ and no partition into $n$ parts each of which has $\sum_{k\in A_i}\frac{1}{k}<1$. A `Finpartition` has no empty parts, so partitions into at most $n$ parts are tested. Testing exactly $n$ parts would let the singleton $\{2\}$ satisfy the statement vacuously.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

