@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/328.lean
@decl Erdos328.erdos_328
-- L4DEL: title: Suppose $A\subseteq\mathbb{N}$ and $C>0$ is such that $1_A\ast 1_A(n)\leq C$ for all $n\in\mathbb{N}$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Suppose $A\subseteq\mathbb{N}$ and $C>0$ is such that $1_A\ast 1_A(n)\leq C$ for all $n\in\mathbb{N}$. Can $A$ be partitioned into $t$ many subsets $A_1,\ldots,A_t$ (where $t=t(C)$ depends only on $C$) such that $1_{A_i}\ast 1_{A_i}(n)<C$ for all $1\leq i\leq t$ and $n\in \mathbb{N}$? The answer is no. Asked by Erdős and Newman. Nešetřil and Rödl [NeRo85] have shown the answer is no for all $C$ (even if $t$ is also allowed to depend on $A$). Erdős [Er80e] had previously shown the answer is no for $C=3,4$ and infinitely many other values of $C$. See also [774]. The linked proof writes the representation function as `Set.ncard {p : ℕ × ℕ | p.1 ∈ A ∧ p.2 ∈ A ∧ p.1 + p.2 = n}`, which counts the same ordered pairs as `sumRep`, and states the partition condition as a named definition with the same two conjuncts used below. Its `∃ t` additionally carries `1 ≤ t`, which costs nothing: `t = 0` forces `A = ∅`, and `A = {1}` has all representation counts at most `C` for `C ≥ 1`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/329.lean
@decl Erdos329.erdos_329
