@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/346.lean
@decl Erdos346.erdos_346
-- L4DEL: title: Is it true that for every lacunary, strongly complete sequence `A` that is not complete whenever infinitely many terms are removed from it, `lim A (n + 1) / A n = (1 + √5) / 2`?
-- L4DEL: intuition: Is it true that for every lacunary, strongly complete sequence `A` that is not complete whenever infinitely many terms are removed from it, `lim A (n + 1) / A n = (1 + √5) / 2`? The answer is no. A counterexample recorded at [erdosproblems.com/346] has all successive ratios at least `6 / 5`, but has subsequences of successive ratios tending to two different limits, `(1 + √5) / 2` and `(1 + √5) / 2 + 1 / 4`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

