@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/350.lean
@decl Erdos350.erdos_350.variants.strengthening
-- L4DEL: title: If `A ⊂ ℕ` is a finite set of integers all of whose subset sums are distinct then `∑ n ∈ A, 1/n^s < 1/(1 - 2^(-s))`, for any `s > 0`
-- L4DEL: intuition: If `A ⊂ ℕ` is a finite set of integers all of whose subset sums are distinct then `∑ n ∈ A, 1/n^s < 1/(1 - 2^(-s))`, for any `s > 0`. Proved by Hanson, Steele, and Stenger [HSS77]. We exclude here the case `s = 0`, because in the informal formulation then the right hand side is to be interpreted as `∞`, while the left hand side counts the elements in `A`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/351.lean
@decl Erdos351.erdos_351
-- L4DEL: title: Let $p(x) \in \mathbb{Q}[x]$ be a non-constant rational polynomial with positive leading coefficient
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $p(x) \in \mathbb{Q}[x]$ be a non-constant rational polynomial with positive leading coefficient. Is it true that $$A=\{ p(n)+1/n : n \in \mathbb{N}\}$$ is strongly complete, in the sense that, for any finite set $B$, $$\left\{\sum_{a \in X} a : X \subseteq A \setminus B, X \textrm{ is finite}\right\}$$ contains all sufficiently large integers?
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

