@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/352.lean
@decl Erdos352.erdos_352
-- L4DEL: title: Is there some $c > 0$ such that every measurable $A \subseteq \mathbb{R}^2$ of measure $\geq c$ contains the vertices of a triangle of area 1?
-- L4DEL: intuition: Is there some $c > 0$ such that every measurable $A \subseteq \mathbb{R}^2$ of measure $\geq c$ contains the vertices of a triangle of area 1?
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/353.lean
@decl Erdos353.erdos_353
-- L4DEL: title: Let $A\subseteq \mathbb{R}^2$ be a measurable set with infinite measure
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $A\subseteq \mathbb{R}^2$ be a measurable set with infinite measure. Must $A$ contain the vertices of an isosceles trapezoid of area $1$? What about an isosceles triangle, or a right-angled triangle, or a cyclic quadrilateral, or a convex polygon with congruent sides? Koizumi [Ko25] has resolved this question, proving that any set with infinite measure must contain the vertices of an isosceles trapezoid, an isosceles triangle, and a right-angled triangle, all of area $1$. This statement formalizes the leading question, for isosceles trapezoids; the remaining configurations are given as variants below. The area of a polygon is taken to be the Lebesgue measure of the convex hull of its vertices.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/353.lean
@decl Erdos353.erdos_353.variants.congruent_sides
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: congruent_sides
-- L4DEL: intuition: The answer is negative for convex polygons with congruent sides: Kovač and Predojević [KoPr24] prove that there exists a set of infinite measure such that every convex polygon with congruent sides and all vertices in the set has area $<1$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/353.lean
@decl Erdos353.erdos_353.variants.cyclic_quadrilateral
-- L4DEL: title: Every measurable $A\subseteq \mathbb{R}^2$ with infinite measure contains the vertices of a cyclic quadrilateral of area $1$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Every measurable $A\subseteq \mathbb{R}^2$ with infinite measure contains the vertices of a cyclic quadrilateral of area $1$. Kovač and Predojević [KoPr24] have proved that this is true for cyclic quadrilaterals - that is, every set with infinite measure contains four distinct points on a circle such that the quadrilateral determined by these four points has area $1$. The quadrilateral determined by four distinct concyclic points is their convex hull.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/353.lean
@decl Erdos353.erdos_353.variants.isosceles_triangle
-- L4DEL: title: Every measurable $A\subseteq \mathbb{R}^2$ with infinite measure contains the vertices of an isosceles triangle of area $1$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Every measurable $A\subseteq \mathbb{R}^2$ with infinite measure contains the vertices of an isosceles triangle of area $1$. Koizumi [Ko25] has resolved this question, proving that any set with infinite measure must contain the vertices of an isosceles trapezoid, an isosceles triangle, and a right-angled triangle, all of area $1$. Note the area condition forces `a`, `b`, `c` to be affinely independent, so no separate non-degeneracy hypothesis is needed.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/353.lean
@decl Erdos353.erdos_353.variants.right_angled_triangle
-- L4DEL: title: Every measurable $A\subseteq \mathbb{R}^2$ with infinite measure contains the vertices of a right-angled triangle of area $1$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Every measurable $A\subseteq \mathbb{R}^2$ with infinite measure contains the vertices of a right-angled triangle of area $1$. Koizumi [Ko25] has resolved this question, proving that any set with infinite measure must contain the vertices of an isosceles trapezoid, an isosceles triangle, and a right-angled triangle, all of area $1$. Note the area condition forces `a`, `b`, `c` to be affinely independent, so no separate non-degeneracy hypothesis is needed.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/354.lean
