@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/358.lean
@decl Erdos358.erdos_358.parts.i
-- L4DEL: title: Let $A=\{a_1 < \cdots\}$ be an infinite sequence of integers
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $A=\{a_1 < \cdots\}$ be an infinite sequence of integers. Let $f(n)$ count the number of solutions to $$n=\sum_{u\leq i\leq v}a_i.$$ Is there such an $A$ for which $f(n)\to \infty$ as $n\to \infty$? Tao [Ta26] constructed such a sequence with $f(n) \gg \log n$ for all sufficiently large $n$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/358.lean
@decl Erdos358.erdos_358.parts.ii
-- L4DEL: title: Let $A=\{a_1 < \cdots\}$ be an infinite sequence of integers
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $A=\{a_1 < \cdots\}$ be an infinite sequence of integers. Let $f(n)$ count the number of solutions to $$n=\sum_{u\leq i\leq v}a_i.$$ Is there an $A$ such that $f(n)\geq 2$ for all large $n$? This also follows from Tao's construction with $f(n) \gg \log n$ [Ta26].
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/358.lean
@decl Erdos358.erdos_358.variants.one_le
-- L4DEL: title: In [ErGr80] Erdős and Graham further asked whether there is an $A$ with $f(n)\geq 1$ for all large $n$
-- L4DEL: intuition: In [ErGr80] Erdős and Graham further asked whether there is an $A$ with $f(n)\geq 1$ for all large $n$. Egami observed that this holds trivially for $a_n=n$, so they may have intended to count only those representations $$n=\sum_{u\leq i\leq v}a_i$$ that use at least two consecutive terms, which is what $g$ counts. This follows from Tao's construction [Ta26], which gives $f(n)\gg\log n$: see `erdos_358.parts.ii` and `one_le_g_of_two_le_f`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

