-- L4DEL: intuition: Or perhaps even $\prod_{n \leq m < n+k} B_2(m) \ll_k n^2$? van Doorn notes in the comments that this fails for all $k \geq 3$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/367.lean
@decl Erdos367.erdos_367.variants.higher_full_parts
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: higher_full_parts
-- L4DEL: intuition: It would also be interesting to find upper and lower bounds for the analogous product with $B_r$ for $r \geq 3$, where $B_r(n)$ is the $r$-full part of $n$ (that is, the product of prime powers $p^a \mid n$ such that $p^{a+1} \nmid n$ and $a \geq r$). Is it true that, for every fixed $r \geq 3$ and $k \geq 2$, there is some $\epsilon = \epsilon(r, k) > 0$ with $\limsup \frac{\prod_{n \leq m < n+k} B_r(m)}{n^{1+\epsilon}} = \infty$? The webpage writes "for every fixed $r,k \geq 2$ and $\epsilon > 0$", but van Doorn observes in the comments that the intended reading lets $\epsilon$ depend on $r$ and $k$. Since $B_r(m) \mid m$, the product is at most $\prod_{n \leq m < n+k} m$, so the reading with $\epsilon$ universally quantified is false.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/367.lean
@decl Erdos367.erdos_367.variants.k_ge_three_lower
-- L4DEL: title: But this fails for all $k \geq 3$, and in fact $\prod_{n \leq m < n+3} B_2(m) \gg n^2 \log n$ infinitely often
-- L4DEL: intuition: But this fails for all $k \geq 3$, and in fact $\prod_{n \leq m < n+3} B_2(m) \gg n^2 \log n$ infinitely often.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

