@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/388.lean
@decl Erdos388.erdos_388
-- L4DEL: title: Are there only finitely many solutions of $$\prod_{1\leq i\leq k_1}(m_1+i)=\prod_{1\leq j\leq k_2}(m_2+j)$$ with $k_1,k_2>3$ and $m_1+k_1\leq m_2$?
-- L4DEL: intuition: Are there only finitely many solutions of $$\prod_{1\leq i\leq k_1}(m_1+i)=\prod_{1\leq j\leq k_2}(m_2+j)$$ with $k_1,k_2>3$ and $m_1+k_1\leq m_2$? Here $m_1, m_2 \geq 0$, so every factor is positive and the condition $m_1 + k_1 \leq m_2$ makes the two blocks disjoint. Negative $m_1$ would give trivial solutions such as $(-5)(-4)(-3)(-2) = 2 \cdot 3 \cdot 4 \cdot 5$. The source also asks to classify all solutions; see `Erdos388.erdos_388.variants.classification`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/388.lean
@decl Erdos388.erdos_388.variants.classification
-- L4DEL: title: Can one classify all solutions of $$\prod_{1\leq i\leq k_1}(m_1+i)=\prod_{1\leq j\leq k_2}(m_2+j)$$ with $k_1,k_2>3$ and $m_1+k_1\leq m_2$?
-- L4DEL: intuition: Can one classify all solutions of $$\prod_{1\leq i\leq k_1}(m_1+i)=\prod_{1\leq j\leq k_2}(m_2+j)$$ with $k_1,k_2>3$ and $m_1+k_1\leq m_2$? A solution must supply the set of all solutions $(m_1, k_1, m_2, k_2)$. Whether a description of this set is a satisfactory classification is up to human judgement.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/388.lean
@decl Erdos388.erdos_388.variants.general
-- L4DEL: title: More generally, if $k_1>2$ then for fixed $a$ and $b$ the equation $$a\prod_{1\leq i\leq k_1}(m_1+i)=b\prod_{1\leq j\leq k_2}(m_2+j)$$ should have only finitely many solutions
-- L4DEL: intuition: More generally, if $k_1>2$ then for fixed $a$ and $b$ the equation $$a\prod_{1\leq i\leq k_1}(m_1+i)=b\prod_{1\leq j\leq k_2}(m_2+j)$$ should have only finitely many solutions. The source states no condition on $a$, $b$, $k_2$ or the position of the blocks, so this is our reading: $a$ and $b$ are positive integers, we keep the condition $m_1+k_1\leq m_2$ of the main problem (the $k_1$-block lies below the $k_2$-block), and we assume $k_2 > 2$ as well. Without these conditions there are infinite families of solutions: - for $k_2 = 1$ and $b = 1$, take $m_2 + 1 = a\prod_{1\leq i\leq k_1}(m_1+i)$; - for $k_2 = 2$, $a = 1$ and $b = 4$, the identity $(n+1)(n+2)(n+3)(n+4) = Y(Y+2)$ with $Y = (n+1)(n+4)$ even gives a solution with $m_2 + 1 = Y/2$ for every $n \geq 2$; - overlapping blocks give $2\prod_{1\leq i\leq k}(k-1+i)=\prod_{1\leq j\leq k}(k+j)$ for every $k \geq 2$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

