@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/402.lean
@decl Erdos402.erdos_402.variants.equality
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: equality
-- L4DEL: intuition: A conjecture of Graham [Gr70], who also conjectured that (assuming $A$ itself has no common divisor) the only cases where equality is achieved are when $A = \{1, \dots, n\}$ or $A = \{L/1, \dots, L/n\}$ (where $L = \operatorname{lcm}(1, \dots, n)$) or $A = \{2, 3, 4, 6\}$. Note: The source [BaSo96] mentioned on the Erdős page makes it clear what quantifiers to use for "where equality is achieved". See Theorem 1.1 there. TODO(firsching): Consider if we should have the other direction here as well or an iff statement.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/402.lean
@decl Erdos402.erdos_402.variants.szegedy_zaharescu_weak
-- L4DEL: title: Proved for all sufficiently large sets (including the sharper version which characterises the case of equality) independently by Szegedy [Sz86] and Zaharescu [Za87]
-- L4DEL: intuition: Proved for all sufficiently large sets (including the sharper version which characterises the case of equality) independently by Szegedy [Sz86] and Zaharescu [Za87]. The following is taken from [Sz86]. There exists an effectively computable $n_0$ with the following properties: (i) if $n \ge n_0$ and $a_1, a_2, \dots, a_n$ are distinct natural numbers then $\max_{i, j} \frac{a_i}{(a_i, a_j)} \ge n$. (ii) If equality holds then the system $\{a_1, a_2, \dots, a_n\}$ is either of the type $\{k, 2k, \dots, nk\}$ or of the type $\left\{\frac{k}{1}, \frac{k}{2}, \dots, \frac{k}{n}\right\}$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/403.lean
@decl Erdos403.erdos_403
-- L4DEL: title: Does the equation $$2^m=a_1!+\cdots+a_k!$$ with $a_1 0$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Does the equation $$2^m=a_1!+\cdots+a_k!$$ with $a_1 0$. The linked proof gives more than finiteness: it classifies the solutions outright, as $(0,\{1\})$, $(1,\{2\})$, $(3,\{2,3\})$, $(5,\{2,3,4\})$ and $(7,\{2,3,5\})$, so the set below has exactly five elements.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/405.lean
@decl Erdos405.erdos_405
-- L4DEL: title: Let $p$ be an odd prime
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $p$ be an odd prime. Is it true that the equation $(p-1)!+a^{p-1}=p^k$ has only finitely many solutions? Originally proposed by Erdős and Graham [ErGr80]. Brindza and Erdős [BrEr91] proved that there are finitely many such solutions.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/405.lean
@decl Erdos405.erdos_405.variants.nonodd_p
