@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/426.lean
@decl Erdos426.erdos_426
-- L4DEL: title: We say $H$ is a unique subgraph of $G$ if there is exactly one way to find $H$ as a subgraph (not necessarily induced) of $G$
-- L4DEL: intuition: We say $H$ is a unique subgraph of $G$ if there is exactly one way to find $H$ as a subgraph (not necessarily induced) of $G$. Is there a graph on $n$ vertices with $$\gg \frac{2^{\binom{n}{2}}}{n!}$$ many distinct unique subgraphs? Bradač and Christoph [BrCh24] have proved the answer is no: if $f(n)$ is the maximum number of unique subgraphs in a graph on $n$ vertices then $$f(n) = o\left(\frac{2^{\binom{n}{2}}}{n!}\right).$$ The $\gg$ below is read as: some constant $c>0$ works for arbitrarily large $n$. The negation of the proposition on the right is then exactly $f(n) = o(2^{\binom{n}{2}}/n!)$, the form in which Bradač and Christoph [BrCh24] resolved the problem. The linked file states the resolution in that negated form, as `Tendsto fSeq atTop (nhds 0)`. It counts the isomorphism classes occurring as unique subgraphs, whereas `uniqueSubgraphCount` counts their representatives $G\leq H$; uniqueness forces exactly one representative per class, so the two counts agree.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/426.lean
@decl Erdos426.isUniqueSubgraph_bot_bot
-- L4DEL: title: Sanity check: the empty graph `⊥` is a unique subgraph of itself
-- L4DEL: intuition: Sanity check: the empty graph `⊥` is a unique subgraph of itself. Its only subgraph is `⊥` (everything `≤ ⊥` equals `⊥`), which is isomorphic to `⊥` via the identity.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

