@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/44.lean
@decl Erdos44.erdos_44
-- L4DEL: title: Erdős Problem 44:** Let N ≥ 1 and `A ⊆ {1,…,N}` be a Sidon set
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Erdős Problem 44:** Let N ≥ 1 and `A ⊆ {1,…,N}` be a Sidon set. Is it true that, for any ε > 0, there exist M = M(ε) and `B ⊆ {N+1,…,M}` such that `A ∪ B ⊆ {1,…,M}` is a Sidon set of size at least `(1−ε)M^{1/2}`? This problem asks whether any Sidon set can be extended to achieve a density arbitrarily close to the optimal density for Sidon sets.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/44.lean
@decl Erdos44.erdos_44.variants.empty_start
-- L4DEL: title: The case where we start with an empty set (constructing large Sidon sets)
-- L4DEL: intuition: The case where we start with an empty set (constructing large Sidon sets). The answer is yes. Singer [Si38] constructed Sidon sets of size $q + 1$ in $\{1, \ldots, q^2 + q + 1\}$ for every prime $q$. Since $p_{n+1}/p_n \to 1$ for consecutive primes, this gives Sidon sets of size $(1 - o(1))M^{1/2}$ in $\{1, \ldots, M\}$ for every $M$; see erdosproblems.com/30.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

