@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/443.lean
@decl Erdos443.erdos_443.parts.i
-- L4DEL: title: Let $m,n\geq 1$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $m,n\geq 1$. What is $$\# \{ k(m-k) : 1\leq k\leq m/2\} \cap \{ l(n-l) : 1\leq l\leq n/2\}?$$ Can it be arbitrarily large? This was solved independently by Hegyvári [He25] and Cambie (unpublished), who show that if $m>n$ then the set in question has size $$\leq m^{O(1/\log\log m)},$$ and that for any integer $s$ there exist infinitely many pairs $(m,n)$ such that the set in question has size $s$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/443.lean
@decl Erdos443.erdos_443.parts.ii
-- L4DEL: title: Let $m,n\geq 1$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $m,n\geq 1$. What is $$\# \{ k(m-k) : 1\leq k\leq m/2\} \cap \{ l(n-l) : 1\leq l\leq n/2\}?$$ Is it $\leq (mn)^{o(1)}$ for all sufficiently large $m,n$? This was solved independently by Hegyvári [He25] and Cambie (unpublished), who show that if $m>n$ then the set in question has size $$\leq m^{O(1/\log\log m)},$$ and that for any integer $s$ there exist infinitely many pairs $(m,n)$ such that the set in question has size $s$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

