@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/447.lean
@decl Erdos447.erdos_447.parts.i
-- L4DEL: title: How large can a union-free collection $\mathcal{F}$ of subsets of $[n]$ be?
-- L4DEL: intuition: How large can a union-free collection $\mathcal{F}$ of subsets of $[n]$ be? By union-free we mean there are no solutions to $A\cup B=C$ with distinct $A,B,C\in \mathcal{F}$. Must $\lvert \mathcal{F}\rvert =o(2^n)$? In [Er65b] Erdős reported that the estimate $\lvert \mathcal{F}\rvert=o(2^n)$ was proved in unpublished work by Sárközy and Szemerédi.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/447.lean
@decl Erdos447.erdos_447.parts.ii
-- L4DEL: title: How large can a union-free collection $\mathcal{F}$ of subsets of $[n]$ be?
-- L4DEL: intuition: How large can a union-free collection $\mathcal{F}$ of subsets of $[n]$ be? By union-free we mean there are no solutions to $A\cup B=C$ with distinct $A,B,C\in \mathcal{F}$. Perhaps even $$\lvert \mathcal{F}\rvert <(1+o(1))\binom{n}{\lfloor n/2\rfloor}?$$ Solved by Kleitman [Kl71], who proved $$\lvert \mathcal{F}\rvert <(1+o(1))\binom{n}{\lfloor n/2\rfloor}.$$
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

