@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/448.lean
@decl Erdos448.erdos_448.variants.erdos_tenenbaum
-- L4DEL: title: Quantitative form of the (negative) answer to `erdos_448`
-- L4DEL: intuition: Quantitative form of the (negative) answer to `erdos_448`. Erdős and Tenenbaum [ErTe81] showed that the upper density of $\{n : \tau^+(n) < \epsilon \cdot \tau(n)\}$ is in fact $\asymp \epsilon^{1 - o(1)}$, where the $o(1)$ in the exponent tends to $0$ as $\epsilon \to 0$. Equivalently, $\log(\text{upper density}) / \log \epsilon \to 1$ as $\epsilon \to 0^+$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/448.lean
@decl Erdos448.erdos_448.variants.ford
-- L4DEL: title: Erdős and Graham asked whether there is a good inequality for $\sum_{n \le x} \tau^+(n)$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Erdős and Graham asked whether there is a good inequality for $\sum_{n \le x} \tau^+(n)$. This was answered by Ford [Fo08], who proved $$ \sum_{n \le x} \tau^+(n) \asymp x \cdot \frac{(\log x)^{1 - \alpha}}{(\log\log x)^{3/2}}, $$ where $\alpha = 1 - \frac{1 + \log\log 2}{\log 2} = 0.08607\ldots$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/448.lean
@decl Erdos448.erdos_448.variants.hall_tenenbaum_distribution
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: hall_tenenbaum_distribution
-- L4DEL: intuition: Hall and Tenenbaum [HaTe88] further prove that $\tau^+(n)/\tau(n)$ has a distribution function: there is a distribution function `F` (non-decreasing, with $F(-\infty) = 0$ and $F(+\infty) = 1$) such that, for every continuity point $z$ of `F`, the set $\{n : \tau^+(n)/\tau(n) \le z\}$ has density `F z`. Tenenbaum [Te13] proved that `F` is continuous at $z = 1$ and asked to determine its discontinuity points, if any.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/448.lean
@decl Erdos448.erdos_448.variants.hall_tenenbaum_upper_bound
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: hall_tenenbaum_upper_bound
-- L4DEL: intuition: A more precise result of Hall and Tenenbaum [HaTe88, §4.6]: the upper density of $\{n : \tau^+(n) < \epsilon \cdot \tau(n)\}$ is $\ll \epsilon \cdot \log(2/\epsilon)$ as $\epsilon \to 0^+$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/448.lean
@decl Erdos448.tauPlus
-- L4DEL: title: `tauPlus n` (written $\tau^+(n)$) counts the number of $k$ such that $n$ has a divisor in $[2^k, 2^{k+1})$
-- L4DEL: intuition: `tauPlus n` (written $\tau^+(n)$) counts the number of $k$ such that $n$ has a divisor in $[2^k, 2^{k+1})$. Equivalently, the number of distinct values $\lfloor \log_2 d \rfloor$ as $d$ ranges over the divisors of $n$: a divisor $d$ lies in $[2^k, 2^{k+1})$ iff `Nat.log 2 d = k`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/448.lean
@decl Erdos448.tauPlus_le_tau
-- L4DEL: title: Always $\tau^+(n) \le \tau(n)$: the occupied dyadic blocks are the image of the divisor set under `Nat.log 2`, and an image has at most as many elements as its source
-- L4DEL: intuition: Always $\tau^+(n) \le \tau(n)$: the occupied dyadic blocks are the image of the divisor set under `Nat.log 2`, and an image has at most as many elements as its source. This is what makes the $\epsilon < 1$ comparison in the problem meaningful.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/448.lean
@decl Erdos448.tauPlus_six
-- L4DEL: title: Sanity check: $\tau^+(6) = 3$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Sanity check: $\tau^+(6) = 3$. Divisors $1, 2, 3, 6$ lie in dyadic blocks $k = 0, 1, 1, 2$, so the distinct blocks are $\{0, 1, 2\}$. ($\tau(6) = 4$.)
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/448.lean
@decl Erdos448.tauPlus_twelve
-- L4DEL: title: Sanity check: $\tau^+(12) = 4$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Sanity check: $\tau^+(12) = 4$. Divisors $1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12$ lie in dyadic blocks $k = 0, 1, 1, 2, 2, 3$, so the distinct blocks are $\{0, 1, 2, 3\}$. ($\tau(12) = 6$.)
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

