@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/487.lean
@decl Erdos487.erdos_487
-- L4DEL: title: Let $A\subseteq \mathbb{N}$ have positive density
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $A\subseteq \mathbb{N}$ have positive density. Must there exist distinct $a,b,c\in A$ such that $[a,b]=c$ (where $[a,b]$ is the least common multiple of $a$ and $b$)? This is true, a consequence of the positive solution to [447] by Kleitman [Kl71]. Davenport and Erdős [DaEr36] showed that there must exist an infinite sequence $a_1<a_2\cdots$ in $A$ such that $a_i\mid a_j$ for all $i\leq j$, under the assumption that the upper logarithmic density of $A$ is positive.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

