@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/495.lean
@decl Erdos495.erdos_495
-- L4DEL: title: Let $\alpha,\beta \in \mathbb{R}$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $\alpha,\beta \in \mathbb{R}$. Is it true that$$\liminf_{n\to \infty} n \| n\alpha \| \| n\beta\| =0$$? This is also known as the Littlewood conjecture.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/497.lean
@decl Erdos497.erdos_497
-- L4DEL: title: How many antichains in $[n]$ are there?
-- L4DEL: intuition: How many antichains in $[n]$ are there? That is, how many families of subsets of $[n]$ are there such that, if $\mathcal{F}$ is such a family and $A,B\in \mathcal{F}$, then $A\not\subseteq B$? Sperner's theorem states that $\lvert \mathcal{F}\rvert \leq \binom{n}{\lfloor n/2\rfloor}$. This is also known as Dedekind's problem. Resolved by Kleitman [Kl69], who proved that the number of such families is $$2^{(1+o(1))\binom{n}{\lfloor n/2\rfloor}}.$$
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/498.lean
@decl Erdos498.erdos_498
-- L4DEL: title: Let $z_1,\ldots,z_n\in\mathbb{C}$ with $1\leq \lvert z_i\rvert$ for $1\leq i\leq n$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $z_1,\ldots,z_n\in\mathbb{C}$ with $1\leq \lvert z_i\rvert$ for $1\leq i\leq n$. Let $D$ be an arbitrary disc of radius $1$. Is it true that the number of sums of the shape $$\sum_{i=1}^n\epsilon_iz_i \textrm{ for }\epsilon_i\in \{-1,1\}$$ which lie in $D$ is at most $\binom{n}{\lfloor n/2\rfloor}$? A strong form of the Littlewood-Offord problem. Erdős [Er45] proved this is true if $z_i\in\mathbb{R}$, and for general $z_i\in\mathbb{C}$ proved a weaker upper bound of $$\ll \frac{2^n}{\sqrt{n}}.$$ This was solved in the affirmative by Kleitman [Kl65], who also later generalised this to arbitrary Hilbert spaces [Kl70]. See also [395].
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/499.lean
@decl Erdos499.erdos_499
-- L4DEL: title: Let $M$ be a real $n \times n$ doubly stochastic matrix
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $M$ be a real $n \times n$ doubly stochastic matrix. Does there exist some $σ \in S_n$ such that $$ \prod_{1 \leq i \leq n} M_{i, σ(i)} \geq n^{-n}? $$ This is true, and was proved by Marcus and Minc [MaMi62] [MaMi62] Marcus, Marvin and Minc, Henryk, Some results on doubly stochastic matrices. Proc. Amer. Math. Soc. (1962), 571-579.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/499.lean
@decl Erdos499.erdos_499.variants.one_le
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: one_le
-- L4DEL: intuition: A weaker version of Erdős' problem 499, which asks whether for every doubly stochastic matrix, there exists a permutation $σ \in S_n$ with $M_{i, σ(i)} ≠ 0$ and such that $$ \sum_{1 \leq i \leq n} M_{i, σ(i)} \geq 1 $$ Proved by Marcus and Ree [MaRe59]. [MaRe59] Marcus, M. and Ree, R., Diagonals of doubly stochastic matrices. Quart. J. Math. Oxford Ser. (2) (1959), 296-302.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/499.lean
@decl Erdos499.vanDerWaerden
-- L4DEL: title: The conjecture of van der Waerden, which states that the permanent of a doubly stochastic matrix is at least $n^{-n} n!$
-- L4DEL: intuition: The conjecture of van der Waerden, which states that the permanent of a doubly stochastic matrix is at least $n^{-n} n!$. Proved by Gyires [Gy80], Egorychev [Eg81], and Falikman [Fa81]. [Gy80] Gyires, B., The common source of several inequalities concerning doubly stochastic matrices. Publ. Math. Debrecen (1980), 291-304. [Eg81] Egorychev, G. P., The solution of the van der Waerden problem for permanents. Dokl. Akad. Nauk SSSR (1981), 1041-1044. [Fa81] Falikman, D. I., Proof of the van der Waerden conjecture on the permanent of a doubly stochastic matrix. Mat. Zametki (1981), 931-938, 957.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/5.lean
@decl Erdos5.dense_iff_limit_point_set
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: dense_iff_limit_point_set
-- L4DEL: intuition: Weisenberg's remark, as reported on erdosproblems.com/5: since $S$ is closed, asking that $S$ be everywhere dense in $[0,\infty)$ is the same as asking that $S=[0,\infty)$, so `erdos_5.variants.dense` and `erdos_5.variants.limit_point_set` pose the same question.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/5.lean
@decl Erdos5.erdos_5
-- L4DEL: title: Let $C\geq 0$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $C\geq 0$. Is there an infinite sequence of $n_i$ such that $$\lim_{i\to \infty}\frac{p_{n_i+1}-p_{n_i}}{\log n_i}=C?$$ We formalise "an infinite sequence of $n_i$" as a strictly monotone sequence of indices `n : ℕ → ℕ`. Note that the numerator is the gap between the two *consecutive* primes $p_{n_i}$ and $p_{n_i+1}$, which is `primeGap (n i)`, and not the gap between the primes indexed by two consecutive members of the sequence.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/5.lean
@decl Erdos5.erdos_5.variants.banks_freiberg_maynard
-- L4DEL: title: Banks, Freiberg, and Maynard [BFM16] showed that at least $12.5\%$ of $[0,\infty)$ belongs to $S$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Banks, Freiberg, and Maynard [BFM16] showed that at least $12.5\%$ of $[0,\infty)$ belongs to $S$. This is [BFM16, Theorem 1.1]: for any nine nonnegative reals $\beta_1\leq\beta_2\leq\cdots\leq\beta_9$, at least one of the differences $\beta_j-\beta_i$ with $i<j$ belongs to $S$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/5.lean
@decl Erdos5.erdos_5.variants.banks_freiberg_maynard_measure
-- L4DEL: title: The $12.5\%$ claim itself, as deduced in [BFM16, Corollary 1.2]: $\lambda([0,T]\cap S)\geq (1-o(1))T/8$ as $T\to\infty$
-- L4DEL: intuition: The $12.5\%$ claim itself, as deduced in [BFM16, Corollary 1.2]: $\lambda([0,T]\cap S)\geq (1-o(1))T/8$ as $T\to\infty$. Note that the constant $1/8$ is only attained asymptotically and ineffectively; the bound [BFM16] obtain for *all* $T>0$ is the weaker $\lambda([0,T]\cap S)>T/22$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/5.lean
@decl Erdos5.erdos_5.variants.dense
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: dense
-- L4DEL: intuition: In [Er65b], [Er85c], and [Er97c] Erdős asks whether $S$ is everywhere dense (but Weisenberg notes that clearly $S$ is closed so this is equivalent to asking whether $S=[0,\infty]$).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

