@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/5.lean
@decl Erdos5.erdos_5.variants.limit_point_set
-- L4DEL: title: Let $S$ be the set of limit points of $(p_{n+1}-p_n)/\log n$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $S$ be the set of limit points of $(p_{n+1}-p_n)/\log n$. This problem asks whether $S=[0,\infty]$. Since $\infty\in S$ is known (see `erdos_5.variants.westzynthius`), the open content is the equality of the finite part of $S$ with $[0,\infty)$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/5.lean
@decl Erdos5.erdos_5.variants.merikoski
-- L4DEL: title: Merikoski [Me20] showed that at least $1/3$ of $[0,\infty)$ belongs to $S$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Merikoski [Me20] showed that at least $1/3$ of $[0,\infty)$ belongs to $S$. This is [Me20, Theorem 1]: for any reals $\beta_1\leq\beta_2\leq\beta_3\leq\beta_4$, at least one of the differences $\beta_j-\beta_i$ with $i<j$ belongs to $S$. Note that, in contrast with `erdos_5.variants.banks_freiberg_maynard`, the $\beta_i$ are not required to be nonnegative.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/5.lean
@decl Erdos5.erdos_5.variants.merikoski_bounded_gaps
-- L4DEL: title: Merikoski [Me20] showed that $S$ has bounded gaps
-- L4DEL: intuition: Merikoski [Me20] showed that $S$ has bounded gaps. This is [Me20, Corollary 3]: there is a (ineffective) constant $C\geq 0$ such that $S\cap[T,T+C]\neq\emptyset$ for all $T\geq 0$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/5.lean
@decl Erdos5.erdos_5.variants.merikoski_measure
-- L4DEL: title: The $1/3$ claim itself [Me20, Corollary 2]: $\lambda([0,T]\cap S)\geq T/3$ for all $T>0$
-- L4DEL: intuition: The $1/3$ claim itself [Me20, Corollary 2]: $\lambda([0,T]\cap S)\geq T/3$ for all $T>0$. Unlike the $1/8$ of [BFM16], this bound holds uniformly in $T$ with no error term.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

