@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/50.lean
@decl Erdos50.erdos_50
-- L4DEL: title: Let $f$ be the asymptotic distribution function of $\varphi(n)/n$, so that for each $c \in [0,1]$, $f(c)$ is the natural density of $\{n : \varphi(n) < cn\}$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $f$ be the asymptotic distribution function of $\varphi(n)/n$, so that for each $c \in [0,1]$, $f(c)$ is the natural density of $\{n : \varphi(n) < cn\}$. Is it true that there is no $x$ such that the derivative $f'(x)$ exists and is positive?
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/50.lean
@decl Erdos50.erdos_50_schoenberg
-- L4DEL: title: Schoenberg [Sch38] proved that the asymptotic distribution function of $\varphi(n)/n$ exists
-- L4DEL: intuition: Schoenberg [Sch38] proved that the asymptotic distribution function of $\varphi(n)/n$ exists. That is, for any $c \in [0, 1]$, the proportion of integers $n \le N$ satisfying $\varphi(n)/n < c$ approaches a limit as $N \to \infty$. This limit function is the cumulative distribution function of the values of $\varphi(n)/n$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/50.lean
@decl Erdos50.erdos_50_singular
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: erdos_50_singular
-- L4DEL: intuition: Erdős [Er95] proved that the distribution function of $\varphi(n)/n$ is purely singular on $[0, 1]$: it is continuous there, but its derivative is zero almost everywhere on $[0, 1]$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/50.lean
@decl Erdos50.IsDistributionOfPhiRatio
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: IsDistributionOfPhiRatio
-- L4DEL: intuition: A function $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ is the asymptotic distribution function of the values of $\varphi(n)/n$ if for all $c \in [0, 1]$, the natural density of $\{n : \varphi(n) < cn\}$ exists and equals $f(c)$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/50.lean
@decl Erdos50.IsPurelySingularOn
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: IsPurelySingularOn
-- L4DEL: intuition: A function $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ is purely singular (or singular continuous) on a set $s \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ if it is continuous on $s$ and its derivative within $s$ equals zero almost everywhere on $s$ with respect to Lebesgue measure.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/500.lean
@decl Erdos500.erdos_500
-- L4DEL: title: What is $\mathrm{ex}_3(n,K_4^3)$?
-- L4DEL: intuition: What is $\mathrm{ex}_3(n,K_4^3)$? That is, the largest number of $3$-edges which can placed on $n$ vertices so that there exists no $K_4^3$, a set of 4 vertices which is covered by all 4 possible $3$-edges.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/501.lean
@decl Erdos501.erdos_501
-- L4DEL: title: For every $x \in \mathbb{R}$ let $A_x \subset \mathbb{R}$ be a bounded set with outer measure $< 1$
-- L4DEL: intuition: For every $x \in \mathbb{R}$ let $A_x \subset \mathbb{R}$ be a bounded set with outer measure $< 1$. Must there exist an infinite independent set, that is, some infinite $X \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ such that $x \notin A_y$ for all $x \neq y \in X$? If the sets $A_x$ are closed and have measure $< 1$, then must there exist an independent set of size $3$? Known results: Erdős–Hajnal [ErHa60] proved the existence of arbitrarily large finite independent sets. Hechler [He72] showed the answer is **no** assuming the continuum hypothesis.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/501.lean
@decl Erdos501.erdos_501.tests.empty_family_is_valid
-- L4DEL: title: The constant family `A x = ∅` satisfies all hypotheses of the main problem: each `A x` is bounded (the empty set is bounded) and has Lebesgue outer measure 0 < 1
-- L4DEL: intuition: The constant family `A x = ∅` satisfies all hypotheses of the main problem: each `A x` is bounded (the empty set is bounded) and has Lebesgue outer measure 0 < 1. Moreover, all of ℝ is an independent set, showing the conclusion holds trivially. This demonstrates that the hypotheses are non-vacuous: the family `A x = ∅` is a valid input to the theorem, and `ℝ` (which is infinite) witnesses the conclusion.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/501.lean
@decl Erdos501.erdos_501.tests.pair_independent_empty
-- L4DEL: title: Two reals form an independent set for the empty family `A _ = ∅`: neither 0 nor 1 belongs to ∅, so both conditions of `pair_independent_iff` hold
-- L4DEL: intuition: Two reals form an independent set for the empty family `A _ = ∅`: neither 0 nor 1 belongs to ∅, so both conditions of `pair_independent_iff` hold.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/501.lean
@decl Erdos501.erdos_501.tests.singleton_outer_measure_lt_one
-- L4DEL: title: The hypothesis `volume.toOuterMeasure (A x) < 1` is strictly satisfied when `A x = {x}` (a singleton), since Lebesgue measure of a singleton is 0
-- L4DEL: intuition: The hypothesis `volume.toOuterMeasure (A x) < 1` is strictly satisfied when `A x = {x}` (a singleton), since Lebesgue measure of a singleton is 0.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/501.lean
@decl Erdos501.erdos_501.tests.singleton_zero_independent
-- L4DEL: title: A singleton `{0}` is an independent set for any family `A : ℝ → Set ℝ`, as witnessed by `erdos_501.variants.singleton_independent`
-- L4DEL: intuition: A singleton `{0}` is an independent set for any family `A : ℝ → Set ℝ`, as witnessed by `erdos_501.variants.singleton_independent`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/501.lean
@decl Erdos501.erdos_501.tests.unit_interval_measure
-- L4DEL: title: The boundary case: the measure condition `< 1` is sharp
-- L4DEL: intuition: The boundary case: the measure condition `< 1` is sharp. An interval of length ≥ 1 has Lebesgue measure ≥ 1, so it would fail the hypothesis. Here `[0, 1]` has measure exactly 1.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/501.lean
@decl Erdos501.erdos_501.variants.erdosHajnal_finite
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: erdosHajnal_finite
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Erdős–Hajnal (1960): arbitrarily large finite independent sets exist.** For every `n : ℕ` and every family `A : ℝ → Set ℝ` of bounded sets with Lebesgue outer measure `< 1`, there exists a finite independent set of size at least `n`. This was proved by Erdős and Hajnal [ErHa60].
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/501.lean
@decl Erdos501.erdos_501.variants.gladysz_size2
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: gladysz_size2
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Gladysz (1962) [Gl62]: independent set of size 2 in the closed case.** If all the sets `A x` are closed with Lebesgue measure `< 1`, then there exist two distinct reals `x y` such that `x ∉ A y` and `y ∉ A x`. This is a weaker result proved by Gladysz before the full Newelski–Pawlikowski– Seredyński theorem [NPS87].
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/501.lean
@decl Erdos501.erdos_501.variants.hechler_CH
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: hechler_CH
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Hechler (1972) [He72]: the answer to the main question is NO, assuming the continuum hypothesis.** Assuming CH (`ℵ₁ = 𝔠`), there exists a family `A : ℝ → Set ℝ` of bounded sets with Lebesgue outer measure `< 1` for which no infinite independent set exists.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/501.lean
@decl Erdos501.erdos_501.variants.pair_independent_iff
-- L4DEL: title: Two-element sets: independent iff mutual non-membership.** A two-element set `{x, y}` (with `x ≠ y`) is independent for `A` if and only if `x ∉ A y` and `y ∉ A x`
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Two-element sets: independent iff mutual non-membership.** A two-element set `{x, y}` (with `x ≠ y`) is independent for `A` if and only if `x ∉ A y` and `y ∉ A x`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/501.lean
@decl Erdos501.erdos_501.variants.singleton_independent
-- L4DEL: title: Trivial lower bound: a single-element set is always independent.** For any family `A`, any singleton `{x}` is vacuously independent: there are no two distinct elements
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Trivial lower bound: a single-element set is always independent.** For any family `A`, any singleton `{x}` is vacuously independent: there are no two distinct elements.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/502.lean
@decl Erdos502.erdos_502
-- L4DEL: title: What is the size of the largest $A\subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$ such that there are only two distinct distances between elements of $A$?
-- L4DEL: intuition: What is the size of the largest $A\subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$ such that there are only two distinct distances between elements of $A$? That is, $$\# \{ \lvert x-y\rvert : x\neq y\in A\} = 2.$$ Asked to Erdős by Coxeter. Bannai, Bannai, and Stanton [BBS83] have proved that $$\lvert A\rvert \leq \binom{n+2}{2}.$$ A simple proof of this upper bound was given by Petrov and Pohoata [PePo21]. The exact maximum is not known in general: a lower bound of $\binom{n+1}{2}$ follows from the construction of Alweiss (see [503]).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/503.lean
@decl Erdos503.erdos_503
-- L4DEL: title: What is the size of the largest $A \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$ such that every three points from $A$ determine an isosceles triangle?
-- L4DEL: intuition: What is the size of the largest $A \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$ such that every three points from $A$ determine an isosceles triangle? That is, for any three points $x$, $y$, $z$ from $A$, at least two of the distances $|x - y|$, $|y - z|$, $|x - z|$ are equal.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/503.lean
@decl Erdos503.erdos_503.variants.lower_bound
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: lower_bound
-- L4DEL: intuition: Alweiss has observed a lower bound of $\binom{n + 1}{2}$ follows from considering the subset of $\mathbb{R}^{n + 1}$ formed of all vectors $e_i + e_j$ where $e_i$, $e_j$ are distinct coordinate vectors. This set can be viewed as a subset of some $\mathbb{R}^n$, and is easily checked to have the required property.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/503.lean
@decl Erdos503.erdos_503.variants.R2
-- L4DEL: title: When $n = 2$, the answer is 6 (due to Kelly [ErKe47] - an alternative proof is given by Kovács [Ko24c])
-- L4DEL: intuition: When $n = 2$, the answer is 6 (due to Kelly [ErKe47] - an alternative proof is given by Kovács [Ko24c]). [ErKe47] Erdős, Paul and Kelly, L. M., Elementary Problems and Solutions: Solutions: E735. Amer. Math. Monthly (1947), 227-229. [Ko24c] Z. Kovács, A note on Erdős's mysterious remark. arXiv:2412.05190 (2024).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/503.lean
@decl Erdos503.erdos_503.variants.R3
-- L4DEL: title: When $n = 3$, the answer is 8 (due to Croft [Cr62])
-- L4DEL: intuition: When $n = 3$, the answer is 8 (due to Croft [Cr62]). [Cr62] Croft, H. T., $9$-point and $7$-point configurations in $3$-space. Proc. London Math. Soc. (3) (1962), 400-424.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/503.lean
@decl Erdos503.erdos_503.variants.upper_bound
-- L4DEL: title: The best upper bound known in general is due to Blokhius [Bl84] who showed that $$ |A| \leq \binom{n + 2}{2} $$ [Bl84] Blokhuis, A., Few-distance sets
-- L4DEL: intuition: The best upper bound known in general is due to Blokhius [Bl84] who showed that $$ |A| \leq \binom{n + 2}{2} $$ [Bl84] Blokhuis, A., Few-distance sets. (1984), iv+70.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/505.lean
@decl Erdos505.erdos_505.small_dim
-- L4DEL: title: Borsuk's conjecture, small dimensions**: `Borsuk.BorsukConjecture n` holds for $n \leq 3$
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Borsuk's conjecture, small dimensions**: `Borsuk.BorsukConjecture n` holds for $n \leq 3$. Elementary for $n \leq 1$, proved by Borsuk [Bo33] for $n = 2$ and by Eggleston [Eg55] for $n = 3$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/506.lean
@decl Erdos506.erdos_506
-- L4DEL: title: What is the minimum number of circles determined by any $n$ points in $\mathbb{R}^2$, not all on a circle?
-- L4DEL: intuition: What is the minimum number of circles determined by any $n$ points in $\mathbb{R}^2$, not all on a circle? There is clearly some non-degeneracy condition intended here - probably either that not all the points are on a line, or the stronger condition that no three points are on a line. The answer is known for $n > 393$ (see `erdos_506.variants.large_n`) but the problem appears to remain open for small $n$ (see `erdos_506.variants.small_n`).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/506.lean
@decl Erdos506.erdos_506.variants.large_n
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: large_n
-- L4DEL: intuition: For $n > 393$ the answer is $\binom{n-1}{2} + 1 - \left\lfloor \frac{n-1}{2} \right\rfloor$ (Elliott [El67], with the correction of Purdy and Smith [PuSm], also reported in [BaBa94]): this is both a lower bound for every such configuration and is attained, e.g. by a circle with $n - 1$ points together with a single point off the circle.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/506.lean
@decl Erdos506.erdos_506.variants.segre_n_eq_eight
-- L4DEL: title: Segre's observation: the lower bound $\binom{n-1}{2}$ (without the correction of [PuSm]) is already false for $n = 8$, as witnessed by the projection of a cube onto a plane
-- L4DEL: intuition: Segre's observation: the lower bound $\binom{n-1}{2}$ (without the correction of [PuSm]) is already false for $n = 8$, as witnessed by the projection of a cube onto a plane. Hence the minimum number of circles determined by $8$ such points is strictly less than $\binom{7}{2} = 21$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

