@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/532.lean
@decl Erdos532.erdos_532
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: erdos_532
-- L4DEL: intuition: If $\mathbb{N}$ is 2-coloured then is there some infinite set $A\subseteq \mathbb{N}$ such that all finite subset sums$$ \sum_{n\in S}n$$(as $S$ ranges over all non-empty finite subsets of $A$) are monochromatic? Asked by Graham and Rothschild. Proved by Hindman [Hi74] (for any number of colours).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/533.lean
@decl Erdos533.erdos_533.variants.delta_four_eq_zero
-- L4DEL: title: The contrasting positive result $\delta_3(4) = 0$ of Erdős, Hajnal, Simonovits, Sós, and Szemerédi [EHSSS94]: the $K_4$ analogue of `erdos_533` is **true**
-- L4DEL: intuition: The contrasting positive result $\delta_3(4) = 0$ of Erdős, Hajnal, Simonovits, Sós, and Szemerédi [EHSSS94]: the $K_4$ analogue of `erdos_533` is **true**. For every $\delta > 0$ there is a $c > 0$ such that for all sufficiently large $n$, every $K_4$-free graph $G$ on $n$ vertices with at least $\delta n^2$ edges contains a triangle-free vertex set of size at least $c n$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/533.lean
@decl Erdos533.erdos_533.variants.delta_seven_ge_quarter
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: delta_seven_ge_quarter
-- L4DEL: intuition: The observation $\delta_3(7) \geq 1/4$ of Erdős, Hajnal, Simonovits, Sós, and Szemerédi [EHSSS94], via a construction of Erdős and Rogers [ErRo62]: for every $\epsilon, \delta > 0$ and all sufficiently large $n$ there is a $K_7$-free graph $G$ on $n$ vertices in which every triangle-free vertex set has at most $\delta n$ vertices, yet which has at least $(1/4 - \epsilon)n^2$ edges.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/533.lean
@decl Erdos533.erdos_533.variants.ehsss_upper
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: ehsss_upper
-- L4DEL: intuition: The upper bound $\delta_3(5) \leq 1/12$ of Erdős, Hajnal, Simonovits, Sós, and Szemerédi [EHSSS94]: for every $\epsilon > 0$ there is a $\delta > 0$ such that for all sufficiently large $n$, every $K_5$-free graph $G$ on $n$ vertices in which every triangle-free vertex set has at most $\delta n$ vertices has at most $(1/12 + \epsilon)n^2$ edges.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/533.lean
@decl Erdos533.erdos_533.variants.lrss_lower
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: lrss_lower
-- L4DEL: intuition: The matching lower bound $\delta_3(5) \geq 1/12$, from the construction of Liu, Reiher, Sharifzadeh, and Staden [LRSS21] (improving the earlier $\delta_3(5) > 0$ of Balogh and Lenz [BaLe13]): for every $\epsilon, \delta > 0$ and all sufficiently large $n$ there is a $K_5$-free graph $G$ on $n$ vertices in which every triangle-free vertex set has at most $\delta n$ vertices, yet which has at least $(1/12 - \epsilon)n^2$ edges. In particular this refutes `erdos_533`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

