@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/536.lean
@decl Erdos536.erdos_536
-- L4DEL: title: Let $\epsilon>0$ and $N$ be sufficiently large
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $\epsilon>0$ and $N$ be sufficiently large. Is it true that if $A\subseteq \{1,\ldots,N\}$ has size at least $\epsilon N$ then there must be distinct $a,b,c\in A$ such that $$[a, b]=[b, c]=[a, c],$$ where $[\cdot, \cdot]$ denotes the least common multiple?
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/537.lean
@decl Erdos537.erdos_537
-- L4DEL: title: Let $\epsilon>0$ and $N$ be sufficiently large
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $\epsilon>0$ and $N$ be sufficiently large. If $A\subseteq \{1,\ldots,N\}$ has $\lvert A\rvert \geq \epsilon N$ then must there exist $a_1,a_2,a_3\in A$ and distinct primes $p_1,p_2,p_3$ such that $$a_1p_1=a_2p_2=a_3p_3?$$ A positive answer would imply [536]. Erdős describes a construction of Ruzsa which disproves this: consider the set of all squarefree numbers of the shape $p_1\cdots p_r$ where $p_{i+1}>2p_i$ for $1\leq ia_3$ and hence $p_2<p_3$, and so since $p_2p_3\mid a_1\in A$ we must have $2<p_3/p_2$. On the other hand $p_3/p_2=a_2/a_3\in (1,2)$, a contradiction.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/538.lean
