@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/538.lean
@decl Erdos538.erdos_538
-- L4DEL: title: Let $r\geq 2$ and suppose that $A\subseteq\{1,\ldots,N\}$ is such that, for any $m$, there are at most $r$ solutions to $m=pa$ where $p$ is prime and $a\in A$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $r\geq 2$ and suppose that $A\subseteq\{1,\ldots,N\}$ is such that, for any $m$, there are at most $r$ solutions to $m=pa$ where $p$ is prime and $a\in A$. Give the best possible upper bound for $\sum_{n\in A}\frac{1}{n}$. Erdős observed that $\sum_{n\in A}\frac{1}{n}\ll r\frac{\log N}{\log\log N}$, and the order `Θ_r(log N / loglog N)` is known (see `erdos_538.matching_order`). The best possible upper bound is the asymptotic size of the largest reciprocal sum `maxMass r N` over admissible `A`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/538.lean
@decl Erdos538.erdos_538.matching_order
-- L4DEL: title: The reciprocal sum has matching order `Θ_r(log N / loglog N)`: an explicit upper bound for every admissible `A`, together with a witnessing construction achieving the same order
-- L4DEL: intuition: The reciprocal sum has matching order `Θ_r(log N / loglog N)`: an explicit upper bound for every admissible `A`, together with a witnessing construction achieving the same order. This pins the order (up to the one iterated-logarithm factor) but not the best possible upper bound asked for in `erdos_538`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

