-- L4DEL: intuition: Prove that $$R(C_k,K_n)=(k-1)(n-1)+1$$ for $k\geq n\geq 3$ (except when $n=k=3$). Asked by Erdős, Faudree, Rousseau, and Schelp. This problem is #18 in Ramsey Theory in the graphs problem collection.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/551.lean
@decl Erdos551.erdos_551.variants.sufficiently_large
-- L4DEL: title: For sufficiently large $n$ and every $k\geq n$, $R(C_k,K_n)=(k-1)(n-1)+1$
-- L4DEL: intuition: For sufficiently large $n$ and every $k\geq n$, $R(C_k,K_n)=(k-1)(n-1)+1$. Keevash, Long, and Skokan [KLS21] have proved this identity when $k\geq C\frac{\log n}{\log\log n}$ for some constant $C$, thus establishing the conjecture for sufficiently large $n$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

