@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/582.lean
@decl Erdos582.erdos_582
-- L4DEL: title: Does there exist a graph $G$ which contains no $K_4$, and yet any $2$-colouring of the edges produces a monochromatic $K_3$?
-- L4DEL: intuition: Does there exist a graph $G$ which contains no $K_4$, and yet any $2$-colouring of the edges produces a monochromatic $K_3$? Erdős and Hajnal [ErHa67] first asked for the existence of any such graph. Existence was proved by Folkman [Fo70], but with very poor quantitative bounds. (As a result these quantities are often called Folkman numbers.) The current best bounds on the minimal number of vertices $N$ of such a graph are $21 \leq N \leq 786$, where the lower bound is due to Bikov and Nenov [BiNe20] and the upper bound is due to Lange, Radziszowski, and Xu [LRX14].
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

