@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/593.lean
@decl Erdos593.erdos_593
-- L4DEL: title: Erdős Problem 593 (\$500)**: Characterize those finite 3-uniform hypergraphs which appear in every 3-uniform hypergraph of chromatic number $> \aleph_0$
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Erdős Problem 593 (\$500)**: Characterize those finite 3-uniform hypergraphs which appear in every 3-uniform hypergraph of chromatic number $> \aleph_0$. The answer is the set of obligatory finite 3-uniform hypergraphs, represented here on the labelled vertex sets `Fin n`. Two-colorability (Property B) is a necessary condition, see `erdos_593.variants.obligatory_implies_two_colorable`, but it is not sufficient: two triples sharing a pair form a 2-colorable hypergraph that is not obligatory, see `erdos_593.variants.common_pair_not_obligatory`. In the graph case ($r = 2$) the problem is completely solved by Erdős–Galvin–Hajnal [EGH75]: the obligatory graphs are exactly the finite bipartite graphs. A resolution has been claimed by E. Li (arXiv:2606.24882, 2026); at the time of writing erdosproblems.com still lists the problem as open.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/593.lean
@decl Erdos593.erdos_593.variants.common_pair_not_obligatory
-- L4DEL: title: Two triples sharing a pair are **not** obligatory: by [EHR73] (see [EGH75, p
-- L4DEL: intuition: Two triples sharing a pair are **not** obligatory: by [EHR73] (see [EGH75, p. 426]) there are 3-uniform hypergraphs of arbitrarily large chromatic number consisting of edge-disjoint triples, and `commonPair` does not appear in any of them.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/593.lean
@decl Erdos593.erdos_593.variants.commonPair_isTwoColorable
-- L4DEL: title: Two triples sharing a pair are 2-colorable: color the shared pair with one color and the remaining two vertices with the other
-- L4DEL: intuition: Two triples sharing a pair are 2-colorable: color the shared pair with one color and the remaining two vertices with the other.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/593.lean
@decl Erdos593.erdos_593.variants.empty_hypergraph_obligatory
-- L4DEL: title: The empty hypergraph is trivially obligatory**: The 3-uniform hypergraph on `PEmpty` (no vertices, no edges) appears in every hypergraph via the empty injection
-- L4DEL: intuition: **The empty hypergraph is trivially obligatory**: The 3-uniform hypergraph on `PEmpty` (no vertices, no edges) appears in every hypergraph via the empty injection. This degenerate case confirms the definition is well-formed.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/593.lean
@decl Erdos593.erdos_593.variants.graph_case_bipartite_obligatory
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: graph_case_bipartite_obligatory
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Graph analogue — bipartite graphs are obligatory (Erdős–Galvin–Hajnal [EGH75])**: For the 2-uniform (graph) case, a graph of chromatic cardinal $> \aleph_0$ must contain all finite bipartite graphs. Specifically, for every finite bipartite graph `F` and every graph `G` with chromatic cardinal $> \aleph_0$, there is a graph embedding from `F` into `G`. This uses `F ⊑ G` (`SimpleGraph.IsContained`, an injective graph homomorphism), aligned with the injective edge-preserving map used in the hypergraph `Appears` definition. A graph embedding `F ↪g G` would require an induced copy, which the theorem does not provide.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/593.lean
@decl Erdos593.erdos_593.variants.graph_case_no_odd_cycle
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: graph_case_no_odd_cycle
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Graph analogue — no odd cycle is obligatory (Erdős–Galvin–Hajnal [EGH75])**: For every odd $k \geq 3$, there exists a graph with chromatic cardinal $\aleph_1$ that contains no cycle of length $k$. This shows the class of obligatory graphs is strictly smaller than all finite graphs.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/593.lean
@decl Erdos593.erdos_593.variants.nonempty_edges_if_large_chromatic
-- L4DEL: title: No hyperedges implies chromatic cardinal ≤ 1**: A 3-uniform hypergraph with no edges can be properly colored with a single color, so its chromatic cardinal is at most 1
-- L4DEL: intuition: **No hyperedges implies chromatic cardinal ≤ 1**: A 3-uniform hypergraph with no edges can be properly colored with a single color, so its chromatic cardinal is at most 1. In particular, $\chi(H) > \aleph_0$ implies `H` has at least one hyperedge.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/593.lean
@decl Erdos593.erdos_593.variants.obligatory_implies_two_colorable
-- L4DEL: title: Necessary direction**: every obligatory finite 3-uniform hypergraph is 2-colorable
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Necessary direction**: every obligatory finite 3-uniform hypergraph is 2-colorable. This follows from two constructions in [EGH75]. By [EHR73] (see [EGH75, p. 426]) there are 3-uniform hypergraphs of arbitrarily large chromatic number consisting of edge-disjoint triples, so an obligatory `F` is linear (no two edges share two vertices). By the remark preceding [EGH75, Theorem 10.9] there are, for every infinite cardinal $\kappa$, 3-uniform hypergraphs of chromatic number $> \kappa$ all of whose linear sub-hypergraphs are 2-colorable. An obligatory `F` appears in such a hypergraph, hence is 2-colorable.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/593.lean
@decl Erdos593.erdos_593.variants.obligatory_monotone
-- L4DEL: title: Monotonicity of the obligatory property**: If `F₁` appears in `F₂` and `F₂` is obligatory, then `F₁` is also obligatory
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Monotonicity of the obligatory property**: If `F₁` appears in `F₂` and `F₂` is obligatory, then `F₁` is also obligatory. **Proof:** For any `H` with $\chi(H) > \aleph_0$, since `F₂` is obligatory, `F₂` appears in `H` via some injection `φ₂`. Since `F₁` appears in `F₂` via `φ₁`, the composition `φ₂ ∘ φ₁` witnesses that `F₁` appears in `H`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/593.lean
@decl Erdos593.erdos_593.variants.two_colorable_implies_obligatory
-- L4DEL: title: Sufficient direction fails**: it is not the case that every finite 2-colorable 3-uniform hypergraph is obligatory
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Sufficient direction fails**: it is not the case that every finite 2-colorable 3-uniform hypergraph is obligatory. The hypergraph `commonPair` with edges $\{0,1,2\}$ and $\{0,1,3\}$ is 2-colorable but does not appear in the 3-uniform hypergraphs of large chromatic number consisting of edge-disjoint triples constructed in [EHR73], see `erdos_593.variants.common_pair_not_obligatory`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/593.lean
@decl Erdos593.erdos_593.variants.uncountable_vertices_if_large_chromatic
-- L4DEL: title: Vertices must be uncountable**: Every 3-uniform hypergraph with chromatic cardinal $> \aleph_0$ must have an uncountable vertex set
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Vertices must be uncountable**: Every 3-uniform hypergraph with chromatic cardinal $> \aleph_0$ must have an uncountable vertex set. **Proof:** If `V` is countable, there exists an injection `φ : V → ℕ`. Using distinct natural numbers as colors gives a proper coloring, so $\chi(H) \leq \#\mathbb{N} = \aleph_0$, contradicting $\chi(H) > \aleph_0$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/594.lean
@decl Erdos594.erdos_594
-- L4DEL: title: Erdős Problem 594** (Erdős–Hajnal [ErHa66], [Er69b]): Does every graph $G$ with chromatic number $\geq \aleph_1$ contain all sufficiently large odd cycles?
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Erdős Problem 594** (Erdős–Hajnal [ErHa66], [Er69b]): Does every graph $G$ with chromatic number $\geq \aleph_1$ contain all sufficiently large odd cycles? The answer is **Yes**, proved by Erdős, Hajnal, and Shelah [EHS74]. A graph has chromatic number $\geq \aleph_1$ (i.e. uncountable chromatic number) if and only if it admits no proper colouring with countably many colours; this is encoded as `IsEmpty (G.Coloring ℕ)`. The conclusion states that there is some $N$ such that for every $k \geq N$ the graph contains a cycle of odd length $2k + 1$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/594.lean
@decl Erdos594.erdos_594.variants.bot_countably_colorable
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: bot_countably_colorable
-- L4DEL: intuition: Sanity check for the encoding of "chromatic number $\geq \aleph_1$": a countably colourable graph, such as the empty graph on $\mathbb{N}$, does not satisfy the hypothesis `IsEmpty (G.Coloring ℕ)`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/594.lean
@decl Erdos594.erdos_594.variants.erdos_hajnal
-- L4DEL: title: The earlier result of Erdős and Hajnal [ErHa66]: every graph with chromatic number $\geq \aleph_2$ contains all sufficiently large odd cycles
-- L4DEL: intuition: The earlier result of Erdős and Hajnal [ErHa66]: every graph with chromatic number $\geq \aleph_2$ contains all sufficiently large odd cycles. Chromatic number $\geq \aleph_2$ is encoded as the nonexistence of a proper colouring with any set of at most $\aleph_1$ colours.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/595.lean
@decl Erdos595.erdos_595
-- L4DEL: title: Erdős Problem 595 (\$250)**: Is there an infinite graph $G$ which contains no $K_4$ and is not the union of countably many triangle-free graphs?
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Erdős Problem 595 (\$250)**: Is there an infinite graph $G$ which contains no $K_4$ and is not the union of countably many triangle-free graphs? A problem of Erdős and Hajnal [Er87].
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/595.lean
@decl Erdos595.erdos_595.variants.complete_nat_is_union
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: complete_nat_is_union
-- L4DEL: intuition: **The complete graph `⊤` on `ℕ` is a countable union of triangle-free graphs**: we decompose it into the family of star graphs `{H_m}_{m : ℕ}`, where `H_m` is the graph with edges `{m, n}` for all `n ≠ m`. Each star is triangle-free (any two non-center vertices share no edge within the star), and their union covers all edges of `⊤`. **Proof sketch (star triangle-free):** If `{a, b, c}` were a triangle in `H_m`, then each of the three edges `{a, b}`, `{a, c}`, `{b, c}` would pass through `m`. In particular, from `{a, b}` we get `a = m` or `b = m`; from `{b, c}` we get `b = m` or `c = m`. Case analysis shows that two vertices must equal `m`, contradicting the triangle having three distinct vertices.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/595.lean
@decl Erdos595.erdos_595.variants.folkman_finite
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: folkman_finite
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Folkman–Nešetřil–Rödl (finite version) [Fo70, NeRo75]**: For every `n ≥ 1`, there exists a graph `G` (on a finite vertex set) that contains no $K_4$ and whose edges cannot be covered by `n` triangle-free graphs. More precisely: for every `n : ℕ` with `1 ≤ n`, there exist a finite type `V` and a graph `G : SimpleGraph V` with: 1. `G.CliqueFree 4` (no $K_4$), and 2. For every family `H : Fin n → SimpleGraph V` of triangle-free graphs, `G ≠ ⨆ i, H i`. This is the finite analogue of Problem 595. The proofs of Folkman [Fo70] and Nešetřil–Rödl [NeRo75] give different explicit constructions.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/595.lean
@decl Erdos595.erdos_595.variants.K4_not_cliqueFree
-- L4DEL: title: The complete graph `⊤` on `Fin 4` is not $K_4$-free**: `⊤` on `Fin 4` equals the complete graph $K_4$, so it contains $K_4$ as a subgraph and is not $K_4$-free
-- L4DEL: intuition: **The complete graph `⊤` on `Fin 4` is not $K_4$-free**: `⊤` on `Fin 4` equals the complete graph $K_4$, so it contains $K_4$ as a subgraph and is not $K_4$-free. This sanity check confirms the $K_4$-free hypothesis of Problem 595 is non-trivial.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/595.lean
@decl Erdos595.erdos_595.variants.reformulation_edge_colouring
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: reformulation_edge_colouring
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Reformulation via edge colourings**: A graph `G` is a countable union of triangle-free graphs if and only if there is a colouring of the edges of `G` by `ℕ` such that no monochromatic triangle exists. More precisely: `IsCountableUnionOfTriangleFree G` is equivalent to the existence of a map `c : G.edgeSet → ℕ` such that for each `n : ℕ`, the subgraph of edges coloured `n` is triangle-free.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/595.lean
@decl Erdos595.erdos_595.variants.subgraph_of_countable_union
-- L4DEL: title: Monotonicity**: If `G` is a countable union of triangle-free graphs and `H ≤ G` (i.e., `H` is a subgraph of `G`), then `H` is also a countable union of triangle-free graphs
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Monotonicity**: If `G` is a countable union of triangle-free graphs and `H ≤ G` (i.e., `H` is a subgraph of `G`), then `H` is also a countable union of triangle-free graphs. **Proof**: If `G = ⨆ i, G_i` with each `G_i` triangle-free, then `H = ⨆ i, H ⊓ G_i`. Each `H ⊓ G_i` is triangle-free because it is a subgraph of `G_i`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/595.lean
@decl Erdos595.erdos_595.variants.triangle_free_is_union
-- L4DEL: title: Triangle-free graphs are trivially countable unions of triangle-free graphs**: if `G` is already triangle-free, then `G = ⨆ i : ℕ, G_i` where `G_0 = G` and `G_i = ⊥` for `i ≥ 1`
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Triangle-free graphs are trivially countable unions of triangle-free graphs**: if `G` is already triangle-free, then `G = ⨆ i : ℕ, G_i` where `G_0 = G` and `G_i = ⊥` for `i ≥ 1`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/596.lean
@decl Erdos596.erdos_596
-- L4DEL: title: Erdős Problem 596** (Erdős–Hajnal, [Er87])
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Erdős Problem 596** (Erdős–Hajnal, [Er87]). For which graph pairs $(G_1, G_2)$ is it true that (1) for every $n \geq 1$ there is a graph $H$ without a $G_1$ such that any $n$-colouring of $H$'s edges contains a monochromatic $G_2$, and yet (2) for every graph $H$ without a $G_1$ there is an $\aleph_0$-colouring of $H$'s edges with no monochromatic $G_2$? Erdős and Hajnal originally conjectured that no such pair exists; but $(C_4, C_6)$ witnesses it (Nešetřil–Rödl + Erdős–Hajnal). The full question is to characterise the class of all such pairs, recorded here as `answer(sorry)`. See Problem 595 for the specific case $(G_1, G_2) = (K_4, K_3)$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

