@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/602.lean
@decl Erdos602.disjoint_without_infinite_claim.disproof
-- L4DEL: title: Formal disproof of `disjoint_without_infinite_claim`
-- L4DEL: intuition: Formal disproof of `disjoint_without_infinite_claim`. **Counterexample:** Take `α = ℕ`, `I = Fin 2`, with `A 0 = {0}` and `A 1 = {1}`. These are pairwise disjoint, satisfying the only hypothesis. But singleton sets are vacuously monochromatic under any colouring: the only pair `(x, y) ∈ {0} × {0}` is `(0, 0)`, and `f 0 = f 0` trivially. So any colouring makes `A 0` monochromatic, meaning `HasPropertyB` fails.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/602.lean
@decl Erdos602.erdos_602
-- L4DEL: title: Does every almost-disjoint family of countably infinite sets whose pairwise intersections all have size ≠ 1 have Property B?
-- L4DEL: intuition: Does every almost-disjoint family of countably infinite sets whose pairwise intersections all have size ≠ 1 have Property B? Formally: let `α` be any type, let `(A_i)_{i ∈ I}` be a family of countably infinite subsets of `α` such that for all `i ≠ j`, the intersection `A_i ∩ A_j` is finite and `|A_i ∩ A_j| ≠ 1`. Does there exist a 2-colouring `f : α → Fin 2` such that no `A_i` is monochromatic? This is an open question about Property B for almost-disjoint families with a forbidden intersection size of 1. **Note:** This generalises the formulation in which the ground set is `ℕ`. Since every countably infinite set is in bijection with `ℕ`, the two formulations are equivalent, but working over an arbitrary ground type makes the statement apply immediately to, e.g., almost-disjoint families of countable subsets of an uncountable space.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/602.lean
@decl Erdos602.erdos_602.variants.countable_index
-- L4DEL: title: Countable index set case.** If the index set is countable, the answer is yes, and the intersection condition is unnecessary
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Countable index set case.** If the index set is countable, the answer is yes, and the intersection condition is unnecessary. This is Bernstein's Lemma: every countable system of infinite sets has Property B.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/602.lean
@decl Erdos602.erdos_602.variants.disjoint
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: disjoint
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Trivial case: pairwise disjoint families.** If the `A_i` are pairwise disjoint (all intersections are empty, which in particular satisfies `|A_i ∩ A_j| ≠ 1`), then Property B holds trivially. **Proof sketch:** Since each `A_i` is infinite, it has (at least) two distinct elements `a_i` and `b_i`. We can define a colouring that assigns colour 0 to `a_i` and colour 1 to `b_i` for each `i` (using disjointness, these choices don't conflict), and extend arbitrarily elsewhere. Then no `A_i` is monochromatic.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/602.lean
@decl Erdos602.erdos_602.variants.empty_index
-- L4DEL: title: Empty index set.** If the index set `I` is empty (has no elements), then Property B holds vacuously: any 2-colouring works, since there are no sets to be made non-monochromatic
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Empty index set.** If the index set `I` is empty (has no elements), then Property B holds vacuously: any 2-colouring works, since there are no sets to be made non-monochromatic.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/602.lean
@decl Erdos602.erdos_602.variants.single_set
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: single_set
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Intersections of size ≥ 2 suffice.** For a single countably infinite set `A ⊆ α`, there trivially exists a 2-colouring of `α` that makes `A` non-monochromatic: since `A` is infinite, it has two distinct elements, so any colouring that assigns them different colours works.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/602.lean
@decl Erdos602.erdos_602.variants.two_sets
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: two_sets
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Two infinite sets with pairwise intersection of size ≠ 1.** If the family consists of exactly two countably infinite sets `A₀` and `A₁` with `|A₀ ∩ A₁| ≠ 1` (and finite), then Property B holds. **Proof sketch:** - If `A₀ ∩ A₁ = ∅`: the sets are disjoint. Pick distinct `a, b ∈ A₀` and distinct `c, d ∈ A₁`. Colour `b` and `c` with 1, everything else with 0. Then `A₀` has `a` (colour 0) and `b` (colour 1), and `A₁` has `c` (colour 1) and `d` (colour 0), so neither is monochromatic. - If `|A₀ ∩ A₁| ≥ 2`: the intersection contains two distinct points `x` and `y`. Assign `x` colour 0 and `y` colour 1. Both `A₀` and `A₁` contain `x` and `y`, so neither is monochromatic.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/602.lean
@decl Erdos602.erdos_602.variants.unique_index
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: unique_index
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Unique index set.** If the index set has exactly one element (i.e., `[Unique I]`), then Property B holds: any 2-colouring that makes the single set `A (default : I)` non-monochromatic works. This follows from the single-set case.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/602.lean
@decl Erdos602.HasPropertyB
-- L4DEL: title: A family `(A_i)_{i ∈ I}` of subsets of `α` has **Property B** if there exists a 2-colouring `f : α → Fin 2` such that no `A_i` is monochromatic
-- L4DEL: intuition: A family `(A_i)_{i ∈ I}` of subsets of `α` has **Property B** if there exists a 2-colouring `f : α → Fin 2` such that no `A_i` is monochromatic.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/602.lean
@decl Erdos602.IsMonochromatic
-- L4DEL: title: A set `A ⊆ α` is **monochromatic** under a 2-colouring `f : α → Fin 2` if all elements of `A` receive the same colour
-- L4DEL: intuition: A set `A ⊆ α` is **monochromatic** under a 2-colouring `f : α → Fin 2` if all elements of `A` receive the same colour.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/603.lean
@decl Erdos603.erdos_603
-- L4DEL: title: Let $(A_i)$ be a family of countably infinite sets such that $\lvert A_i\cap A_j\rvert \neq 2$ for all $i\neq j$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $(A_i)$ be a family of countably infinite sets such that $\lvert A_i\cap A_j\rvert \neq 2$ for all $i\neq j$. Find the smallest cardinal $C$ such that $\cup A_i$ can always be coloured with at most $C$ colours so that no $A_i$ is monochromatic. GPT 5.4 Pro (prompted by Chojecki) proved there is no uniform bound on such $C$: for every cardinal $C$ there is a family of countably infinite sets $(A_i)$ such that $\lvert A_i\cap A_j\rvert \neq 2$ for all $i\neq j$, and in any colouring of $\cup A_i$ with $C$ colours some $A_i$ is monochromatic.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/604.lean
@decl Erdos604.erdos_604
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: erdos_604
-- L4DEL: intuition: Given $n$ distinct points $A\subset\mathbb{R}^2$, must there be a point $x\in A$ such that $$\#\{ d(x,y) : y \in A\} \gg n^{1-o(1)}?$$ This is the pinned distance problem, a stronger form of Problem 89. Here $n^{1-o(1)}$ means: for every $\varepsilon > 0$ and all large $n$, the bound is at least $n^{1-\varepsilon}$. We do not count the distance $d(x, x) = 0$; this changes the count by one.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/604.lean
@decl Erdos604.erdos_604.variants.average
-- L4DEL: title: Let $d(x)$ be the number of distinct distances from $x$ to the other points of $A$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $d(x)$ be the number of distinct distances from $x$ to the other points of $A$. Erdős [Er75f] conjectured that $$\sum_{x\in A}d(x) \gg \frac{n^2}{\sqrt{\log n}}$$ for every set $A\subset\mathbb{R}^2$ of $n$ points. The sum is at most $n$ times the largest $d(x)$, so this conjecture implies a positive answer to `Erdos604.erdos_604.variants.sqrt_log`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/604.lean
@decl Erdos604.erdos_604.variants.grid_upper_bound
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: grid_upper_bound
-- L4DEL: intuition: The integer grid shows that $\frac{n}{\sqrt{\log n}}$ would be best possible: there are sets of $n$ points in $\mathbb{R}^2$ in which every point has $O(\frac{n}{\sqrt{\log n}})$ distinct distances to the other points. This follows from the bound of Erdős [Er46] on the total number of distinct distances in the grid (see Erdős Problem 89), since the number of distances from one point is at most the total number of distances. For $n$ that is not a square, take $n$ points of the smallest square grid with at least $n$ points.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/604.lean
@decl Erdos604.erdos_604.variants.katz_tardos
