@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/615.lean
@decl Erdos615.erdos_615.variants.fox_loh_zhao
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: fox_loh_zhao
-- L4DEL: intuition: The result of Fox, Loh, and Zhao [FLZ15] disproving the problem: if $f(n) \geq 0$ satisfies $f(n) = o(\sqrt{\log n/\log\log n})$, then for every $\epsilon > 0$ and all sufficiently large $n$ there is a $K_4$-free graph on $n$ vertices with independence number at most $ne^{-f(n)}$ and at least $(1/8 - \epsilon)n^2$ edges; that is, $\mathrm{rt}(n; 4, ne^{-f(n)}) \geq (1/8 - o(1))n^2$. Applied with $f(n) = \log\log n$, this disproves the headline problem, since $ne^{-f(n)} = n/\log n = o(n)$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/615.lean
@decl Erdos615.erdos_615.variants.sudakov
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: sudakov
-- L4DEL: intuition: The complementary result of Sudakov [Su03]: if $f(n)/\sqrt{\log n} \to \infty$ then $\mathrm{rt}(n; 4, ne^{-f(n)}) = o(n^2)$; that is, for every $\epsilon > 0$ and all sufficiently large $n$, every $K_4$-free graph on $n$ vertices with independence number at most $ne^{-f(n)}$ has at most $\epsilon n^2$ edges.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/615.lean
