@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/618.lean
@decl Erdos618.erdos_618
-- L4DEL: title: For a triangle-free graph $G$ let $h_2(G)$ be the smallest number of edges that need to be added to $G$ so that it has diameter $2$ and is still triangle-free
-- L4DEL: intuition: For a triangle-free graph $G$ let $h_2(G)$ be the smallest number of edges that need to be added to $G$ so that it has diameter $2$ and is still triangle-free. Is it true that if $G$ has maximum degree $o(n^{1/2})$ then $h(G)=o(n^2)$? A problem of Erdős, Gyárfás, and Ruszinkó [EGR98]. Simonovits showed that there exist graphs $G$ with maximum degree $\gg n^{1/2}$ and $h_2(G)\gg n^2$. Alon has observed this problem is essentially identical to [134], and his solution in this note also solves this problem in the affirmative.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/618.lean
@decl Erdos618.h2
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: h2
-- L4DEL: intuition: For a graph `G` on `Fin n`, `h2 G` is the smallest number of edges that need to be added to `G` so that the resulting supergraph has diameter at most `2` (every two distinct vertices are adjacent or have a common neighbour) and is still triangle-free (`CliqueFree 3`). By the `sInf` convention on `ℕ`, `h2 G = 0` if no such supergraph exists.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

