-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/624.lean
@decl Erdos624.ExistsEventuallySurjective
-- L4DEL: title: The condition that an integer `m` ensures the existence of a function `f` covering `Fin n` for all large enough subsets `Y`
-- L4DEL: intuition: The condition that an integer `m` ensures the existence of a function `f` covering `Fin n` for all large enough subsets `Y`. The property is invariant under bijection, so we use a representative `Fin n` for a finite set of size `n`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/624.lean
@decl Erdos624.H
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: H
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $H(n)$ be the minimum integer $m$ such that there is a function $f: \mathcal{P}(X) \to X$ where $X$ is a finite set of size $n$, such that for every subset $Y \subseteq X$ with $|Y| \ge m$, the set $\{f(A) : A \subseteq Y\}$ covers $X$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/627.lean
@decl Erdos627.erdos_627
-- L4DEL: title: Let $\omega(G)$ denote the clique number of $G$ and $\chi(G)$ the chromatic number
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $\omega(G)$ denote the clique number of $G$ and $\chi(G)$ the chromatic number. If $f(n)$ is the maximum value of $\chi(G)/\omega(G)$, as $G$ ranges over all graphs on $n$ vertices, then does $$\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{f(n)}{n/(\log_2n)^2}$$ exist? Since $f(n) \asymp n/(\log_2 n)^2$ (see `Erdos627.erdos_627.variants.theta`), the limit, if it exists, is a finite real number.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/627.lean
@decl Erdos627.erdos_627.variants.afm_upper
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: afm_upper
-- L4DEL: intuition: Araujo, Filipe, and Miyazaki [AFM25, Theorem 1.3] improved the upper bound to $$f(n) \leq (3.71943 + o(1)) \frac{n}{(\log_2 n)^2},$$ using recent improvements in the asymptotics of Ramsey numbers. [AFM25] is an arXiv preprint.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/627.lean
@decl Erdos627.erdos_627.variants.conditional
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: conditional
-- L4DEL: intuition: Araujo, Filipe, and Miyazaki [AFM25, Theorem 1.2]: suppose that $R(s,t) \leq R(k)$ for all $s, t, k \in \mathbb{N}$ with $st \leq k^2$ (their Conjecture 1.1), and that $\lim_{k \to \infty} \frac{\log_2 R(k)}{k}$ exists and equals $\ell$. Then $$f(n) = (\ell^2 + o(1)) \frac{n}{(\log_2 n)^2},$$ so the limit in `Erdos627.erdos_627` exists and equals $\ell^2$. Here $R(s,t)$ is the two-colour Ramsey number and $R(k) = R(k,k)$ (see [77]). [AFM25] is an arXiv preprint.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/627.lean
@decl Erdos627.erdos_627.variants.erdos_1961
-- L4DEL: title: Erdős [Er61d] proved that for every (large) $n$ there is a graph on $n$ vertices with $\omega(G) = 2$ and $$\chi(G) \gg \frac{n^{1/2}}{\log n},$$ whence $f(n) \gg n^{1/2}/\log n$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Erdős [Er61d] proved that for every (large) $n$ there is a graph on $n$ vertices with $\omega(G) = 2$ and $$\chi(G) \gg \frac{n^{1/2}}{\log n},$$ whence $f(n) \gg n^{1/2}/\log n$.
