-- L4DEL: intuition: `g n` is the largest `t` such that there exist integers `2 ≤ a 0 < a 1 < ⋯ < a (t - 1) < n` whose greatest prime factors are strictly decreasing.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/649.lean
@decl Erdos649.erdos_649
-- L4DEL: title: Let $P(m)$ denote the greatest prime factor of $m$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $P(m)$ denote the greatest prime factor of $m$. Is it true that, for any two primes $p,q$, there exists some integer $n$ such that $P(n)=p$ and $P(n+1)=q$? In fact, the answer to this question as written is easily seen to be no, since there are no solutions to $2^k\equiv -1\pmod{7}$, and hence this fails with $p=2$ and $q=7$. It is possible that Erdős meant to exclude such obstructions, by amending this to 'odd primes' or 'all sufficiently large primes' or such. The statement below assumes $p \neq q$: for $p = q$ no such $n$ exists, because $p$ would divide both $n$ and $n+1$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/649.lean
@decl Erdos649.erdos_649.variants.no_solution_two_seven
-- L4DEL: title: In fact, the answer to this question as written is easily seen to be no, since there are no solutions to $2^k\equiv -1\pmod{7}$, and hence this fails with $p=2$ and $q=7$
-- L4DEL: intuition: In fact, the answer to this question as written is easily seen to be no, since there are no solutions to $2^k\equiv -1\pmod{7}$, and hence this fails with $p=2$ and $q=7$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/649.lean
@decl Erdos649.erdos_649.variants.rmm_2020
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: rmm_2020
-- L4DEL: intuition: Problem 6 in the 12th Romanian Master of Mathematics Competitions in 2020 was to prove that there exist infinitely many odd primes $p$ such that, for every $n$, $P(n)P(n+1)\neq 2p$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/649.lean
@decl Erdos649.erdos_649.variants.sampaio
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: sampaio
-- L4DEL: intuition: Sampaio independently observed that the answer to Erdős' original problem is no if one of the primes can be $2$ - for example this is false with $p=19$ and $q=2$, since if $n+1=2^k$ and $19\mid n$ then (since $2$ is a primitive root modulo $19$) we must have $18\mid k$, and hence $73\mid 2^{18}-1\mid n$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/649.lean
@decl Erdos649.erdos_649.variants.tong
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: tong
-- L4DEL: intuition: Even with such amendments, this problem is false in a strong sense: Alan Tong has provided the following elegant elementary proof that, for any given prime $p$, there are infinitely many primes $q$ such that this statement is false: let $m$ be the product of all primes $\leq p$, and choose a prime $q$ congruent to $-1$ modulo $4m$. If $p$ is the greatest prime divisor of $n$ then, using quadratic reciprocity, every prime divisor of $n$ is a quadratic residue modulo $q$, and hence $n$ is a quadratic residue modulo $q$. On the other hand, since $q\equiv 3\pmod{4}$ we know that $-1$ is not a quadratic residue modulo $q$, and hence $n\not\equiv -1\pmod{q}$, so it is impossible for $q\mid n+1$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/649.lean
@decl Erdos649.erdos_649.variants.tong_question
-- L4DEL: title: Tong asks whether, for any given odd prime $q$, there are infinitely many primes $p$ such that there is no integer $n$ with $P(n)=p$ and $P(n+1)=q$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Tong asks whether, for any given odd prime $q$, there are infinitely many primes $p$ such that there is no integer $n$ with $P(n)=p$ and $P(n+1)=q$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/65.lean
@decl Erdos65.erdos_65.parts.i
-- L4DEL: title: Let $G$ be a graph with $n$ vertices and $kn$ edges, and $a_10$ and $k>0$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $G$ be a graph with $n$ vertices and $kn$ edges, and $a_10$ and $k>0$. Is it true that $$\sum\frac{1}{a_i}\gg \log k?$$ Gyárfás, Komlós, and Szemerédi [GKS84] have proved that this sum is $\gg \log k$, so that only the second question remains.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

