@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/653.lean
@decl Erdos653.erdos_653
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: erdos_653
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $x_1,\ldots,x_n\in \mathbb{R}^2$ and let $R(x_i)=\#\{ \lvert x_j-x_i\rvert : j\neq i\}$, where the points are ordered such that $$R(x_1)\leq \cdots \leq R(x_n).$$ Let $g(n)$ be the maximum number of distinct values the $R(x_i)$ can take. Is it true that $g(n) \geq (1-o(1))n$?
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/655.lean
@decl Erdos655.erdos_655
-- L4DEL: title: Let $x_1,\ldots,x_n\in \mathbb{R}^2$ be such that no circle whose centre is one of the $x_i$ contains three other points
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $x_1,\ldots,x_n\in \mathbb{R}^2$ be such that no circle whose centre is one of the $x_i$ contains three other points. Are there at least $$(1+c)\frac{n}{2}$$ distinct distances determined between the $x_i$, for some constant $c>0$ and all $n$ sufficiently large? The answer is **no**: as Zach Hunter observed, the regular `n`-gon (`n` points equally spaced on a circle) is valid and determines only `⌊n/2⌋ < (1+c)n/2` distinct distances, for every `c > 0`. (In the spirit of related conjectures of Erdős and others, presumably some kind of assumption that the points are in general position was intended; see `erdos_655.variants.general_position`.) The disproof — the regular `n`-gon construction together with its supporting lemmas — is formalised at the linked commit.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/655.lean
@decl Erdos655.erdos_655.variants.general_position
-- L4DEL: title: Let $x_1,\ldots,x_n\in \mathbb{R}^2$ be such that no circle whose centre is one of the $x_i$ contains three other points
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $x_1,\ldots,x_n\in \mathbb{R}^2$ be such that no circle whose centre is one of the $x_i$ contains three other points. Are there at least$$(1+c)\frac{n}{2}$$ distinct distances determined between the $x_i$, for some constant $c>0$ and all $n$ sufficiently large? In the spirit of related conjectures of Erdős and others, presumably some kind of assumption that the points are in general position (e.g. no three on a line and no four on a circle) was intended.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

