@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/661.lean
@decl Erdos661.erdos_661
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: erdos_661
-- L4DEL: intuition: Are there, for all large $n$, some points $x_1,\ldots,x_n,y_1,\ldots,y_n\in \mathbb{R}^2$ such that the number of distinct distances $d(x_i,y_j)$ is $$o\left(\frac{n}{\sqrt{\log n}}\right)?$$ We require all $2n$ points to be distinct; without any distinctness one could take all points equal and get a single distance. Writing $F(n)$ for the minimal number of such distances (`minimalCrossDistances ℝ² n`), the question asks whether $F(n) = o(n / \sqrt{\log n})$. This is equivalent to the existence, for all large $n$, of configurations whose count is at most $g(n)$ for some fixed $g(n) = o(n / \sqrt{\log n})$, since the infimum is attained. The source adds that one can also ask this for points in $\mathbb{R}^3$. Taken literally, the question is easy there, since a cubic grid of $2n$ points determines only $O(n^{2/3})$ distinct distances, so we do not state it.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/661.lean
@decl Erdos661.erdos_661.variants.grid_upper_bound
-- L4DEL: title: Take $2n$ points of the smallest square grid with at least $2n$ points; by [Er46] they determine $O(n / \sqrt{\log n})$ distinct distances
-- L4DEL: intuition: Take $2n$ points of the smallest square grid with at least $2n$ points; by [Er46] they determine $O(n / \sqrt{\log n})$ distinct distances. Splitting them into two halves of $n$ points shows that $F(n) = O(n / \sqrt{\log n})$. So the question is whether this trivial bound can be improved by more than a constant factor.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/661.lean
@decl Erdos661.erdos_661.variants.le_minimalDistinctDistances
-- L4DEL: title: Every split of a set of $2n$ points into two halves of $n$ points gives a configuration for $F(n)$, and the distances between the halves are among the distances of the whole set
-- L4DEL: intuition: Every split of a set of $2n$ points into two halves of $n$ points gives a configuration for $F(n)$, and the distances between the halves are among the distances of the whole set. Hence $F(n) \leq f(2n)$, where $f(2n)$ is the minimal number of distinct distances determined by $2n$ points in $\mathbb{R}^2$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/661.lean
@decl Erdos661.erdos_661.variants.lenz
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: lenz
-- L4DEL: intuition: In $\mathbb{R}^4$ Lenz observed (as stated on erdosproblems.com/661) that for every $n$ there are $2n$ distinct points $x_1,\ldots,x_n,y_1,\ldots,y_n\in \mathbb{R}^4$ with $d(x_i,y_j)=1$ for all $i,j$: take the $x_i$ on the circle of radius $1/\sqrt{2}$ about the origin in the plane of the first two coordinates, and the $y_j$ on the circle of radius $1/\sqrt{2}$ about the origin in the plane of the last two coordinates.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/661.lean
@decl Erdos661.erdos_661.variants.little_o_distinct_distances
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: little_o_distinct_distances
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $F(2n)$ be the minimal number of distinct distances $d(x_i, y_j)$ for $2n$ distinct points $x_1,\ldots,x_n,y_1,\ldots,y_n\in \mathbb{R}^2$, and $f(2n)$ the minimal number of distinct distances determined by any $2n$ points in $\mathbb{R}^2$. Is $F = o(f)$? Here $F(2n)$ of erdosproblems.com is `minimalCrossDistances ℝ² n` (indexed by $n$ points on each side) and $f(2n)$ is `minimalDistinctDistances ℝ² (2 * n)`. See also Erdős Problem 89.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/661.lean
@decl Erdos661.minimalCrossDistances
-- L4DEL: title: The minimal number of distinct distances $d(x_i, y_j)$ over all choices of $2n$ distinct points $x_1, \ldots, x_n, y_1, \ldots, y_n$ in `X`
-- L4DEL: intuition: The minimal number of distinct distances $d(x_i, y_j)$ over all choices of $2n$ distinct points $x_1, \ldots, x_n, y_1, \ldots, y_n$ in `X`. The $x_i$ form a set `A` of $n$ points, the $y_j$ form a set `B` of $n$ points, and `A` and `B` are disjoint, so all $2n$ points are distinct.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/664.lean
@decl Erdos664.erdos_664.variants.block_design
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: block_design
-- L4DEL: intuition: In [Er81] the condition $\lvert A_i\cap A_j\rvert\leq 1$ for all $i\neq j$ is replaced by every two points in $\{1,\ldots,n\}$ being contained in exactly one $A_i$, that is, $A_1,\ldots,A_m$ is a pairwise balanced block design. The bound $\lvert A_i\rvert>c\sqrt{n}$ is kept, only the condition $c<1$ is omitted. This weaker version remains open, although Alon conjectures the answer there to also be no.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/665.lean
@decl Erdos665.erdos_665
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: erdos_665
-- L4DEL: intuition: A pairwise balanced design for $\{1,\ldots,n\}$ is a collection of sets $A_1,\ldots,A_m\subseteq \{1,\ldots,n\}$ such that $2\leq \lvert A_i\rvert 0$ and, for all large $n$, a pairwise balanced design such that $$\lvert A_i\rvert > n^{1/2}-C$$ for all $1\leq i\leq m$?
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/666.lean
@decl Erdos666.erdos_666
-- L4DEL: title: Let $Q_n$ be the $n$-dimensional hypercube graph (so that $Q_n$ has $2^n$ vertices and $n2^{n-1}$ edges)
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $Q_n$ be the $n$-dimensional hypercube graph (so that $Q_n$ has $2^n$ vertices and $n2^{n-1}$ edges). Is it true that, for every $\epsilon>0$, if $n$ is sufficiently large, every subgraph of $Q_n$ with $$\geq \epsilon n2^{n-1}$$ many edges contains a $C_6$? The answer to this problem is no: Chung [Ch92] and Brouwer, Dejter, and Thomassen [BDT93] constructed an edge-partition of $Q_n$ into four subgraphs, each containing no $C_6$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/667.lean
@decl Erdos667.c
-- L4DEL: title: The growth exponent `c(p, q) = liminf_{n → ∞} (log H(n)) / (log n)`
-- L4DEL: intuition: The growth exponent `c(p, q) = liminf_{n → ∞} (log H(n)) / (log n)`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/667.lean
@decl Erdos667.erdos_667
-- L4DEL: title: Let $p, q \geq 1$ be fixed integers
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $p, q \geq 1$ be fixed integers. Define $H(n) = H(n; p, q)$ to be the largest $m$ such that any graph on $n$ vertices where every set of $p$ vertices spans at least $q$ edges must contain a complete graph on $m$ vertices. Is $$ c(p, q) = \liminf_{n \to \infty} \frac{\log H(n)}{\log n} $$ a strictly increasing function of $q$ for $1 \leq q \leq \binom{p-1}{2} + 1$? A problem of Erdős, Faudree, Rousseau, and Schelp. When $q = 1$ this corresponds exactly to the classical Ramsey problem, and hence $\tfrac{1}{p-1} \leq c(p, 1) \leq \tfrac{2}{p+1}$ for $p \geq 3$. It is easy to see that if $q = \binom{p-1}{2} + 1$ then $c(p, q) = 1$. erdosproblems.com/667 also states that Erdős, Faudree, Rousseau and Schelp showed $c(p, \binom{p-1}{2}) \leq 1/2$. This cannot hold for all $p$: for $p = 6$, $q = 10$, the complement of a $(6, 10)$-locally dense graph is $C_6$-free, so Bondy–Simonovits together with $\alpha(F) \geq n^2 / (2e(F) + n)$ gives $c(6, 10) \geq 2/3$. That remark is therefore not formalised here.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/667.lean
@decl Erdos667.erdos_667.variants.endpoint
-- L4DEL: title: The right endpoint: when $q = \binom{p-1}{2} + 1$ one has $c(p, q) = 1$ (stated as "easy to see" on erdosproblems.com/667)
-- L4DEL: intuition: The right endpoint: when $q = \binom{p-1}{2} + 1$ one has $c(p, q) = 1$ (stated as "easy to see" on erdosproblems.com/667).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/667.lean
@decl Erdos667.erdos_667.variants.monotoneOn
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: monotoneOn
-- L4DEL: intuition: That $c(p, q)$ is *nondecreasing* in $q$ for fixed $p$ is a straightforward consequence of the definitions: enlarging $q$ shrinks the family of $(p, q)$-locally dense graphs, which can only increase the guaranteed clique size $H(n)$, and hence the exponent.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/667.lean
@decl Erdos667.erdos_667.variants.ramsey_bounds
-- L4DEL: title: The case $q = 1$ is the classical Ramsey problem, giving the bounds $\tfrac{1}{p-1} \leq c(p, 1) \leq \tfrac{2}{p+1}$ for $p \geq 3$
-- L4DEL: intuition: The case $q = 1$ is the classical Ramsey problem, giving the bounds $\tfrac{1}{p-1} \leq c(p, 1) \leq \tfrac{2}{p+1}$ for $p \geq 3$. (For $p = 2$ the condition forces $G$ to be complete, so $c(2, 1) = 1$ and the upper bound fails.)
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/667.lean
@decl Erdos667.H
-- L4DEL: title: `H p q n` is `H(n) = H(n; p, q)`: the largest `m` such that *every* `(p, q)`-locally dense graph on `n` vertices must contain a complete graph on `m` vertices `Kₘ`
-- L4DEL: intuition: `H p q n` is `H(n) = H(n; p, q)`: the largest `m` such that *every* `(p, q)`-locally dense graph on `n` vertices must contain a complete graph on `m` vertices `Kₘ`. The condition "`G` contains `Kₘ`" is `¬ G.CliqueFree m`. Any `m`-clique needs `m ≤ n` vertices, and the property is downward closed in `m`, so `Nat.findGreatest _ n` returns the genuine maximum.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/667.lean
@decl Erdos667.LocallyDense
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: LocallyDense
-- L4DEL: intuition: A graph `G` on `n` vertices is *`(p, q)`-locally dense* if every set of `p` of its vertices spans at least `q` edges of `G`; equivalently, every induced subgraph on `p` vertices has at least `q` edges.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/67.lean
@decl Erdos67.erdos_67
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: erdos_67
-- L4DEL: intuition: **The Erdős discrepancy problem** If $f\colon \mathbb N \rightarrow \{-1, +1\}$ then is it true that for every $C>0$ there exist $d, m \ge 1$ such that $$\left\lvert \sum_{1\leq k\leq m}f(kd)\right\rvert > C?$$ This is true, and was proved by Tao [Ta16]
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/67.lean
@decl Erdos67.erdos_67.variants.complex
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: complex
-- L4DEL: intuition: **The Erdős discrepancy problem (complex variant)** If $f\colon \mathbb N \rightarrow S^1 ⊆ ℂ$ then is it true that for every $C>0$ there exist $d, m \ge 1$ such that $$\left\lvert \sum_{1\leq k\leq m}f(kd)\right\rvert > C?$$ This is true, and was proved by Tao [Ta16]
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/670.lean
@decl Erdos670.erdos_670
-- L4DEL: title: Let $A\subseteq \mathbb{R}^d$ be a set of $n$ points such that all pairwise distances differ by at least $1$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $A\subseteq \mathbb{R}^d$ be a set of $n$ points such that all pairwise distances differ by at least $1$. Is the diameter of $A$ at least $(1+o(1))n^2$? The quantifiers in the source are ambiguous. Following the remarks on erdosproblems.com, this is the regime where $d$ is fixed and the $o(1)$ term tends to $0$ as $n \to \infty$, at a rate which may depend on $d$. The case $d = 1$ was proved by Erdős (`Erdos670.erdos_670.variants.dim_one`), so the question is open for $d \geq 2$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/670.lean
@decl Erdos670.erdos_670.variants.choose_two
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: choose_two
-- L4DEL: intuition: The trivial lower bound: if $A\subseteq \mathbb{R}^d$ is a set of $n \neq 2$ points such that all pairwise distances differ by at least $1$, then the diameter of $A$ is at least $\binom{n}{2}$. For $n \geq 3$, take a closest pair $a, b$ and a third point $c$. Then $1 \leq \lvert d(a, c) - d(b, c)\rvert \leq d(a, b)$, so every distance is at least $1$. The $\binom{n}{2}$ distances are at least $1$ apart, so the largest one is at least $\binom{n}{2}$. For $n = 2$ the hypothesis is vacuous, and two points at distance $1/2$ show that the bound fails.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/670.lean
@decl Erdos670.erdos_670.variants.dim_one
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: dim_one
-- L4DEL: intuition: Erdős [Er97f] proved the claim when $d = 1$: if $A\subseteq \mathbb{R}$ is a set of $n$ points such that all pairwise distances differ by at least $1$, then the diameter of $A$ is at least $(1+o(1))n^2$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

