@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/678.lean
@decl Erdos678.erdos_678
-- L4DEL: title: Write $M(n, k)$ be the least common multiple of $\{n+1, \dotsc, n+k\}$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Write $M(n, k)$ be the least common multiple of $\{n+1, \dotsc, n+k\}$. Let $k$ be sufficiently large. Are there infinitely many $m, n$ with $m \geq n + k$ such that $$ M(n, k) > M(m, k + 1) $$? The answer is yes, as proved in a strong form by Cambie [Ca24]. [Ca24] S. Cambie, Resolution of an Erdős' problem on least common multiples. arXiv:2410.09138 (2024). This was formalized in Lean by Alexeev using Aristotle, on top of the PNT+ project. For a fixed $k$ there are only finitely many such pairs, so "infinitely many" is read here as ranging over $k$ as well: for every sufficiently large $k$ at least one pair $(m, n)$ occurs. See `erdos_678.variants.infinitely_many_triples` for the reading in which the infinitude is stated directly, and `erdos_678.variants.not_infinitely_many_pairs` for why it cannot be asked of a single $k$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/678.lean
@decl Erdos678.erdos_678.variants.infinitely_many_triples
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: infinitely_many_triples
-- L4DEL: intuition: The pairs $(m, n)$ with $m \geq n + k$ and $M(n, k) > M(m, k + 1)$ are infinite in number once $k$ is allowed to vary, which is the sense in which Cambie's result answers the question.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/678.lean
@decl Erdos678.erdos_678.variants.not_infinitely_many_pairs
-- L4DEL: title: For a fixed sufficiently large $k$ only finitely many pairs occur: $M(m, k + 1) \geq m + 1$ bounds $m$ by $M(n, k)$, and for large $n$ the inequality reverses
-- L4DEL: intuition: For a fixed sufficiently large $k$ only finitely many pairs occur: $M(m, k + 1) \geq m + 1$ bounds $m$ by $M(n, k)$, and for large $n$ the inequality reverses. So the question cannot be read as asking for infinitely many pairs at a single $k$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

