@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/682.lean
@decl Erdos682.erdos_682
-- L4DEL: title: Is it true that for almost all $n$ there exists some $m\in (p_n,p_{n+1})$ such that $$p(m) \geq p_{n+1}-p_n,$$ where $p(m)$ denotes the least prime factor of $m$?
-- L4DEL: intuition: Is it true that for almost all $n$ there exists some $m\in (p_n,p_{n+1})$ such that $$p(m) \geq p_{n+1}-p_n,$$ where $p(m)$ denotes the least prime factor of $m$? This was solved in the affirmative by Gafni and Tao [GaTa25]. This was formalized in Lean by Codex and GPT-5.6 Sol.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/682.lean
@decl Erdos682.erdos_682.variants.dickson_counterexample
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: dickson_counterexample
-- L4DEL: intuition: Erdős first thought this should be true for all large $n$, but found a (conditional) counterexample: Dickson's conjecture says there are infinitely many $d$ such that $$2183+30030d\textrm{ and }2201+30030d$$ are both prime, and then they must necessarily be consecutive primes. These give a counterexample since $30030=2\cdot 3 \cdot 5\cdot 7\cdot 11\cdot 13$ and every integer in $[2184,2200]$ is divisible by at least one of these primes. This was formalized in Lean by Codex and GPT-5.6 Sol.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

