@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/683.lean
@decl Erdos683.erdos_683
-- L4DEL: title: Let $P(n, k)$ be the largest prime factor of $\binom{n}{k}$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $P(n, k)$ be the largest prime factor of $\binom{n}{k}$. There exists $c > 0$ such that $P(n, k) \ge \min(n - k + 1, k^{1 + c})$ for all $0 < k \le n/2$. Erdős stated this for $1 \le k \le n$ with the bound $\min(n-k+1, k^{1+c})$ [Er79d]. The minimum is needed even for $k \le n/2$: at $n = 2k$ every prime factor of $\binom{2k}{k}$ is at most $2k$, so $P(n, k) \ge k^{1+c}$ fails for large $k$. The range $k \le n/2$ is natural (cf. #961 and the discussion).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/683.lean
@decl Erdos683.erdos_683.variant.erdos_log
-- L4DEL: title: Erdős [Er55d] improved this to $P(n, k) \gg \min(n - k + 1, k \log k)$ for $k \le n/2$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Erdős [Er55d] improved this to $P(n, k) \gg \min(n - k + 1, k \log k)$ for $k \le n/2$. The minimum cannot be dropped: at $n = 2k$ one has $P(n, k) \le 2k$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

