@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/728.lean
@decl Erdos728.erdos_728
-- L4DEL: title: Let $\varepsilon$ be sufficiently small and $C, C' > 0$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $\varepsilon$ be sufficiently small and $C, C' > 0$. Are there integers $a, b, n$ such that $$a, b > \varepsilon n\quad a!\, b! \mid n!\, (a + b - n)!, $$ and $$C \log n < a + b - n < C' \log n ?$$ Note that the website currently displays a simpler (trivial) version of this problem because $a + b$ isn't assumed to be in the $n + O(\log n)$ regime. Barreto and ChatGPT-5.2 have proved that, for any $0 < C_1 < C_2$, there are infinitely many $a, b, n$ with $b = n/2$, $a = n/2 + O(\log n)$, and $C_1 \log n < a + b - n < C_2 \log n$ such that $a! b! \mid n! (a + b - n)!$ This appears to answer the question in the spirit it was intended. This was formalized in Lean by Alexeev using Aristotle.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/729.lean
@decl Erdos729.erdos_729
-- L4DEL: title: Let $C>0$ be a constant
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $C>0$ be a constant. Are there infinitely many integers $a,b,n$ with $a+b> n+C\log n$ such that the denominator of $$\frac{n!}{a!b!}$$contains only primes $\ll_C 1$? Erdős [Er68c] proved that if $a!b!\mid n!$ then $a+b\leq n+O(\log n)$. This has been proved in the affirmative by Barreto and Leeham, using ChatGPT and Aristotle, with a modification of the argument used for [728].
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

