@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/769.lean
@decl Erdos769.erdos_769
-- L4DEL: title: Let $c(n)$ be minimal such that if $k\geq c(n)$ then the $n$-dimensional unit cube can be decomposed into $k$ homothetic $n$-dimensional cubes
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $c(n)$ be minimal such that if $k\geq c(n)$ then the $n$-dimensional unit cube can be decomposed into $k$ homothetic $n$-dimensional cubes. Give good bounds for $c(n)$ — in particular, is it true that $c(n)\gg n^n$? The `c(n) \gg n^n` conjecture is **false**: for odd `n`, one can tile the unit `n`-cube into `k` homothetic cubes for every `k ≥ U(n)` with `U(n)/n^n → 0`, so `c(n)/n^n → 0` along odd dimensions and no absolute constant lower-bounds it. Determining good bounds for `c(n)` in general remains open.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/769.lean
@decl Erdos769.erdos_769.variants.growth_rate
-- L4DEL: title: What refuting $c(n)\gg n^n$ leaves open: give good bounds for $c(n)$
-- L4DEL: intuition: What refuting $c(n)\gg n^n$ leaves open: give good bounds for $c(n)$. Asked here on the scale the problem itself sets, $n^n$: does $c$ have a well-defined order $$\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{\log c(n)}{n\log n}?$$ The refuted conjecture would have forced this limit to be at least $1$. The cutoff condition is restricted to positive dimensions: in dimension zero, exact coverage permits only one tile, so no cutoff exists.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

