@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/772.lean
@decl Erdos772.erdos_772.variants.upper_bound
-- L4DEL: title: Erdős [Er84d] proved that $H_k(n) \ll n^{2/3}$, where the implied constant is absolute
-- L4DEL: intuition: Erdős [Er84d] proved that $H_k(n) \ll n^{2/3}$, where the implied constant is absolute. Since $1_A\ast 1_A$ counts ordered pairs, the bound needs $k\geq 4$: the construction of Erdős has at most two unordered representations of each sum, hence at most four ordered ones, while $k\leq 2$ forces $A$ itself to be Sidon and so $H_k(n)=n$. For $k=3$ the only collisions are $a+b=2c$, so the Sidon subsets of $A$ are its $3$-term-progression-free subsets, which have size $n^{1-o(1)}$, and the bound fails there too.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/772.lean
@decl Erdos772.H
-- L4DEL: title: $H_k(n)$ is the maximal $r$ such that if $A\subset\mathbb{N}$ has $\lvert A\rvert=n$ and $\| 1_A\ast 1_A\|_\infty \leq k$ then $A$ contains a Sidon set of size at least $r$
-- L4DEL: intuition: $H_k(n)$ is the maximal $r$ such that if $A\subset\mathbb{N}$ has $\lvert A\rvert=n$ and $\| 1_A\ast 1_A\|_\infty \leq k$ then $A$ contains a Sidon set of size at least $r$. Here $1_A\ast 1_A(m)$ counts the ordered pairs $(a,b)\in A^2$ with $a+b=m$. (The value is truncated at $n$, which only matters when no such $A$ exists.)
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

