-- L4DEL: intuition: The graph `G` has no clique and no independent set with `m` vertices.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/78.lean
@decl Erdos78.stronglyExplicitGraph
-- L4DEL: title: The graph on `Fin n` described by an adjacency oracle `adj` which receives `n` in binary
-- L4DEL: intuition: The graph on `Fin n` described by an adjacency oracle `adj` which receives `n` in binary. Two distinct vertices `u` and `v` are adjacent if `adj (n, u, v)` or `adj (n, v, u)` is `true`. If `adj` is computable in polynomial time, then each adjacency query is answered in time polynomial in $\log n$. This is the notion of a *strongly explicit* family of graphs, which is what "explicit Ramsey graph" usually means in the literature (e.g. [Co15], [Li23b]). Compare `explicitGraph`, where only time polynomial in $n$ is allowed.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/780.lean
@decl Erdos780.erdos_780.variants.sharp
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: sharp
-- L4DEL: intuition: This would be best possible: if $n=kr-1+(t-1)(k-1)$ then decomposing $[n]$ as one set $X_1$ of size $kr-1$ and $t-1$ sets $X_2,\ldots,X_{t}$ of size $k-1$, a colouring without $k$ pairwise disjoint edges is given colouring all subsets of $X_1$ in colour $1$ and assigning an edge with colour $2\leq i\leq t$ if $i$ is minimal such that $X_i$ intersects the edge.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

