@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/79.lean
@decl Erdos79.erdos_79
-- L4DEL: title: We say $G$ is Ramsey size linear if $R(G,H)\ll m$ for all graphs $H$ with $m$ edges and no isolated vertices
-- L4DEL: intuition: We say $G$ is Ramsey size linear if $R(G,H)\ll m$ for all graphs $H$ with $m$ edges and no isolated vertices. Are there infinitely many graphs $G$ which are not Ramsey size linear but such that all of its proper subgraphs are? Asked by Erdős, Faudree, Rousseau, and Schelp [EFRS93]. $K_4$ was long the only known example. Wigderson [Wi24] proved that there are infinitely many such graphs.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/793.lean
@decl Erdos793.erdos_793
-- L4DEL: title: Let $F(n)$ be the maximum possible size of a subset $A \subseteq \{1, \ldots, n\}$ such that $a \nmid bc$ whenever $a, b, c \in A$ with $a \neq b$ and $a \neq c$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $F(n)$ be the maximum possible size of a subset $A \subseteq \{1, \ldots, n\}$ such that $a \nmid bc$ whenever $a, b, c \in A$ with $a \neq b$ and $a \neq c$. Is there a constant $c$ such that $$F(n) = \pi(n) + (c + o(1)) n^{2/3} (\log n)^{-2}?$$ A problem of Erdős [Er69, Er70b], who proved in [Er38] that $F(n) = \pi(n) + O(n^{2/3} (\log n)^{-2})$. The answer is yes, with $c = 27/2$: this was proved by GPT-5.6 Sol (prompted by Chojecki), refining the argument of [Er38]; see `erdos_793.variants.constant`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

