@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/839.lean
@decl Erdos839.erdos_839.parts.ii
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: ii
-- L4DEL: intuition: Erdős Problem 839 (Part 2, stronger) [Er78f][Er92c]: Let $1 \leq a_1 < a_2 < \cdots$ be a strictly increasing sequence of positive integers such that no $a_i$ is the sum of consecutive $a_j$ for $j < i$. Is it true that $\lim_{x \to \infty} \frac{1}{\log x} \sum_{a_n < x} \frac{1}{a_n} = 0$? This is equivalent to asking whether the range $\{a_1,a_2,\ldots\}$ has logarithmic density zero (see `Set.HasLogDensity`).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/839.lean
@decl Erdos839.SumOfConsecutiveFree
-- L4DEL: title: A sequence $a : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$ is "sum-of-consecutive-free" if no term equals the sum of a contiguous block of earlier terms
-- L4DEL: intuition: A sequence $a : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$ is "sum-of-consecutive-free" if no term equals the sum of a contiguous block of earlier terms. That is, for all $i$, $a_i \neq a_j + a_{j+1} + \cdots + a_k$ for any $j \leq k < i$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/841.lean
@decl Erdos841.erdos_841
-- L4DEL: title: Let $t_n$ be minimal such that $\{n+1,\ldots,n+t_n\}$ contains a subset whose product with $n$ is a square number (and let $t_n=0$ if $n$ is itself square)
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $t_n$ be minimal such that $\{n+1,\ldots,n+t_n\}$ contains a subset whose product with $n$ is a square number (and let $t_n=0$ if $n$ is itself square). Estimate $t_n$. A problem of Erdős, Graham, and Selfridge. For example, $t_6=6$ since $6\cdot 8\cdot 12=24^2$. It is trivial that $t_n\geq P(n)$, where $P(n)$ is the largest prime divisor of $n$. Bui, Pratt, and Zaharescu [BPZ24] proved that the distribution of $t_n$ continues to follow $P(n)$, in that for any fixed $c\in (0,1]$ $$\lim_{x\to \infty}\frac{\lvert \{ n\leq x : t_n\leq n^c\}\rvert}{x} = \lim_{x\to \infty}\frac{\lvert \{ n\leq x : P(n)\leq n^c\}\rvert}{x}.$$ The statement below is that the difference of the two counting functions is $o(x)$. This was formalized in Lean by Codex.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/841.lean
@decl Erdos841.erdos_841.variants.lower_bound
-- L4DEL: title: Bui, Pratt, and Zaharescu [BPZ24] proved that for all non-square $n$ $$t_n \gg (\log\log n)^{6/5}(\log\log\log n)^{-1/5}.$$ This was formalized in Lean by Codex
-- L4DEL: intuition: Bui, Pratt, and Zaharescu [BPZ24] proved that for all non-square $n$ $$t_n \gg (\log\log n)^{6/5}(\log\log\log n)^{-1/5}.$$ This was formalized in Lean by Codex.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

