-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/867.lean
@decl Erdos867.ConsecutiveSumFree
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: ConsecutiveSumFree
-- L4DEL: intuition: A finite set of naturals $A=\{a_1<\cdots<a_t\}$ is *consecutive-sum-free* if it has no solutions to $a_i+a_{i+1}+\cdots+a_j\in A$ with $i<j$; equivalently, whenever an interval $[m,n]$ contains at least two elements of $A$, the sum of the elements of $A$ lying in $[m,n]$ is not itself an element of $A$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/867.lean
@decl Erdos867.erdos_867
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: erdos_867
-- L4DEL: intuition: Is it true that if $A=\{a_1<\cdots <a_t\}\subseteq \{1,\ldots,N\}$ has no solutions to $$a_i+a_{i+1}+\cdots+a_j\in A$$ then $$\lvert A\rvert \leq \frac{N}{2}+O(1)?$$ In fact this problem is false. Freud [Fr93] constructed a sequence with density $\geq 19/36$. The current best bounds are due to Coppersmith and Phillips [CoPh96], who prove that the maximal size of such an $A$ satisfies $$\frac{13}{24}N -O(1)\leq \lvert A\rvert \leq \left(\frac{2}{3}-\frac{1}{512}\right)N+\log N.$$
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/867.lean
