@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/868.lean
@decl Erdos868.erdos_868.parts.i
-- L4DEL: title: Let $A$ be an additive basis of order $2$, let $f(n)$ denote the number of ways in which $n$ can be written as the sum of two elements from $A$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $A$ be an additive basis of order $2$, let $f(n)$ denote the number of ways in which $n$ can be written as the sum of two elements from $A$. If $f(n) \to \infty$ as $n \to \infty$, then must $A$ contain a minimal additive basis of order $2$? Larsen and Larsen [LaLa26] answered this in the negative.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/868.lean
@decl Erdos868.erdos_868.parts.ii
-- L4DEL: title: Let $A$ be an additive basis of order $2$, let $f(n)$ denote the number of ways in which $n$ can be written as the sum of two elements from $A$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $A$ be an additive basis of order $2$, let $f(n)$ denote the number of ways in which $n$ can be written as the sum of two elements from $A$. If $f(n) > \epsilon \log n$ for large $n$ and an arbitrary fixed $\epsilon > 0$, then must $A$ contain a minimal additive basis of order $2$? Larsen and Larsen [LaLa26] constructed a counterexample with $f(n) > c \log n$ for all large $n$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

