@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/883.lean
@decl Erdos883.erdos_883.parts.i
-- L4DEL: title: For $A\subseteq \{1,\ldots,n\}$ let $G(A)$ be the graph with vertex set $A$, where two integers are joined by an edge if they are coprime
-- L4DEL: intuition: For $A\subseteq \{1,\ldots,n\}$ let $G(A)$ be the graph with vertex set $A$, where two integers are joined by an edge if they are coprime. Is it true that if $$|A| > \lfloor n/2 \rfloor + \lfloor n/3 \rfloor - \lfloor n/6 \rfloor$$ then $G(A)$ contains all odd cycles of length $\leq n/3 + 1$? A problem of Erdős and Sárközy [ErSa97].
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/883.lean
@decl Erdos883.erdos_883.parts.ii
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: ii
-- L4DEL: intuition: Is it true that, for every $\ell\geq 1$, if $n$ is sufficiently large and $$|A| > \lfloor n/2\rfloor + \lfloor n/3\rfloor - \lfloor n/6\rfloor$$ then $G(A)$ must contain a complete $(1,\ell,\ell)$ tripartite graph on $2\ell+1$ vertices? The second question was solved by Sárközy [Sa99], who proved this with $\ell \gg \log n/\log\log n$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/884.lean
@decl Erdos884.Erdos884Prop
-- L4DEL: title: For a natural number n, let $1 = d_1 < \dotsc < d_{\tau(n)} = n$ denote the divisors of $n$ in increasing order
-- L4DEL: intuition: For a natural number n, let $1 = d_1 < \dotsc < d_{\tau(n)} = n$ denote the divisors of $n$ in increasing order. Does it hold that $\sum_{1 \le i < j \le \tau(n)} \frac{1}{d_j - d_i} \ll 1 + \sum_{1 \le i < \tau(n)} \frac{1}{d_{i + 1} - d_i}$ for $n \to \infty$, i.e. $\sum_{1 \le i < j \le \tau(n)} \frac{1}{d_j - d_i} \in O \left( 1 + \sum_{1 \le i < \tau(n)} \frac{1}{d_{i + 1} - d_i} \right)$? This conjecture has been **disproved**: - In September 2025, Terence Tao gave a conditional _negative_ answer assuming the prime tuples conjecture, see `erdos_884_false_of_hardy_littlewood` for this implication. - Daniel Larsen subsequently gave an unconditional disproof.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/884.lean
@decl Erdos884.erdos_884
-- L4DEL: title: For a natural number n, let $1 = d_1 < \dotsc < d_{\tau(n)} = n$ denote the divisors of $n$ in increasing order
-- L4DEL: intuition: For a natural number n, let $1 = d_1 < \dotsc < d_{\tau(n)} = n$ denote the divisors of $n$ in increasing order. Does it hold that $\sum_{1 \le i < j \le \tau(n)} \frac{1}{d_j - d_i} \ll 1 + \sum_{1 \le i < \tau(n)} \frac{1}{d_{i + 1} - d_i}$ for $n \to \infty$, i.e. $\sum_{1 \le i < j \le \tau(n)} \frac{1}{d_j - d_i} \in O \left( 1 + \sum_{1 \le i < \tau(n)} \frac{1}{d_{i + 1} - d_i} \right)$? This conjecture has been **disproved**: - In September 2025, Terence Tao gave a conditional _negative_ answer assuming the prime tuples conjecture, see `erdos_884_false_of_hardy_littlewood` for this implication. - Daniel Larsen subsequently gave an unconditional disproof. *Reference:* erdosproblems.com/884
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/884.lean
@decl Erdos884.erdos_884_false_of_hardy_littlewood
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: erdos_884_false_of_hardy_littlewood
-- L4DEL: intuition: In September 2025, Terence Tao gave a conditional _negative_ answer to Erdős problem 884, disproving it under the assumption of the *qualitative Hardy–Littlewood prime tuples conjecture*, see Conjecture 1.1 and Theorem 1.1 of Tao25. The qualitative conjecture states that for every admissible tuple $(h_1, \dotsc, h_k)$ of integers, i.e. one which avoids at least one residue class modulo $p$ for every prime $p$, there are infinitely many natural numbers $n$ such that $n + h_1, \dotsc, n + h_k$ are all prime. Unlike the full Hardy–Littlewood conjecture, it does not give an asymptotic for the number of such $n$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

