-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: a
-- L4DEL: intuition: If $\omega_k(n)$ counts the number of distinct prime factors of $n$ which are $>k$, then is it true that, for every $k\geq 1$, $$\liminf_{n\to \infty}\sum_{0\leq i < k}\omega_k(n+i)\leq k?$$
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/890.lean
@decl Erdos890.erdos_890.parts.b
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: b
-- L4DEL: intuition: Is it true that $$\limsup_{n\to \infty}\left(\sum_{0\leq i < k}\omega(n+i)\right) \frac{\log\log n}{\log n}=1,$$ where $\omega$ counts the number of distinct prime factors without restriction?
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/890.lean
@decl Erdos890.erdos_890.variants.liminf_lower_bound
-- L4DEL: title: A question of Erdős and Selfridge [ErSe67], who observe that $\liminf_{n\to \infty}\sum_{0\leq i < k}\omega(n+i)\geq k+\pi(k)-1$ for every $k$
-- L4DEL: intuition: A question of Erdős and Selfridge [ErSe67], who observe that $\liminf_{n\to \infty}\sum_{0\leq i < k}\omega(n+i)\geq k+\pi(k)-1$ for every $k$. This follows from Pólya's theorem that the set of $k$-smooth integers has unbounded gaps - indeed, $n(n+1)\cdots (n+k-1)$ is divisible by all primes $\leq k$ and, provided $n$ is large, all but at most one of $n,n+1,\ldots,n+k-1$ has a prime factor $>k$ by Pólya's theorem.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/890.lean
@decl Erdos890.erdos_890.variants.omega_limsup
-- L4DEL: title: It is a classical fact that $\limsup_{n\to \infty}\omega(n)\frac{\log\log n}{\log n}=1.$
-- L4DEL: intuition: It is a classical fact that $\limsup_{n\to \infty}\omega(n)\frac{\log\log n}{\log n}=1.$
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

