@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/891.lean
@decl Erdos891.erdos_891.variants.schinzel
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: schinzel
-- L4DEL: intuition: Schinzel deduced from Pólya's theorem [Po18] (that the sequence of $k$-smooth integers has unbounded gaps) that this is true with $p_1\cdots p_k$ replaced by $p_1\cdots p_{k-1}p_{k+1}$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/891.lean
@decl Erdos891.erdos_891.variants.weisenberg
-- L4DEL: title: Weisenberg has observed that Dickson's conjecture implies the answer is no if we replace $p_1\cdots p_k$ with $p_1\cdots p_k-1$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Weisenberg has observed that Dickson's conjecture implies the answer is no if we replace $p_1\cdots p_k$ with $p_1\cdots p_k-1$. Indeed, let $L_k$ be the lowest common multiple of all integers at most $p_1\cdots p_k$. By Dickson's conjecture [Wikipedia], there are infinitely many $n'$ such that $\frac{L_k}{m}n'+1$ is prime for all $1\leq m < p_1\cdots p_k$. It follows that, if $n=L_kn'+1$, then all integers in $[n,n+p_1\cdots p_k-1)$ have at most $k$ prime factors. The statement is conditional on Dickson's conjecture, formalised as `Dickson.dickson_conjecture`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/893.lean
