@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/895.lean
@decl Erdos895.erdos_895.variants.hindman
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: hindman
-- L4DEL: intuition: Hajnal thought that there is in fact an independent set which is a Hindman set - that is, an independent set of the shape $$\left\{ \sum_{i\in S}a_i : S\subseteq \{1,\ldots,k\}\right\}$$ for some $a_1,\ldots,a_k$ (provided $n$ is sufficiently large depending on $k$). The general question of an independent Hindman set remains open. Here the $a_i$ are taken to be distinct positive integers and $S$ ranges over the nonempty subsets of $\{1,\ldots,k\}$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/897.lean
@decl Erdos897.erdos_897.variants.parts.i
-- L4DEL: title: Let $f(n)$ be an additive function (so that $f(ab)=f(a)+f(b)$ if $(a,b)=1$) such that $\limsup_{p,k} f(p^k) / \log(p^k) = ∞$ and $f(p^k) = f(p)$ or $f(p^k) = kf(p)$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $f(n)$ be an additive function (so that $f(ab)=f(a)+f(b)$ if $(a,b)=1$) such that $\limsup_{p,k} f(p^k) / \log(p^k) = ∞$ and $f(p^k) = f(p)$ or $f(p^k) = kf(p)$. Is it true that $\limsup_n (f(n+1)−f(n))/ \log n = ∞$? The known counterexample does not satisfy either of these extra hypotheses, so this variant remains open.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/897.lean
