-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $f(n)$ be an additive function (so that $f(ab)=f(a)+f(b)$ if $(a,b)=1$) such that $\limsup_{p,k} f(p^k) / \log(p^k) = ∞$ and $f(p^k) = f(p)$ or $f(p^k) = kf(p)$. Is it true that $\limsup_n f(n+1)/f(n) = ∞$? The known counterexample does not satisfy either of these extra hypotheses, so this variant remains open.
