@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/90.lean
@decl Erdos90.erdos_90
-- L4DEL: title: Does every set of $n$ distinct points in $\mathbb{R}^2$ contain at most $n^{1+O(\frac{1}{\log\log n})}$ many pairs which are distance $1$ apart?
-- L4DEL: intuition: Does every set of $n$ distinct points in $\mathbb{R}^2$ contain at most $n^{1+O(\frac{1}{\log\log n})}$ many pairs which are distance $1$ apart? This was disproved by an internal model at OpenAI, which constructed (for infinitely many $n$) a set $P$ of $n$ points in $\mathbb{R}^2$ such that the number of unit distance pairs in $P$ is at least $n^{1+c}$, where $c > 0$ is an absolute constant.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/90.lean
@decl Erdos90.erdos_90.variants.polynomial_lower_bound_implies_erdos_90
