@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/939.lean
@decl Erdos939.erdos_939.variants.eight
-- L4DEL: title: Cambie has also found solutions when $r=8$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Cambie has also found solutions when $r=8$. The source adds that the $r=8$ solution works "even with the sum of $5$ $8$-powerful numbers". That is a stronger result than this statement, which asks for the $r - 2 = 6$ summands of `Erdos939Sums`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/939.lean
@decl Erdos939.erdos_939.variants.euler
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: euler
-- L4DEL: intuition: Euler had conjectured that the sum of $k - 1$ many $k$-th powers is never a $k$-th power, but this is false for $k=5$, as Lander and Parkin [LaPa67] found $$27^5+84^5+110^5+133^5=144^5$$. The summands must be positive. Without that condition a set containing `0` would count, so the negation would be satisfied by a sum of fewer than $k-1$ powers and would claim less than the refutation of Euler's conjecture that this theorem records.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/939.lean
@decl Erdos939.erdos_939.variants.examples
-- L4DEL: title: Cambie has found several examples of the sum of $r - 2$ coprime $r$-powerful numbers being itself $r$-powerful
-- L4DEL: intuition: Cambie has found several examples of the sum of $r - 2$ coprime $r$-powerful numbers being itself $r$-powerful. For example when $r=5$ we have $$3^7\cdot 61^5 = 2^8\cdot3^{10}\cdot 5^7 + 2^{12}\cdot 23^6 + 11^5\cdot 13^5$$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/939.lean
@decl Erdos939.erdos_939.variants.finite
-- L4DEL: title: If $r≥4$, are there at most finitely many sums of $r-2$ coprime $r$-powerful numbers that are themselves $r$-powerful?
-- L4DEL: intuition: If $r≥4$, are there at most finitely many sums of $r-2$ coprime $r$-powerful numbers that are themselves $r$-powerful? The answer is no: for every $r \ge 6$ there are infinitely many such sums, see `erdos_939.variants.infinite_of_six_le`. (For $r = 4$ and $r = 5$ the question is open; for $r = 4$ no example is known at all, see `erdos_939`.) A construction in the site's comments, from GPT-5.5 Pro prompted by Price, gives infinitely many for every $r \ge 6$. This statement quantifies over every $r \ge 4$, so it stays open at $r = 4$ and $r = 5$. The category is unchanged because the construction is recorded in the comments and not in the literature.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/939.lean
@decl Erdos939.erdos_939.variants.infinite_of_six_le
-- L4DEL: title: For every $r \ge 6$ there are infinitely many sums of $r - 2$ coprime $r$-powerful numbers that are themselves $r$-powerful
-- L4DEL: intuition: For every $r \ge 6$ there are infinitely many sums of $r - 2$ coprime $r$-powerful numbers that are themselves $r$-powerful. A construction, found by GPT-5.5 Pro prompted by Liam Price and recorded in the comments on erdosproblems.com/939, expands $(X+Y)^r = (X-Y)^r + \sum_{j \text{ odd}} 2\binom{r}{j} X^{r-j} Y^j$, splits the $j = 3$ term into $\lfloor r/2 \rfloor - 2$ distinct pieces to obtain exactly $r - 2$ summands, and takes $X = q^r$, $Y = B^r$ with $B$ divisible by all primes in the coefficients and $q > B$ a prime not dividing $B$; varying $q$ gives infinitely many solutions.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

