@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/939.lean
@decl Erdos939.erdos_939.variants.triples
-- L4DEL: title: Are there infinitely many triples of coprime $3$-powerful numbers $a, b, c$ such that $a + b = c$?
-- L4DEL: intuition: Are there infinitely many triples of coprime $3$-powerful numbers $a, b, c$ such that $a + b = c$? The answer is yes. Nitaj [Ni95] proved it, with $2^3\cdot 3^5\cdot 73^3 + 271^3 = 919^3$ as an example. In Nitaj's construction at least two of $a, b, c$ are perfect cubes. Cohn [Co98] constructed infinitely many triples of which none is a perfect cube, and Walsh [Wa24] gave a further construction.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/94.lean
@decl Erdos94.erdos_94
-- L4DEL: title: Suppose $n$ points in $\mathbb{R}^2$ determine a convex polygon and the set of distances between them is $\{u_1,\ldots,u_t\}$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Suppose $n$ points in $\mathbb{R}^2$ determine a convex polygon and the set of distances between them is $\{u_1,\ldots,u_t\}$. Suppose $u_i$ appears as the distance between $f(u_i)$ many pairs of points. Then $$\sum_i f(u_i)^2 \ll n^3.$$ In [Er97c] Erdős claims that Fishburn solved this, but gives no reference.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

