@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/955.lean
@decl Erdos955.erdos_955.variants.empty_preimage
-- L4DEL: title: Erdős [Er73b] proved that there are sets $A$ of positive density such that $s^{-1}(A)$ is empty
-- L4DEL: intuition: Erdős [Er73b] proved that there are sets $A$ of positive density such that $s^{-1}(A)$ is empty. Here "positive density" means positive lower density: Erdős proved that the set of untouchable numbers (those not of the form $s(n)$) has positive lower density [Er73b, Satz I]. Pollack and Pomerance [PoPo16] conjecture that this set has a natural density and give a heuristic for its value. The same result is stated as `erdos_418.variants.sigma` in Erdős Problem 418.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/955.lean
@decl Erdos955.erdos_955.variants.pollack_pomerance_thompson_bound
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: pollack_pomerance_thompson_bound
-- L4DEL: intuition: Pollack, Pomerance, and Thompson [PPT18] prove that if $\epsilon(x)=o(1)$ and $A\subset \mathbb{N}$ has size at most $x^{1/2+\epsilon(x)}$ then $\#\{ n\leq x: s(n)\in A\} =o(x)$ as $x\to \infty$. It follows that (using $s(n)\ll n\log\log n$) if $A$ grows like $\lvert A\cap [1,x]\rvert\leq x^{1/2+o(1)}$ then $s^{-1}(A)$ has density $0$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

