@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/959.lean
@decl Erdos959.erdos_959
-- L4DEL: title: Let $A\subseteq \mathbb{R}^2$ be a set of size $n$ and let $\{d_1,\ldots,d_k\}$ be the set of distinct distances determined by $A$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $A\subseteq \mathbb{R}^2$ be a set of size $n$ and let $\{d_1,\ldots,d_k\}$ be the set of distinct distances determined by $A$. Let $f(d)$ be the number of times the distance $d$ is determined, ordered so that $f(d_1)\geq f(d_2)\geq \cdots \geq f(d_k)$. Estimate $$\max (f(d_1)-f(d_2)),$$ where the maximum is taken over all $A$ of size $n$ (this is `extremalGap n`).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/959.lean
@decl Erdos959.erdos_959.lower_bound
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: lower_bound
-- L4DEL: intuition: A superlinear lower bound: there is a $c>0$ with $$\text{extremalGap}(n)\geq n^{1 + c/\log\log n}$$ for all large $n$, so $\max (f(d_1)-f(d_2))$ grows faster than any linear function of $n$. Determining the exact order remains open, which is `erdos_959`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/959.lean
