@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/968.lean
@decl Erdos968.erdos_968.variants.infinite_decreasingTriples
-- L4DEL: title: Erdős asked whether there are infinitely many solutions to `uₙ > uₙ₊₁ > uₙ₊₂`
-- L4DEL: intuition: Erdős asked whether there are infinitely many solutions to `uₙ > uₙ₊₁ > uₙ₊₂`. The answer is yes. Since `uₙ > uₙ₊₁` is equivalent to `pₙ₊₁ - pₙ < pₙ / n`, it suffices to have infinitely many `n` with `pₙ₊₂ - pₙ` bounded, because `pₙ / n → ∞`. Maynard [Ma15] proved that `liminf (pₙ₊₂ - pₙ) < ∞`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/968.lean
@decl Erdos968.erdos_968.variants.infinite_increasingTriples
-- L4DEL: title: Erdős asked whether there are infinitely many solutions to `uₙ < uₙ₊₁ < uₙ₊₂`
-- L4DEL: intuition: Erdős asked whether there are infinitely many solutions to `uₙ < uₙ₊₁ < uₙ₊₂`. The answer is yes. Since `uₙ < uₙ₊₁` is equivalent to `pₙ₊₁ - pₙ > pₙ / n`, it suffices to have infinitely many `n` with two consecutive prime gaps larger than `pₙ / n ∼ log n`. Ford, Maynard, and Tao [FMT18] proved that for every fixed `k` there are infinitely many `n` with `k` consecutive prime gaps all of size `≫ log pₙ · log log pₙ · log log log log pₙ / log log log pₙ`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/968.lean
@decl Erdos968.erdos_968.variants.sum_abs_diff_isTheta_log_sq
-- L4DEL: title: Erdős and Prachar proved `∑_{pₙ < x} |u (n+1) - u n| ≍ (log x)^2` (see [ErPr61])
-- L4DEL: intuition: Erdős and Prachar proved `∑_{pₙ < x} |u (n+1) - u n| ≍ (log x)^2` (see [ErPr61]). We encode `∑_{pₙ < x}` as a sum over `n < Nat.primeCounting' x` (the number of primes `< x`).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/968.lean
@decl Erdos968.u
-- L4DEL: title: `u n` is the normalized `n`th prime, defined as `pₙ / (n+1)` where `pₙ` is the `n`th prime (with `0.nth Nat.Prime = 2`)
-- L4DEL: intuition: `u n` is the normalized `n`th prime, defined as `pₙ / (n+1)` where `pₙ` is the `n`th prime (with `0.nth Nat.Prime = 2`). This corresponds to the classical sequence `(p₁/1, p₂/2, p₃/3, ...)` while using `Nat.nth Prime`'s `0`-based indexing; in particular, the denominator is always positive.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

