-- L4DEL: intuition: Call $x_1,x_2,\ldots \in (0,1)$ well-distributed if, for every $\epsilon>0$, if $k$ is sufficiently large then, for all $n>0$ and intervals $I\subseteq [0,1]$, $\lvert \# \{ n < m\leq n+k : x_m\in I\} - \lvert I\rvert k\rvert < \epsilon k.$ The notion of a well-distributed sequence was introduced by Hlawka and Petersen [Hl55].
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/999.lean
@decl Erdos999.erdos_999
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: erdos_999
-- L4DEL: intuition: For any function $f:\mathbb{N}\to \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}$ the property that, for almost all $\alpha$ $$\left\lvert \alpha-\frac{p}{q}\right\rvert < \frac{f(q)}{q}$$ has infinitely many solutions with $(p,q)=1$, is equivalent to $$\sum_{q\geq 1}\phi(q)\frac{f(q)}{q}=\infty.$$ The Duffin–Schaeffer conjecture. It is easy to prove that the latter follows from the former. Erdős proved this in the special case when $f(q)q$ is bounded. The full conjecture was proved by Koukoulopoulos and Maynard [KoMa20].
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

