@file FormalConjectures/Mathoverflow/10799.lean
@decl Mathoverflow10799.discrete_isoperimetric_inequality
-- L4DEL: title: Now a famous isoperimetric relation asserts that (IR) $I^p(F) \ge \frac{1}{p} \mu_p(F) \log_p \mu_p(F)$ This relation holds for monotone increasing families $F$ and $0 < p < 1$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Now a famous isoperimetric relation asserts that (IR) $I^p(F) \ge \frac{1}{p} \mu_p(F) \log_p \mu_p(F)$ This relation holds for monotone increasing families $F$ and $0 < p < 1$. It is especially famous and simple when $p=1/2$ and $\mu_p(F)=1/2$. In this case, it says that given a set of half the vertices of the discrete cube $2^X$, the number of edges between $F$ and its complement is at least $2^{n-1}$. *Note on translation:* We use `Real.logb p m` to represent the logarithm base $p$ directly. The factor of $p$ in the denominator (equivalent to $1/p$ in front) is consistent with the definition of `IsOptimal` used in the counterexample proof.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Mathoverflow/10799.lean
@decl Mathoverflow10799.edgeBoundary
-- L4DEL: title: The edge-boundary of $F$ is the expectation of $h(S)$ (according to $\mu_p$) over all subsets $S$ of $X$
-- L4DEL: intuition: The edge-boundary of $F$ is the expectation of $h(S)$ (according to $\mu_p$) over all subsets $S$ of $X$. It is denoted by $I^p(F)$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Mathoverflow/10799.lean
