@file FormalConjectures/Mathoverflow/10799.lean
@decl Mathoverflow10799.IsMonotoneIncreasing
-- L4DEL: title: A family $F$ of subsets of $2^X$ is monotone increasing if when $S$ belongs to $F$ and $T$ contains $S$ then $T$ also belongs to $F$
-- L4DEL: intuition: A family $F$ of subsets of $2^X$ is monotone increasing if when $S$ belongs to $F$ and $T$ contains $S$ then $T$ also belongs to $F$. (Monotone increasing families also also called "filtes" and "up-families".) From now on we will restrict our attention to the case of monotone increasing families. This is Mathlib's `IsUpperSet` applied to the coercion of $\mathcal F$ to a set, using the fact that `≤` on `Finset` is `⊆`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Mathoverflow/10799.lean
@decl Mathoverflow10799.IsOptimal
-- L4DEL: title: We say that a family is optimal for $\mu_p$ if the isoperimetric inequality (IR) is sharp up to a multiplicative constant $1000 \log (1/p)$
-- L4DEL: intuition: We say that a family is optimal for $\mu_p$ if the isoperimetric inequality (IR) is sharp up to a multiplicative constant $1000 \log (1/p)$. Since the lower bound from (IR) is $$\frac{\mu_p(\mathcal F) \cdot \log(1/\mu_p(\mathcal F))}{p \cdot \log(1/p)},$$ multiplying by $1000 \log(1/p)$ gives the condition $$I^p(\mathcal F) \le \frac{1000}{p} \cdot \mu_p(\mathcal F) \cdot \log \frac{1}{\mu_p(\mathcal F)}.$$
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Mathoverflow/10799.lean
@decl Mathoverflow10799.isOptimal_iff_isOptimalWith
-- L4DEL: title: `IsOptimal` is `IsOptimalWith` for the constant $C = 1000$
-- L4DEL: intuition: `IsOptimal` is `IsOptimalWith` for the constant $C = 1000$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Mathoverflow/10799.lean
@decl Mathoverflow10799.IsOptimalWith
-- L4DEL: title: Following Kahn–Kalai, a family is $(C \log(1/p), p)$-optimal if the isoperimetric inequality (IR) is sharp up to the multiplicative constant $C \log(1/p)$, i.e
-- L4DEL: intuition: Following Kahn–Kalai, a family is $(C \log(1/p), p)$-optimal if the isoperimetric inequality (IR) is sharp up to the multiplicative constant $C \log(1/p)$, i.e. $$I^p(\mathcal F) \le \frac{C}{p} \cdot \mu_p(\mathcal F) \cdot \log \frac{1}{\mu_p(\mathcal F)}.$$ `IsOptimal` is the special case $C = 1000$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Mathoverflow/10799.lean
@decl Mathoverflow10799.mathoverflow_10799.variants.weak_kahn_kalai
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: weak_kahn_kalai
-- L4DEL: intuition: Weaker version proven by Kahn–Kalai (2006): for every fixed $\varepsilon > 0$ there is a constant $C = C_\varepsilon$ such that for every monotone increasing family $F$ with critical probability $t$, i.e. $\mu_t(F) = 1/2$, there is some $p \in [n^{-\varepsilon} t, t]$ for which $F$ is $(C \log(1/p), p)$-optimal.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Mathoverflow/10799.lean
@decl Mathoverflow10799.μ
-- L4DEL: title: Start with a set $X=\{1,2,...,n\}$ of $n$ elements and the family $2^X$ of all subsets of $X$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Start with a set $X=\{1,2,...,n\}$ of $n$ elements and the family $2^X$ of all subsets of $X$. For a real number $p$ between zero and one, we consider a probability distribution $\mu_p$ on $2^X$ where the probability that $i \in S$ is $p$, independently for different $i$'s. Thus for $p=1/2$ we get the uniform probability distribution. For $S \subseteq \{0, \ldots, n-1\}$, its probability is $p^{|S|} (1-p)^{n - |S|}$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Mathoverflow/10799.lean
