@file FormalConjectures/OEIS/105020.lean
@decl OeisA105020.antidiagonalIndex
-- L4DEL: title: The index $c$ of the antidiagonal containing $n$, i.e., the largest $c$ such that $T_c \le n$
-- L4DEL: intuition: The index $c$ of the antidiagonal containing $n$, i.e., the largest $c$ such that $T_c \le n$. This is derived from $c = \lfloor (\sqrt{8n+1} - 1) / 2 \rfloor$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/OEIS/105020.lean
@decl OeisA105020.conjecture
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: conjecture
-- L4DEL: intuition: A "Goldbach Conjecture" for this sequence: when there are $n$ terms between consecutive odd integers $2n+1$ and $2n+3$ for $n > 0$, at least one will be the product of 2 primes (not necessarily distinct). Example: $n=3$ for consecutive odd integers $a(7) = 7$ and $a(11) = 9$ and of the 3 sequence entries $a(8) = 12$, $a(9) = 15$ and $a(10) = 16$ between them, one is the product of 2 primes $a(9) = 15=3*5$. - _Michael Hiebl_, Jul 15 2007
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

