@file FormalConjectures/OEIS/105751.lean
@decl OeisA105751.prime_divides_some_term
-- L4DEL: title: Numerical calculation suggests that a similar division holds in this case
-- L4DEL: intuition: Numerical calculation suggests that a similar division holds in this case. Type 1: primes p that do not divide any element of the sequence {$a(n)$}. In this case, unlike in A105750, the set of type 1 primes is empty; that is, every prime p divides some term of this sequence. Note: The triangular number n*(n+1)/2 divides $a(n)$ (see A164652). In particular, if $p$ is an odd prime then $p$ divides $a(p)$. For $p=2$, $2$ divides $a(4)=-40$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/OEIS/105801.lean
@decl OeisA105801.a
-- L4DEL: title: The primary defining sequence `a`
-- L4DEL: intuition: The primary defining sequence `a`. $a(n)$ is the Fibonacci-Collatz sequence: $a(1)=1, a(2)=2$; for $n > 2$, let $\mathrm{fib} = a(n-1) + a(n-2)$; if $\mathrm{fib}$ is odd then $a(n) = 3 \cdot \mathrm{fib} + 1$ else $a(n) = \mathrm{fib}/2$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

