@file FormalConjectures/OEIS/167918.lean
@decl OeisA167918.conjecture1
-- L4DEL: title: Conjecture: $f(n, k) = 2$ for infinitely many cases, where $k = a(n)$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Conjecture: $f(n, k) = 2$ for infinitely many cases, where $k = a(n)$. No hypothesis on the existence of $a(n)$ is needed: if no suitable $k > n$ exists then $a(n) = 0$ and $S(0) = 4 < 2 S(n)$, so such $n$ cannot be witnesses.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/OEIS/167918.lean
@decl OeisA167918.conjecture2
-- L4DEL: title: Open problem: is the ratio $f(n, k)$ bounded, where $k = a(n)$?
-- L4DEL: intuition: Open problem: is the ratio $f(n, k)$ bounded, where $k = a(n)$? No hypothesis on the existence of $a(n)$ is needed: if no suitable $k > n$ exists then $a(n) = 0$ and $S(0) / S(n) = 4 / S(n) = 0$ for $n > 0$, so such $n$ do not affect boundedness. When $a(n) \neq 0$ we have $S(n) \mid S(a(n))$ by definition, so the division is exact.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/OEIS/167918.lean
@decl OeisA167918.conjecture_infinite
-- L4DEL: title: Conjecture: the sequence is infinite, that is, for every $n \geq 1$ there is some $k > n$ with $S(n) \mid S(k)$, so that $a(n)$ is defined
-- L4DEL: intuition: Conjecture: the sequence is infinite, that is, for every $n \geq 1$ there is some $k > n$ with $S(n) \mid S(k)$, so that $a(n)$ is defined.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

