@decl OeisA2326.conjecture1
-- L4DEL: title: If $p$ is an odd prime then $a((p^3-1)/2) = p \cdot a((p^2-1)/2)$
-- L4DEL: intuition: If $p$ is an odd prime then $a((p^3-1)/2) = p \cdot a((p^2-1)/2)$. Because otherwise $a((p^3-1)/2) < p \cdot a((p^2-1)/2)$ iff $a((p^3-1)/2) = a((p-1)/2)$ for a prime $p$. Equivalently $p^3$ divides $2^{p-1}-1$, but no such prime $p$ is known. - Thomas Ordowski, Feb 10 2014
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/OEIS/2326.lean
@decl OeisA2326.conjecture2
-- L4DEL: title: A generalization of the previous conjecture: For each $k \ge 2$, if $p$ is an odd prime then $a((p^{k+1}-1)/2) = p \cdot a((p^k-1)/2)$
-- L4DEL: intuition: A generalization of the previous conjecture: For each $k \ge 2$, if $p$ is an odd prime then $a((p^{k+1}-1)/2) = p \cdot a((p^k-1)/2)$. Computer testing of this generalized conjecture shows that there is no counterexample for $k$ and $p$ both up to 1000. - Ahmad J. Masad, Oct 17 2020 The two are in fact equivalent. Its $k = 2$ instance is `conjecture1`. Conversely, write $d = \operatorname{ord}_{p^2}(2)$. `conjecture1` for $p$ says $v_p(2^d - 1) = 2$, and lifting the exponent for the odd prime $p$ then gives $v_p(2^{d p^t} - 1) = 2 + t$, hence $\operatorname{ord}_{p^{2+t}}(2) = d p^t$ for every $t \ge 0$, which is every instance for that $p$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/OEIS/237271.lean
@decl OeisA237271.a
-- L4DEL: title: Number of parts in the symmetric representation of $\sigma(n)$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Number of parts in the symmetric representation of $\sigma(n)$. $a(n)$ is $1$ plus the number of pairs $(d_k, d_{k+1})$ of consecutive divisors of $n$ such that $d_{k+1}$ is odd and $d_{k+1} \ge 2 d_k$. The formula used is $1 + |\{(d_k, d_{k+1}) \in \text{consecutive pairs of divisors of } n \mid d_{k+1} \text{ is odd and } d_{k+1} \ge 2 d_k\}|$, which is a known characterization of the sequence.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

