@decl OeisA38098.conjecture1
-- L4DEL: title: Conjecture (i): for any integer $k > 2$, the sequence $\pi(n^k)/n^k$ ($n = 2, 3, \ldots$) is strictly decreasing, where $\pi(x)$ denotes the number of primes not exceeding $x$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Conjecture (i): for any integer $k > 2$, the sequence $\pi(n^k)/n^k$ ($n = 2, 3, \ldots$) is strictly decreasing, where $\pi(x)$ denotes the number of primes not exceeding $x$. - Zhi-Wei Sun, Oct 17 2015
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/OEIS/38098.lean
@decl OeisA38098.conjecture2
-- L4DEL: title: Conjecture (ii): all the numbers $\pi(n^2)/n^2$ ($n = 1, 2, 3, \ldots$) are pairwise distinct
-- L4DEL: intuition: Conjecture (ii): all the numbers $\pi(n^2)/n^2$ ($n = 1, 2, 3, \ldots$) are pairwise distinct. Moreover, we have $\pi(n^2)/n^2 > \pi((n+1)^2)/(n+1)^2$ for all $n > 15646$. - Zhi-Wei Sun, Oct 17 2015
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

