@file FormalConjectures/OEIS/382590.lean
@decl OeisA382590.a
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: a
-- L4DEL: intuition: The sequence defined by the mutual recurrence relations: $a(n) = a(n-1)b(n-2) + a(n-2)b(n-1)$ and $b(n) = a(n-1)b(n-2) - a(n-2)b(n-1)$ starting with $a(0) = b(0) = b(1) = 1$ and $a(1) = 2$. The terms are in $\mathbb{Z}$ due to negative values.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/OEIS/382590.lean
@decl OeisA382590.abPair
-- L4DEL: title: Helper function for a, computing the pair $(a(n), b(n))$ such that: $a(n) = a(n-1)b(n-2) + a(n-2)b(n-1)$ $b(n) = a(n-1)b(n-2) - a(n-2)b(n-1)$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Helper function for a, computing the pair $(a(n), b(n))$ such that: $a(n) = a(n-1)b(n-2) + a(n-2)b(n-1)$ $b(n) = a(n-1)b(n-2) - a(n-2)b(n-1)$
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/OEIS/382590.lean
@decl OeisA382590.kthPrimeFactor
-- L4DEL: title: The $k$-th smallest distinct prime factor of an integer $n$ (where $k \ge 1$)
-- L4DEL: intuition: The $k$-th smallest distinct prime factor of an integer $n$ (where $k \ge 1$). This is defined as the $k$-th element (0-indexed $k-1$) of the increasing list of distinct prime factors of `n.natAbs`. Returns 1 if n has fewer than k distinct prime factors or if n is 0, 1, or -1, following the informal convention.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/OEIS/382590.lean
@decl OeisA382590.kthPrimeFactor_periodic
-- L4DEL: title: Conjecture: For any $k > 1$, if you take the $k$-th prime factor of each term, you get an eventually periodic sequence
-- L4DEL: intuition: Conjecture: For any $k > 1$, if you take the $k$-th prime factor of each term, you get an eventually periodic sequence. - _Bryle Morga_, Mar 31 2025 Here the $k$-th prime factor of $a(n)$ is the $k$-th smallest distinct prime divisor of $|a(n)|$; for example the second prime factors of $a(5) = 18$, $a(6) = 20$ and $a(7) = 896$ are $3$, $5$ and $7$. This was proved by Terence Tao in a MathOverflow answer, using that $a(n)$ divides $a(n+3)$ for all $n$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

