-- L4DEL: title: Conjecture: let $P(n)$ be the $(n+1) \times (n+1)$ Hankel-type determinant with $(i,j)$-entry equal to $a(i+j)$ for all $i,j = 0, \ldots, n$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Conjecture: let $P(n)$ be the $(n+1) \times (n+1)$ Hankel-type determinant with $(i,j)$-entry equal to $a(i+j)$ for all $i,j = 0, \ldots, n$. Then $P(n)/2^{n(n+3)}$ is a positive odd integer. - Zhi-Wei Sun, Aug 14 2013 Proved by [ZS18], Theorem 1.2: $2^{-n(n+3)} |P_{i+j}|_{0 \le i,j \le n}$ is a positive odd integer for every $n \in \mathbb{N}$, where $P_n$ are the Catalan-Larcombe-French numbers.
