@file FormalConjectures/OEIS/69004.lean
@decl OeisA69004.conjecture2
-- L4DEL: title: Stronger conjecture: Let $\pi(n)$ be the prime counting function (A000720)
-- L4DEL: intuition: Stronger conjecture: Let $\pi(n)$ be the prime counting function (A000720). Then $\pi(n) \ge a(n) \ge \pi(n)/5$ for $n > 1$, with the following equalities: $\pi(2) = a(2)$, $\pi(10) = a(10)$ and $a(12) = \pi(12)/5$. This conjunction is false: its upper bound $\pi(n) \ge a(n)$ fails at $n = 512720 = 2^4 \cdot 5 \cdot 13 \cdot 17 \cdot 29$, where $a(n) = 42666$ and $\pi(n) = 42493$. The OEIS entry tabulates $a$ only up to $n = 10^4$. The lower bound and the three equalities are unaffected; see `conjecture2.variants.without_upper_bound`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/OEIS/69004.lean
@decl OeisA69004.conjecture2.variants.upper_bound_false
-- L4DEL: title: The upper bound of the stronger conjecture on its own
-- L4DEL: intuition: The upper bound of the stronger conjecture on its own. It is false: $a(512720) = 42666$ while $\pi(512720) = 42493$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/OEIS/69004.lean
@decl OeisA69004.conjecture2.variants.without_upper_bound
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: without_upper_bound
-- L4DEL: intuition: The part of the stronger conjecture that survives the counterexample to its upper bound: $5 a(n) \ge \pi(n)$ for $n > 1$, together with the three claimed equalities $\pi(2) = a(2)$, $\pi(10) = a(10)$ and $a(12) = \pi(12)/5$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

