@file FormalConjectures/Other/GerstenhaberThreeMatrices.lean
@decl Gerstenhaber.exists_finrank_adjoin_quadruple_gt
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: exists_finrank_adjoin_quadruple_gt
-- L4DEL: intuition: The analogue of Gerstenhaber's theorem fails for four pairwise commuting matrices: over any field there are four pairwise commuting $4 \times 4$ matrices generating a unital algebra of dimension greater than $4$. The standard example is $e_{13}, e_{14}, e_{23}, e_{24}$, whose pairwise products all vanish, so the algebra they generate has dimension $5$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Other/GerstenhaberThreeMatrices.lean
@decl Gerstenhaber.finrank_adjoin_pair_le
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: finrank_adjoin_pair_le
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Gerstenhaber's theorem** [Ger61]: if $A$ and $B$ are commuting $n \times n$ matrices over a field $K$, then the unital $K$-algebra $K[A, B]$ they generate has dimension at most $n$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Other/GerstenhaberThreeMatrices.lean
@decl Gerstenhaber.finrank_adjoin_triple_le
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: finrank_adjoin_triple_le
-- L4DEL: intuition: **The Gerstenhaber problem**: if $A$, $B$, and $C$ are pairwise commuting $n \times n$ matrices over a field $K$, is the dimension of the unital $K$-algebra $K[A, B, C]$ they generate always at most $n$?
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Other/HyperbolicGroupsResiduallyFinite.lean
@decl HyperbolicGroupsResiduallyFinite.hyperbolic_exists_finiteIndex_ne_top
-- L4DEL: title: Does every nontrivial hyperbolic group have a proper subgroup of finite index?
-- L4DEL: intuition: Does every nontrivial hyperbolic group have a proper subgroup of finite index? This is Problem (H1)(b) in [BMS]. The trivial group has no proper subgroup, so we assume that $G$ is nontrivial. No. By [KaWi00] (see `hyperbolic_residuallyFinite_iff_exists_finiteIndex_ne_top`), this problem is equivalent to `hyperbolic_residuallyFinite`, which was disproved in [OAI26a].
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Other/HyperbolicGroupsResiduallyFinite.lean
@decl HyperbolicGroupsResiduallyFinite.hyperbolic_residuallyFinite
-- L4DEL: title: Is every hyperbolic group residually finite?
-- L4DEL: intuition: Is every hyperbolic group residually finite? This is Problem (H1)(a) in [BMS] and Q 1.15 in [Be04]. No. This was disproved by an internal OpenAI model in September 2026 [OAI26a], which constructs a torsion-free hyperbolic group that is not residually finite. The Lean proof of [OAI26a] defines hyperbolicity by uniformly thin geodesic triangles in a Cayley graph. That definition is equivalent to `Group.IsHyperbolic`, but the equivalence is not part of the Lean proof.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Other/HyperbolicGroupsResiduallyFinite.lean
@decl HyperbolicGroupsResiduallyFinite.hyperbolic_residuallyFinite_iff_exists_finiteIndex_ne_top
-- L4DEL: title: Every hyperbolic group is residually finite if and only if every nontrivial hyperbolic group has a proper subgroup of finite index
-- L4DEL: intuition: Every hyperbolic group is residually finite if and only if every nontrivial hyperbolic group has a proper subgroup of finite index. This is the theorem of [KaWi00] that Problems (H1)(a) and (H1)(b) are equivalent.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

