@file FormalConjectures/Other/Rule30.lean
@decl Rule30.step
-- L4DEL: title: The Rule 30 local update on a bi-infinite row `row : ℤ → Bool`
-- L4DEL: intuition: The Rule 30 local update on a bi-infinite row `row : ℤ → Bool`. The new value at position $i$ is `row (i-1) ⊕ (row i ∨ row (i+1))`, i.e. Wolfram's rule $30$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Other/SchurTruncatedExponential.lean
@decl SchurTruncatedExponential.schur_truncatedExp_galoisGroup_equiv
-- L4DEL: title: Schur's Theorem (1924):** Let `f_n(x) = ∑_{j=0}^n x^j/j!` be the `n`-th truncated exponential polynomial over `ℚ`
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Schur's Theorem (1924):** Let `f_n(x) = ∑_{j=0}^n x^j/j!` be the `n`-th truncated exponential polynomial over `ℚ`. Then for `n ≥ 2`: - If `n ≡ 0 (mod 4)`, the Galois group of `f_n` is isomorphic to the alternating group `A_n` - If `n ≢ 0 (mod 4)`, the Galois group of `f_n` is isomorphic to the symmetric group `S_n`
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Other/SchurTruncatedExponential.lean
@decl SchurTruncatedExponential.truncatedExp
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: truncatedExp
-- L4DEL: intuition: The truncated exponential polynomial `truncatedExp n` is given by `∑_{j=0}^{n} x^j / j!` over `ℚ`, which is the `n`-th partial sum of the Taylor series for the exponential function `e^x`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Other/SuffixPrefixAvoidance.lean
@decl SuffixPrefixAvoidance.IsSuffixPrefixAvoiding
-- L4DEL: title: Two sets of words $A, B$ over `Fin q` of length `n` are suffix-prefix avoiding if no nonempty suffix of any word in $A$ equals any prefix of any word in $B$ of the same length
-- L4DEL: intuition: Two sets of words $A, B$ over `Fin q` of length `n` are suffix-prefix avoiding if no nonempty suffix of any word in $A$ equals any prefix of any word in $B$ of the same length.
