@file FormalConjectures/Paper/AuslanderReiten.lean
@decl AuslanderReiten.auslander_reiten
-- L4DEL: title: The Auslander-Reiten conjecture** [AR75]
-- L4DEL: intuition: **The Auslander-Reiten conjecture** [AR75]. Let $\Lambda$ be an Artin algebra and $M$ a finitely generated $\Lambda$-module with $\operatorname{Ext}^i_\Lambda(M, \Lambda) = 0$ and $\operatorname{Ext}^i_\Lambda(M, M) = 0$ for all $i > 0$. Then $M$ is projective. That $\Lambda$ is an Artin algebra is the hypothesis that it is an algebra over some commutative Artinian ring `A` and is finitely generated as an `A`-module.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Paper/AuslanderReiten.lean
@decl AuslanderReiten.auslander_reiten.variants.commutative
-- L4DEL: title: The Auslander-Reiten conjecture for commutative Noetherian rings** [Ki23]
-- L4DEL: intuition: **The Auslander-Reiten conjecture for commutative Noetherian rings** [Ki23]. If $R$ is a commutative Noetherian ring and $M$ is a finitely generated $R$-module with $\operatorname{Ext}^i_R(M, R \oplus M) = 0$ for all $i > 0$, then $M$ is projective.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Paper/AuslanderReiten.lean
@decl AuslanderReiten.auslander_reiten.variants.local_free
-- L4DEL: title: Over a Noetherian local ring a finitely generated projective module is free, so the conjecture takes the following form there
-- L4DEL: intuition: Over a Noetherian local ring a finitely generated projective module is free, so the conjecture takes the following form there. It is open in this case too.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Paper/AuslanderReiten.lean
@decl AuslanderReiten.auslander_reiten.variants.normal
-- L4DEL: title: The conjecture holds for normal rings [Ki23, Corollary 1.2]
-- L4DEL: intuition: The conjecture holds for normal rings [Ki23, Corollary 1.2]. Stated here for normal domains, since Mathlib does not currently have a standalone class for normal rings, which are the rings whose localizations at all primes are normal domains.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Paper/AuslanderReiten.lean
@decl AuslanderReiten.auslander_reiten.variants.regular_local
-- L4DEL: title: Over a regular local ring the conjecture is standard: every finitely generated module has finite projective dimension $p$, and if $p > 0$ then $\operatorname{Ext}^p_R(M, R) \ne 0$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Over a regular local ring the conjecture is standard: every finitely generated module has finite projective dimension $p$, and if $p > 0$ then $\operatorname{Ext}^p_R(M, R) \ne 0$. Only the vanishing of $\operatorname{Ext}^i_R(M, R)$ is used, and the conclusion is freeness.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Paper/AuslanderReiten.lean
@decl AuslanderReiten.subsingleton_ext_of_projective
-- L4DEL: title: The converse of the conjecture is elementary: over any ring, all higher `Ext` out of a projective module vanish
-- L4DEL: intuition: The converse of the conjecture is elementary: over any ring, all higher `Ext` out of a projective module vanish. So the conjecture says that its two `Ext` vanishing hypotheses characterise the projective modules.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

