@decl CatchUp.value_of_even_mul_succ_self_div_two
-- L4DEL: title: Let $T_N = \sum_{k=1}^{N} k = \frac{N(N+1)}{2}$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $T_N = \sum_{k=1}^{N} k = \frac{N(N+1)}{2}$. If $T_N$ is even (equivalently $N \equiv 0 \pmod 4$ or $N \equiv 3 \pmod 4$), then under optimal play the game `Catch-Up($\{1, \ldots, N\}$)` ends in a draw.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Paper/CatchUpConjecture.lean
@decl CatchUp.valueAux
-- L4DEL: title: `value remaining s_me s_opp isFirstMove` evaluates the position where: * `remaining` is the set `S'` of numbers not yet taken
-- L4DEL: intuition: `value remaining s_me s_opp isFirstMove` evaluates the position where: * `remaining` is the set `S'` of numbers not yet taken. * `s_me` is the current score of the player who is about to act (the “current player”). * `s_opp` is the opponent’s current score. * `isFirstMove` indicates whether we are in the special very first move of the whole game, where the rules force exactly one pick and then the turn passes. The result is the game-theoretic value from the current player’s point of view: `.win` / `.loss` / `.draw`, assuming optimal play from both sides. We model a single *turn* (which may consist of several picks `$x_1,...,x_k$`) by recursion: the current player chooses one number `x`; if they are still strictly behind after taking it, they must continue the same turn (so the recursive call keeps the same “current player”); once they catch up (score ≥ opponent), the turn ends and we swap players.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

