@file FormalConjectures/Paper/EisenbudGreenHarris.lean
@decl EisenbudGreenHarris.eisenbud_green_harris
-- L4DEL: title: The Eisenbud-Green-Harris conjecture.** Let $I \subseteq k[x_1, \dots, x_n]$ be a homogeneous ideal containing a regular sequence of forms of degrees $d_1 \le \dots \le d_c$
-- L4DEL: intuition: **The Eisenbud-Green-Harris conjecture.** Let $I \subseteq k[x_1, \dots, x_n]$ be a homogeneous ideal containing a regular sequence of forms of degrees $d_1 \le \dots \le d_c$. Then there is a lex ideal $L$ such that $I$ has the same Hilbert function as $L + (x_1^{d_1}, \dots, x_c^{d_c})$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Paper/EisenbudGreenHarris.lean
@decl EisenbudGreenHarris.eisenbud_green_harris.variants.caviglia_deStefani
-- L4DEL: title: The conjecture holds when the degrees grow quickly enough, namely when $d_i \ge \sum_{j < i} (d_j - 1)$ for all $i \ge 3$
-- L4DEL: intuition: The conjecture holds when the degrees grow quickly enough, namely when $d_i \ge \sum_{j < i} (d_j - 1)$ for all $i \ge 3$. This is [CDS20, Theorem A]; Caviglia and Maclagan [CM08] had proved it under the strict inequality. Indices here are `0`-based, so $i \ge 3$ becomes `2 ≤ i`. The subtraction is on `ℕ`, which is harmless because a regular sequence of degrees `d` forces `1 ≤ d j`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

