@file FormalConjectures/Paper/KaplanskyUInvariant.lean
@decl KaplanskyUInvariant.IsUInvariant
-- L4DEL: title: `IsUInvariant n` means that some field of characteristic not `2` has $u$-invariant $n$, i.e
-- L4DEL: intuition: `IsUInvariant n` means that some field of characteristic not `2` has $u$-invariant $n$, i.e. $n$ is the largest dimension of an anisotropic quadratic form over that field. The field is taken in `Type` (universe $0$), which loses nothing. A quadratic form in $m$ variables is given by finitely many coefficients, so for fixed $n$ the property "characteristic not $2$ and $u$-invariant $n$" is one first-order sentence in the language of rings: $1 + 1 \ne 0$, some form in $n$ variables is anisotropic, and every form in $n + 1$ variables is isotropic (then so is every form in more variables). By the downward Löwenheim–Skolem theorem, a field with this property in any universe has a countable elementary subfield, which has the same property and is isomorphic to a field in `Type`. Conversely, `ULift` moves a field in `Type` to any universe.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Paper/KaplanskyUInvariant.lean
@decl KaplanskyUInvariant.not_bddAbove_anisotropicDims_real
-- L4DEL: title: $u(\mathbb{R}) = \infty$ [MerkurjevParimala2025, §5.1]: the sum of $n$ squares is anisotropic for every $n$, so there is no largest dimension of an anisotropic form
-- L4DEL: intuition: $u(\mathbb{R}) = \infty$ [MerkurjevParimala2025, §5.1]: the sum of $n$ squares is anisotropic for every $n$, so there is no largest dimension of an anisotropic form.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Paper/KaplanskyUInvariant.lean
@decl KaplanskyUInvariant.u_invariant_values
-- L4DEL: title: The values of the $u$-invariant**, stated as in [MerkurjevParimala2025, §5.1]: which integers are $u$-invariants of fields (of characteristic not $2$)?
-- L4DEL: intuition: **The values of the $u$-invariant**, stated as in [MerkurjevParimala2025, §5.1]: which integers are $u$-invariants of fields (of characteristic not $2$)? The question goes back to [Kaplansky1953], who conjectured that only powers of $2$ occur (`u_invariant_values.variants.kaplansky_conjecture`). Every positive even integer is a $u$-invariant (`u_invariant_values.variants.even`), $3$, $5$ and $7$ are not (`u_invariant_values.variants.not_three`, `u_invariant_values.variants.not_five`, `u_invariant_values.variants.not_seven`), and it is expected that every odd integer $\ge 9$ is (`u_invariant_values.variants.odd`). Granting the preprint [Karpenko2026] (`u_invariant_values.variants.karpenko`), the answer is known for every $n$ except $n = 2^r - 1$ and $n = 2^r - 3$ with $r \ge 4$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Paper/KaplanskyUInvariant.lean
@decl KaplanskyUInvariant.u_invariant_values.variants.eleven
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: eleven
-- L4DEL: intuition: The special case $n = 11$ of `u_invariant_values.variants.karpenko` (neither $12$ nor $14$ is a power of $2$), the first odd value not of the form $2^r + 1$ shown to be a $u$-invariant; it was announced in the preprint *Fields of $u$-invariant 11* (21 April 2026) that [Karpenko2026] absorbs.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Paper/KaplanskyUInvariant.lean
@decl KaplanskyUInvariant.u_invariant_values.variants.even
-- L4DEL: title: Merkurjev's theorem** [Merkurjev1991], see also [EKM2008, Theorem 38.4]: every positive even integer is a $u$-invariant
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Merkurjev's theorem** [Merkurjev1991], see also [EKM2008, Theorem 38.4]: every positive even integer is a $u$-invariant. The case $6$, the first counterexample to Kaplansky's conjecture, is [Merkurjev1989].
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Paper/KaplanskyUInvariant.lean
