@file FormalConjectures/Paper/KaplanskyUInvariant.lean
@decl KaplanskyUInvariant.u_invariant_values.variants.kaplansky_conjecture
-- L4DEL: title: Kaplansky's conjecture** [Kaplansky1953, p
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Kaplansky's conjecture** [Kaplansky1953, p. 202]: the $u$-invariant of a field is a power of $2$ whenever it is finite. Disproved by Merkurjev, who constructed a field of $u$-invariant $6$ [Merkurjev1989] (`u_invariant_values.variants.even`).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Paper/KaplanskyUInvariant.lean
@decl KaplanskyUInvariant.u_invariant_values.variants.karpenko
-- L4DEL: title: Karpenko's theorem** [Karpenko2026, Theorem 1.1]: if neither $n + 1$ nor $n + 3$ is a power of $2$, then there is a field of $u$-invariant $n$
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Karpenko's theorem** [Karpenko2026, Theorem 1.1]: if neither $n + 1$ nor $n + 3$ is a power of $2$, then there is a field of $u$-invariant $n$. This covers every positive integer except $1$, $3$, $5$, $7$ and the integers $2^r - 1$, $2^r - 3$ with $r \ge 4$. As of September 2026 the result is a preprint and not yet peer-reviewed.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Paper/KaplanskyUInvariant.lean
