@file FormalConjectures/Paper/WeaklyFirstCountable.lean
@decl WeaklyFirstCountable.WeaklyFirstCountableTopology
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: WeaklyFirstCountableTopology
-- L4DEL: intuition: A topological space $X$ is called *weakly first countable* if there exists a function $N : X → ℕ → Set X$, such that: * For all $x : X, n : ℕ$ we have $x ∈ V x n$ * For all $x : X, n : ℕ$: $V x (n + 1) ⊆ V x n$ * $O ⊆ X$ is open iff $∀ x ∈ O, ∃ n : ℕ, V x n ⊆ O$
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Paper/WeakTiling.lean
@decl WeakTiling.HasBoundedDensity
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: HasBoundedDensity
-- L4DEL: intuition: A set $\Lambda \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ has **bounded density** if the number of points of $\Lambda$ in any unit open interval is uniformly bounded: $\sup_{x \in \mathbb{R}} \#(\Lambda \cap (x, x + 1)) < \infty$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Paper/WeakTiling.lean
@decl WeakTiling.IsFiniteUnionOfIntervals
-- L4DEL: title: A set $A \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ is a **finite union of intervals** if it is a union of $n$ intervals for some $n$
-- L4DEL: intuition: A set $A \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ is a **finite union of intervals** if it is a union of $n$ intervals for some $n$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Paper/WeakTiling.lean
@decl WeakTiling.IsProperTiling
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: IsProperTiling
-- L4DEL: intuition: A **proper tiling** of $\Omega^c$ by translates of $\Omega$ is specified by a set of translation parameters $T \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ such that the sum of Dirac masses on $T$ is a weak tiling measure for $\Omega$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Paper/WeakTiling.lean
@decl WeakTiling.IsUnionOfNIntervals
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: IsUnionOfNIntervals
-- L4DEL: intuition: A set $A \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ is a **union of $n$ intervals** if it can be written as $A = \bigcup_{i=1}^{n} (a_i, b_i)$ with $a_1 < b_1 < a_2 < b_2 < \dots < a_n < b_n$, i.e., as a disjoint union of $n$ nondegenerate open intervals in strict order with strict separation. This matches the convention of the paper (see e.g. Theorem 3.4 there).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Paper/WeakTiling.lean
@decl WeakTiling.IsWeakTilingMeasure
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: IsWeakTilingMeasure
-- L4DEL: intuition: A positive, locally finite Borel measure $\nu$ on $\mathbb{R}$ is a **weak tiling measure** for a bounded measurable set $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}$ if the convolution $1_\Omega \ast \nu = 1_{\Omega^c}$ holds almost everywhere, i.e., $\int 1_\Omega(x - t) \, d\nu(t) = 1_{\Omega^c}(x)$ for a.e. $x \in \mathbb{R}$. This is Definition 1.1 from the paper.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

