@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/ArtinPrimitiveRootsConjecture.lean
@decl ArtinPrimitiveRootsConjecture.artin_primitive_roots.parts.i
-- L4DEL: title: Artin's Conjecture on Primitive Roots**, first half
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Artin's Conjecture on Primitive Roots**, first half. Let $a$ be an integer that is not a square number and not $−1$. Then the set $S(a)$ of primes $p$ such that $a$ is a primitive root modulo $p$ has a positive asymptotic density inside the set of primes. In particular, $S(a)$ is infinite.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/ArtinPrimitiveRootsConjecture.lean
@decl ArtinPrimitiveRootsConjecture.artin_primitive_roots.parts.ii
-- L4DEL: title: Artin's Conjecture on Primitive Roots**, second half
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Artin's Conjecture on Primitive Roots**, second half. Write $a = a_0 b^2$ where $a_0$ is squarefree. Under the conditions that $a$ is not a perfect power and $a_0\not\equiv 1\pmod{4}$ (sequence A85397 in the OEIS), the density of the set $S(a)$ of primes $p$ such that $a$ is a primitive root modulo $p$ is independent of $a$ and equals Artin's constant.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/ArtinPrimitiveRootsConjecture.lean
@decl ArtinPrimitiveRootsConjecture.artin_primitive_roots.variants.part_ii_power_squarefreePart_modeq_one
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: part_ii_power_squarefreePart_modeq_one
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Artin's Conjecture on Primitive Roots**, second half, power version If $a = b^m$ is a perfect power of a number $b$ whose squarefree part $b_0\equiv 1 \pmod{4}$, then the density of the set $S(a)$ of primes $p$ such that $a$ is a primitive root modulo $p$ is given by $$C \left(\prod_{p \mid m} \frac{p(p-2)}{(p ^ 2 - p - 1)}\right) \left(1 - \prod_{p \mid \gcd(b_0, m)} \frac{1}{2 - p} \prod_{p \mid b_0, p\nmid m} \frac{1}{(1 + p - p ^ 2)}\right),$$ where $C$ is Artin's constant.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/ArtinPrimitiveRootsConjecture.lean
@decl ArtinPrimitiveRootsConjecture.artin_primitive_roots.variants.part_ii_power_squarefreePart_not_modeq_one
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: part_ii_power_squarefreePart_not_modeq_one
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Artin's Conjecture on Primitive Roots**, second half, power version If $a = b^m$ is a perfect odd power of a number $b$ whose squarefree part $b_0\not\equiv 1 \pmod{4}$, then the density of the set $S(a)$ of primes $p$ such that $a$ is a primitive root modulo $p$ is given by $$C\prod_{p \mid m} \frac{p(p - 2)}{p^2 - p - 1}$$, where $C$ is Artin's constant.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/ArtinPrimitiveRootsConjecture.lean
@decl ArtinPrimitiveRootsConjecture.artin_primitive_roots.variants.part_ii_square_or_minus_one
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: part_ii_square_or_minus_one
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Artin's Conjecture on Primitive Roots**, second half, different residue version If $a$ is a square number or $a = −1$, then the density of the set $S(a)$ of primes $p$ such that $a$ is a primitive root modulo $p$ is $0$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/ArtinPrimitiveRootsConjecture.lean
@decl ArtinPrimitiveRootsConjecture.ArtinConstant
-- L4DEL: title: Artin's Constant** is defined to be the product $$\prod_{p\ \text{prime}} \left(1 - \frac{1}{p(p - 1)}\right)$$
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Artin's Constant** is defined to be the product $$\prod_{p\ \text{prime}} \left(1 - \frac{1}{p(p - 1)}\right)$$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

