@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/InvariantSubspaceProblem.lean
@decl InvariantSubspaceProblem.ClosedInvariantSubspace
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: ClosedInvariantSubspace
-- L4DEL: intuition: `ClosedInvariantSubspace T` is the type of non-trivial (different from `H` and `{0}`) closed subspaces of a complex vector space `H` that are invariant under the action of linear map `T`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/InvariantSubspaceProblem.lean
@decl InvariantSubspaceProblem.Invariant_subspace_problem
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: Invariant_subspace_problem
-- L4DEL: intuition: Show that every bounded linear operator `T : H → H` on a separable Hilbert space `H` of dimension at least 2 has a non-trivial closed `T`-invariant subspace: a closed linear subspace `W` of `H`, which is different from `H` and from `{0}`, such that `T ( W ) ⊂ W`. One needs the assumption that the dimension of `H` is at least 2 because otherwise any subspace would be either `H` or `{0}`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/InvariantSubspaceProblem.lean
@decl InvariantSubspaceProblem.Invariant_subspace_problem_finite_dimensional
-- L4DEL: title: Every (bounded) linear operator `T : H → H` on a finite-dimensional normed space `H` of dimension at least 2 has a non-trivial (closed) `T`-invariant subspace
-- L4DEL: intuition: Every (bounded) linear operator `T : H → H` on a finite-dimensional normed space `H` of dimension at least 2 has a non-trivial (closed) `T`-invariant subspace. Since `ℂ` is algebraically closed, `T` has an eigenvector `v ≠ 0`, and the line `ℂ ∙ v` is such a subspace. `H` is a complex normed space: with a bare `Module ℂ H` unrelated to the norm, finite-dimensional subspaces need not be closed, and the statement fails already in complex dimension `2`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/InvariantSubspaceProblem.lean
@decl InvariantSubspaceProblem.Invariant_subspace_problem_l1
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: Invariant_subspace_problem_l1
-- L4DEL: intuition: There exists a bounded linear operator `T` on the l1 space `(lp (fun (_ : ℕ) => ℂ) 1))` without non-trivial closed `T`-invariant subspace Read 1985, see also the first counterexample by Enflo Enflo 1987, submitted in 1981.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/InvariantSubspaceProblem.lean
@decl InvariantSubspaceProblem.Invariant_subspace_problem_non_separable
-- L4DEL: title: Every bounded linear operator `T : H → H` on a non-separable Hilbert space `H` has a non-trivial closed `T`-invariant subspace
-- L4DEL: intuition: Every bounded linear operator `T : H → H` on a non-separable Hilbert space `H` has a non-trivial closed `T`-invariant subspace. Such an invariant space is given by considering the closure of the linear span of the orbit of any single non-zero vector.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/InvariantSubspaceProblem.lean
@decl InvariantSubspaceProblem.Invariant_subspace_problem_normal_operator
-- L4DEL: title: Every normal linear operator `T : H → H` on a Hilbert space `H` of dimension at least 2 has a non-trivial closed `T`-invariant subspace
-- L4DEL: intuition: Every normal linear operator `T : H → H` on a Hilbert space `H` of dimension at least 2 has a non-trivial closed `T`-invariant subspace. If `T` is a multiple of the identity, one can tafrake any non-trivial subspace . If not, one can take any nontrivial spectral subspace of `T`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

