-- L4DEL: title: An abelian group is a Leinster group if and only if it is cyclic with order equal to a perfect number
-- L4DEL: intuition: An abelian group is a Leinster group if and only if it is cyclic with order equal to a perfect number. Reference: Leinster, Tom (2001). "Perfect numbers and groups". Theorem 2.1.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/LeinsterGroup.lean
@decl LeinsterGroup.cyclic_of_perfect_is_leinster
-- L4DEL: title: Cyclic groups of perfect number order are Leinster groups
-- L4DEL: intuition: Cyclic groups of perfect number order are Leinster groups. This follows from the fact that for a cyclic group, all subgroups are normal and correspond to divisors of the group order, and a number is perfect if and only if the sum of its divisors (including itself) equals twice the number.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/LeinsterGroup.lean
@decl LeinsterGroup.dihedral_is_leinster_iff_odd_perfect
-- L4DEL: title: The dihedral group `DihedralGroup n` (of order `2n`) is a Leinster group if and only if `n` is an odd perfect number
-- L4DEL: intuition: The dihedral group `DihedralGroup n` (of order `2n`) is a Leinster group if and only if `n` is an odd perfect number. This gives a one-to-one correspondence between dihedral Leinster groups and odd perfect numbers. In particular, the existence of odd perfect numbers is equivalent to the existence of dihedral Leinster groups. Reference: Leinster, Tom (2001). "Perfect numbers and groups".
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/LeinsterGroup.lean
@decl LeinsterGroup.exists_nonabelian_leinster_group
-- L4DEL: title: Non-abelian Leinster groups exist
-- L4DEL: intuition: Non-abelian Leinster groups exist. For example, `S₃ × C₅` (order 30) and `A₅ × C₁₅₁₂₈` are Leinster groups. Reference: Leinster, Tom (2001). "Perfect numbers and groups".
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/LeinsterGroup.lean
@decl LeinsterGroup.infinitely_many_leinster_groups
-- L4DEL: title: Conjecture:** Are there infinitely many Leinster groups?
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Conjecture:** Are there infinitely many Leinster groups? This asks whether there exist infinitely many (non-isomorphic) finite groups that are Leinster groups. Formalized via the negation of "Does there exist an n such that all Leinster groups have order less than n".
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

