@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/LonelyRunnerConjecture.lean
@decl LonelyRunnerConjecture.lonely_runner_conjecture
-- L4DEL: title: Consider $n$ runners on a circular track of unit length
-- L4DEL: intuition: Consider $n$ runners on a circular track of unit length. At the initial time $t = 0$, all runners are at the same position and start to run; the runners' speeds are constant, all distinct, and may be negative. A runner is said to be lonely at time $t$ if they are at a distance (measured along the circle) of at least $\frac 1 n$ from every other runner. The lonely runner conjecture states that each runner is lonely at some time, no matter the choice of speeds.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/LonelyRunnerConjecture.lean
@decl LonelyRunnerConjecture.lonely_runner_conjecture.variants.tao_2017
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: tao_2017
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Theorem 1.3 (Tao, 2017; arXiv:1701.02048).** There exists an absolute constant $c > 0$ such that for all sufficiently large $n$, the gap of loneliness satisfies $\delta_n \ge \frac{1}{2n} + \frac{c \log n}{n^2 (\log \log n)^2}$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

