@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/PompeiuProblem.lean
@decl PompeiuProblem.ball_not_hasPompeiuProperty
-- L4DEL: title: The classical (easy) direction, proved by Brown–Schreiber–Taylor [BST73]: a Euclidean ball fails to have the Pompeiu property
-- L4DEL: intuition: The classical (easy) direction, proved by Brown–Schreiber–Taylor [BST73]: a Euclidean ball fails to have the Pompeiu property. For a ball of radius `R`, an explicit witness is `f(x) = sin(a x₁)` where `a > 0` is chosen so that the Bessel function `J_{n/2}(a R) = 0`; its integral over every congruent ball vanishes.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/PompeiuProblem.lean
@decl PompeiuProblem.HasLipschitzBoundary
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: HasLipschitzBoundary
-- L4DEL: intuition: `Ω` has **Lipschitz boundary** if every boundary point `p` has a neighbourhood `U` in which, after some rigid motion `σ` (used to choose a "vertical" axis), `Ω` coincides with the strict subgraph of a Lipschitz function `g` of the first `N` coordinates: a point `x ∈ U` lies in `Ω` iff its last coordinate (in the `σ`-frame) is below `g` applied to its first `N` coordinates. This is the standard definition of a Lipschitz domain.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/PompeiuProblem.lean
@decl PompeiuProblem.HasPompeiuProperty
-- L4DEL: title: A set `Ω` has the **Pompeiu property** if the only continuous `f : ℝ^{N+1} → ℝ` whose integral over every rigid-motion image `σ(Ω)` vanishes is the zero function
-- L4DEL: intuition: A set `Ω` has the **Pompeiu property** if the only continuous `f : ℝ^{N+1} → ℝ` whose integral over every rigid-motion image `σ(Ω)` vanishes is the zero function.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/PompeiuProblem.lean
@decl PompeiuProblem.integral_zero_eq_zero
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: integral_zero_eq_zero
-- L4DEL: intuition: Sanity check: the zero function always integrates to `0` over every rigid-motion image, so the hypothesis in `HasPompeiuProperty` is satisfiable (the property is not vacuously false).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/PompeiuProblem.lean
@decl PompeiuProblem.IsAdmissibleDomain
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: IsAdmissibleDomain
-- L4DEL: intuition: The domain hypotheses of Pompeiu's problem: $\Omega$ is bounded, open, connected (a "domain"), simply connected, has Lipschitz boundary, and for $N \ge 2$ the complement of its closure is connected. For $N = 1$, connectedness of $(\overline{\Omega})^c$ follows from simple connectivity.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

