@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/RudinsConjecture.lean
@decl RudinsConjecture.Qmax
-- L4DEL: title: $Q(N) = \max Q(N; q, a)$, the largest number of perfect squares occurring among the first $N$ terms of any non-trivial arithmetic progression
-- L4DEL: intuition: $Q(N) = \max Q(N; q, a)$, the largest number of perfect squares occurring among the first $N$ terms of any non-trivial arithmetic progression. The supremum is over a set of naturals that is bounded above by $N$ (each progression has only $N$ terms), so it is attained.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/RudinsConjecture.lean
@decl RudinsConjecture.rudins_conjecture
-- L4DEL: title: Rudin's conjecture.** The maximal number of squares among the first $N$ terms of a non-trivial arithmetic progression grows at most like $\sqrt{N}$: $$Q(N) = O(\sqrt{N}).$$
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Rudin's conjecture.** The maximal number of squares among the first $N$ terms of a non-trivial arithmetic progression grows at most like $\sqrt{N}$: $$Q(N) = O(\sqrt{N}).$$
