@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/SidorenkoConjecture.lean
@decl SidorenkoConjecture.edgeCount_eq_zero_of_subsingleton
-- L4DEL: title: If the domain of `H` has at most one vertex, `H` has no edges
-- L4DEL: intuition: If the domain of `H` has at most one vertex, `H` has no edges. **Proof.** `SimpleGraph.Adj` is irreflexive, so any adjacency `H.Adj u v` forces `u ≠ v`; on a subsingleton that's impossible, so no edges exist.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/SidorenkoConjecture.lean
@decl SidorenkoConjecture.homCount_completeBipartiteGraph_fin_two_eq_sum_inter_sq
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: homCount_completeBipartiteGraph_fin_two_eq_sum_inter_sq
-- L4DEL: intuition: **The `Hom(K_{2,2}, G)` decomposition.** The number of homomorphisms from `K_{2,2}` to `G` equals `∑_{(a, b) ∈ W × W} |N(a) ∩ N(b)|^2`, where `N(v)` is the neighbourhood of `v` in `G`. Equivalently, summing over *ordered* pairs `(b₀, b₁) ∈ W × W` and counting common neighbours squared. **Math.** A homomorphism `K_{2,2} →g G` is an assignment `f : Fin 2 ⊕ Fin 2 → W` with `G.Adj (f (inl i)) (f (inr j))` for all `i, j ∈ Fin 2`. Equivalently, choose `(a₀, a₁) := (f (inl 0), f (inl 1))` arbitrarily in `W × W` and require `(f (inr 0), f (inr 1))` to both lie in `N(a₀) ∩ N(a₁)`. The count is thus `∑_{(a₀, a₁)} |N(a₀) ∩ N(a₁)|²`. **Proof.** Construct an explicit bijection `(K_{2,2} →g G) ≃ Σ (p : W × W), (N(p.1) ∩ N(p.2)) × (N(p.1) ∩ N(p.2))` by sending a homomorphism `f` to `⟨(f (inl 0), f (inl 1)), ⟨f (inr 0), f (inr 1)⟩⟩`. The total cardinality of the sigma-product is then `∑_p (Fintype.card (N(p.1) ∩ N(p.2)))² = ∑_p |N(p.1) ∩ N(p.2)|²`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/SidorenkoConjecture.lean
@decl SidorenkoConjecture.homCount_completeGraph_fin_two_eq_two_mul_card_edgeFinset
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: homCount_completeGraph_fin_two_eq_two_mul_card_edgeFinset
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Homomorphism count of `K_2` into `G` equals `2 · #edgeFinset`.** A homomorphism `K_2 →g G` is the same data as an ordered pair `(f 0, f 1)` of distinct vertices with `G.Adj (f 0) (f 1)`. These are in bijection with the `Dart`s of `G`, and `#Darts(G) = 2 · #E(G)` by `dart_card_eq_twice_card_edges`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/SidorenkoConjecture.lean
@decl SidorenkoConjecture.homCount_eq_of_subsingleton
-- L4DEL: title: If the domain of `H` has at most one vertex, every function `V → W` is a homomorphism `H →g G`
-- L4DEL: intuition: If the domain of `H` has at most one vertex, every function `V → W` is a homomorphism `H →g G`. Equivalently, `homCount H G = |W|^|V|`. **Proof.** The adjacency `H.Adj u v` is irreflexive; on a subsingleton it is uninhabited, so the homomorphism condition `H.Adj u v → G.Adj (f u) (f v)` is vacuous. Hence the `coe : (H →g G) → (V → W)` map is a bijection.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/SidorenkoConjecture.lean
@decl SidorenkoConjecture.homCount_star_eq_sum_degree_pow
-- L4DEL: title: Homomorphism count of the star `K_{1,m}`.** The number of homomorphisms from the star `K_{1,m} = completeBipartiteGraph (Fin 1) (Fin m)` into `G` equals `∑_{c} (G.degree c)^m`
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Homomorphism count of the star `K_{1,m}`.** The number of homomorphisms from the star `K_{1,m} = completeBipartiteGraph (Fin 1) (Fin m)` into `G` equals `∑_{c} (G.degree c)^m`. **Math.** A homomorphism `f : K_{1,m} →g G` is determined by the image `c := f` of the centre `Sum.inl 0` together with the images of the `m` leaves `Sum.inr j`, each of which must lie in the neighbourhood `N(c)` (and there is no constraint between distinct leaves). So the data is a choice of `c` and a function `Fin m → N(c)`, giving `∑_c |N(c)|^m = ∑_c (G.degree c)^m`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/SidorenkoConjecture.lean
@decl SidorenkoConjecture.IsSpider234
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: IsSpider234
-- L4DEL: intuition: **The $(2,3,4)$-spider tree.** A tree composed of three paths of lengths 2, 3, and 4 joined at a single central vertex: the centre is the unique vertex of degree 3 (every other vertex has degree at most 2), so the tree has exactly three leaves, at distances 2, 3 and 4 from the centre.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/SidorenkoConjecture.lean
@decl SidorenkoConjecture.sidorenko_completeBipartiteGraph_graphon
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: sidorenko_completeBipartiteGraph_graphon
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Case: `H = K_{a,b}` is a complete bipartite graph (Sidorenko 1993, graphon version).** Every complete bipartite graph $K_{a,b}$ satisfies Sidorenko's inequality for all graphons on $[0, 1]$.
