@decl SparseRuler.wichmann_conjecture
-- L4DEL: title: Wichmann's conjecture on optimal rulers.** Every optimal ruler of sufficiently large length is a Wichmann ruler $W(r, s)$ (up to reflection, i.e
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Wichmann's conjecture on optimal rulers.** Every optimal ruler of sufficiently large length is a Wichmann ruler $W(r, s)$ (up to reflection, i.e. reversing the segment list). Posed by Wichmann [Wi63]. The Wikipedia article records that no optimal ruler of length $1, 13, 17, 23$ or $58$ is a Wichmann ruler, and that every other optimal length up to $213$ is attained by one; non-Wichmann optimal rulers also occur alongside Wichmann ones at lengths $9, 29, 50$ and $68$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/SparseRuler.lean
@decl SparseRuler.wichmannGaps
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: wichmannGaps
-- L4DEL: intuition: The *Wichmann ruler* $W(r, s)$ [Wi63], given by its segment-length sequence $$1^r,\; (r+1),\; (2r+1)^r,\; (4r+3)^s,\; (2r+2)^{r+1},\; 1^r,$$ where $a^b$ denotes $b$ consecutive segments of length $a$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/SparseRuler.lean
