@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/SteinerSystem.lean
@decl SteinerSystems.fano_plane
-- L4DEL: title: Sanity check: the Fano plane is an $S(2, 3, 7)$-Steiner system
-- L4DEL: intuition: Sanity check: the Fano plane is an $S(2, 3, 7)$-Steiner system. The Fano plane consists of $7$ blocks of size $3$ over $7$ points, where every pair of points is contained in exactly one block.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/SteinerSystem.lean
@decl SteinerSystems.infinitely_many_steiner_t4
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: infinitely_many_steiner_t4
-- L4DEL: intuition: **There are infinitely many Steiner systems with $t = 4$.** Keevash (2014) proved that for any fixed $t$ and $k$, a Steiner system $S(t, k, n)$ exists for all sufficiently large $n$ satisfying the necessary divisibility conditions. Since there are infinitely many such admissible $n$, this implies infinitely many $S(4, k, n)$ systems exist (for any fixed $k > 4$). The proof is nonconstructive. Explicit examples include $S(4, 5, 11)$ (the unique system, related to the Mathieu group $M_{11}$) and $S(4, 7, 23)$ (related to the Mathieu group $M_{23}$). Only nontrivial systems with $4 < k < n$ are counted: for every $n$ the single block $\{1, \dots, n\}$ is an $S(4, n, n)$, and all $4$-subsets of an $n$-set form an $S(4, 4, n)$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/SteinerSystem.lean
@decl SteinerSystems.infinitely_many_steiner_t5
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: infinitely_many_steiner_t5
-- L4DEL: intuition: **There are infinitely many Steiner systems with $t = 5$.** Keevash (2014) proved that for any fixed $t$ and $k$, a Steiner system $S(t, k, n)$ exists for all sufficiently large $n$ satisfying the necessary divisibility conditions. This settles the long-standing open problem of whether infinitely many $S(5, k, n)$ systems exist. The proof is nonconstructive. Only two explicit examples are known: $S(5, 6, 12)$ and $S(5, 8, 24)$, both Witt designs related to the Mathieu groups $M_{12}$ and $M_{24}$ respectively. Only nontrivial systems with $5 < k < n$ are counted, for the same reason as in `infinitely_many_steiner_t4`. No Steiner system with $t \geq 6$ has been explicitly constructed, though Keevash's result guarantees their existence nonconstructively as well.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/SteinerSystem.lean
@decl SteinerSystems.large_steiner_systems
-- L4DEL: title: Construct an $S(t, k, n)$-Steiner system with $n > k > t > 5$, $t < 10$, and $n < 200$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Construct an $S(t, k, n)$-Steiner system with $n > k > t > 5$, $t < 10$, and $n < 200$. No example of a Steiner system with $t > 5$ is known, despite a 2014 existence theorem by Keevash showing that such systems must exist for sufficiently large $n$. *Reference:* Large Steiner Systems
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/SteinerSystem.lean
@decl SteinerSystems.LargeSteinerSystemWitness
-- L4DEL: title: A constructive witness for a large Steiner system: concrete values of $n$, $k$, $t$ satisfying $n > k > t > 5$, $t < 10$, $n < 200$, together with an explicit Steiner system
-- L4DEL: intuition: A constructive witness for a large Steiner system: concrete values of $n$, $k$, $t$ satisfying $n > k > t > 5$, $t < 10$, $n < 200$, together with an explicit Steiner system.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/SteinerSystem.lean
@decl SteinerSystems.steiner_system_5_6_12
-- L4DEL: title: Existence of $S(5, 6, 12)$**: The small Witt design
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Existence of $S(5, 6, 12)$**: The small Witt design. There exists a unique Steiner system $S(5, 6, 12)$, known as the small Witt design. It was constructed by Witt (1938) and is closely related to the Mathieu group $M_{12}$. This is one of only two known Steiner systems with $t = 5$.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

